/**
 * Teach Me explanations for government, written in advance so they load
 * instantly, work offline and cost nothing per student. Keyed by
 * passageId|question|options (see teachMeKey in app.js). A question with no
 * entry here falls back to the live AI explanation.
 */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {})["government"] = {
 "|The doctrine of separation of powers was propounded by:|Karl Marx|Montesquieu|Jean-Jacques Rousseau|John Locke": "Separation of powers means the three functions of government (making laws, carrying them out, and interpreting them) are given to three different organs: the legislature, the executive and the judiciary. The French writer Baron de Montesquieu set it out fully in \"The Spirit of the Laws\" (1748), warning that liberty is lost when one person or body holds all three powers: B.\nThe trap is John Locke: he wrote earlier about separating legislative and executive power, but JAMB credits the full doctrine, with an independent judiciary, to Montesquieu.\nMemory tip: \"Montesquieu – Spirit of the Laws – 1748 – Separation.\"",
 "|In a parliamentary system, the executive is accountable to:|The president|The military|The legislature|The judiciary": "In a parliamentary system the executive (the Prime Minister and cabinet) is drawn from the legislature, needs its majority, and stays in office only while it keeps the legislature's confidence. A vote of no confidence can remove it. So the executive is accountable to the legislature: C.\nThe trap is \"the president\": in a parliamentary system any president or monarch is only a ceremonial head of state, and it is the cabinet that answers to parliament, not to him.\nMemory tip: Parliamentary = the cabinet sits in Parliament and answers to Parliament.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a function of a constitution?|It defines the structure of government|It guarantees fundamental human rights|It provides for the election of all citizens|It defines the relationship between the citizen and the state": "A constitution sets up the organs of government, shares power among them, guarantees citizens' rights and defines how the citizen relates to the state. It does not \"provide for the election of all citizens\": citizens elect representatives; they are not themselves all elected. So C is NOT a function: C.\nThe trap is B: some students think rights belong only in separate laws, but guaranteeing fundamental rights is a core function (Chapter IV of Nigeria's 1999 Constitution).\nMemory tip: a constitution answers three questions: who governs, with what powers, and what rights the citizens keep.",
 "|The ECOWAS was founded in 1975 in:|Abuja|Accra|Lagos|Dakar": "The Economic Community of West African States was created when 15 West African states signed the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975. Generals Yakubu Gowon of Nigeria and Gnassingbé Eyadéma of Togo were its chief promoters. So it was founded in Lagos: C.\nThe trap is Abuja: Abuja is ECOWAS's headquarters today, but Abuja was not yet built in 1975, so the treaty could not have been signed there. Today ECOWAS has 12 members, since Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger left in January 2025.\nMemory tip: born in Lagos (1975), lives in Abuja.",
 "|Proportional representation is a system of representation in which:|Seats are given to the party that wins the most votes in a constituency|Seats in the legislature are distributed in proportion to votes won|Only rural areas are represented|Military appointments fill seats": "Proportional representation (PR) gives each party a share of seats roughly equal to its share of the votes. A party with 30% of the votes gets about 30% of the seats. Common forms are the party list system and the single transferable vote. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: giving the seat to whoever gets the most votes in a constituency is the simple-majority (first-past-the-post) system used in Nigeria, not PR.\nMemory tip: Proportional = seats in Proportion to votes.",
 "|A constitutional monarch is a ruler who:|Rules with absolute power|Rules subject to the provisions of a constitution|Is elected by the people|Is appointed by the military": "A constitutional monarch is a hereditary head of state whose powers are limited by a constitution or by conventions. He \"reigns but does not rule\": real power lies with elected ministers. Britain's King Charles III is an example. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: ruling with unlimited power describes an absolute monarch, the opposite. Option C is also wrong because monarchs inherit the throne; an elected head of state is a president.\nMemory tip: Absolute monarch = no limits; Constitutional monarch = Constitution limits.",
 "|The concept of 'Rule of Law' was developed by:|Montesquieu|Karl Marx|A.V. Dicey|Thomas Hobbes": "The British jurist A.V. Dicey popularised the rule of law in \"Introduction to the Study of the Law of the Constitution\" (1885). His three pillars are: supremacy of the law (no arbitrary power), equality before the law, and rights protected by the ordinary courts. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap is Montesquieu: he is linked with separation of powers, a different doctrine that students often mix up with rule of law.\nMemory tip: \"Dicey – 1885 – rule of law\"; \"Montesquieu – 1748 – separation of powers.\"",
 "|Which of the following is a merit of federalism?|It is too expensive to run|It prevents unity|It allows for the accommodation of diverse groups|It promotes instability": "Federalism divides power between a central government and regional units (in Nigeria, states) by a written constitution. Its great merit is that it lets peoples with different languages, religions and cultures live in one country while keeping some control over their own affairs. That is why Nigeria, the USA and India use it. So C is correct: C.\nOptions A, B and D are not merits. \"Too expensive to run\" is in fact a real criticism of federalism, so it cannot be its merit.\nMemory tip: Federalism = \"unity in diversity.\"",
 "|The United Nations Security Council has _____ permanent members.|3|5|7|10": "The UN Security Council has 15 members: 5 permanent members with the veto power and 10 non-permanent members elected by the General Assembly for two-year terms. The permanent five are the USA, the UK, France, Russia and China, the main victors of the Second World War. So the answer is 5: B.\nThe trap is D (10): that is the number of non-permanent members. The total is 15.\nMemory tip: \"5 permanent + 10 elected = 15\"; remember the P5 by the order U-U-F-R-C (USA, UK, France, Russia, China).",
 "|The system of government in which the state controls all aspects of life is:|Liberal democracy|Totalitarianism|Federalism|Confederalism": "Totalitarianism is a system in which the state, usually under one party or leader, tries to control every part of life: the economy, the press, education, religion and even private life. Hitler's Germany and Stalin's Soviet Union are the usual examples. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is to choose federalism or confederalism. These describe how power is divided between the centre and regions, not how much the state controls its people.\nMemory tip: Totalitarian = Total control.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a pressure group in Nigeria?|INEC|NLC (Nigeria Labour Congress)|NNPC|CBN": "A pressure group is an organised group that tries to influence government policy without trying to take control of government. The Nigeria Labour Congress (NLC), formed in 1978, presses government on wages and workers' welfare through lobbying, protests and strikes. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps are government bodies. INEC is the electoral commission, NNPC is the national oil company and CBN is the central bank. All three are part of the state, so they cannot be pressure groups outside it.\nMemory tip: Pressure groups press on the government from outside: NLC, NBA, NUT, ASUU, NANS.",
 "|The process of removing a president through a constitutional procedure is called:|A coup|A referendum|Impeachment|A vote of no confidence": "Impeachment is the constitutional process by which the legislature charges and removes a President (or Governor) for gross misconduct. In Nigeria, section 143 of the 1999 Constitution sets out the steps: a notice signed by one-third of the National Assembly members, an investigating panel, and removal by a two-thirds majority of each House. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap is D: a vote of no confidence removes a Prime Minister in a parliamentary system. A coup is unconstitutional, and a referendum is a vote by the people on an issue.\nMemory tip: Presidents are Impeached; Prime Ministers lose a vote of no confidence.",
 "|Nigeria's current federal structure has _____ states.|30|36|38|40": "Nigeria has 36 states plus the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja. The number grew in stages: 3 regions (1954), 4 regions (Mid-West, 1963), 12 states (1967), 19 (1976), 21 (1987), 30 (1991) and 36 (1996, under Abacha). So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is 37: students add the FCT, but the FCT is not a state. Options C and D (38, 40) have never been the number of Nigerian states.\nMemory tip: 3-4-12-19-21-30-36, the ladder of state creation.",
 "|What is the main feature of a unitary system of government?|Power is divided between a central and regional governments|All governmental powers are concentrated at the centre|The military controls government|Each region is fully independent": "In a unitary system all governmental power is held by one central government. Any local or regional units exist only because the centre created them, and the centre can change or abolish them. Britain, France and Ghana are examples. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: dividing power between a central and regional governments by the constitution is federalism, the exact opposite of a unitary system.\nMemory tip: Unitary = Unit = one centre of power.",
 "|The head of the Commonwealth of Nations is:|The British Prime Minister|The UN Secretary-General|The British Monarch|The Commonwealth Secretary-General": "The Commonwealth of Nations is a voluntary association of 56 countries, most of them former British territories. Its symbolic head is the British monarch, now King Charles III (since September 2022), whom member leaders accepted as head in 2018. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap is D: the Commonwealth Secretary-General runs the Secretariat in London (set up in 1965) but is the chief administrator, not the head. The British Prime Minister has no special role.\nMemory tip: Head = the Monarch; Manager = the Secretary-General.",
 "|Which of the following political philosophies advocates for the abolition of private property and state?|Capitalism|Fascism|Communism|Conservatism": "Communism, as set out by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels in the \"Communist Manifesto\" (1848), seeks to abolish private ownership of the means of production. After a stage of socialism and the \"dictatorship of the proletariat\", the state is expected to \"wither away\", leaving a classless, stateless society. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap is fascism: fascism also crushes freedom, but it glorifies the state rather than abolishing it, and it keeps private property. Capitalism rests on private property.\nMemory tip: Communism ends with \"no class, no state, no private property.\"",
 "|The legislative, executive and judicial powers in Nigeria are exercised by the:|Senate, President and Supreme Court|National Assembly, Federal Executive Council and the Judiciary|President, Vice-President and Ministers|INEC, NJC and National Assembly": "The 1999 Constitution vests legislative power in the National Assembly (s.4) and judicial power in the courts (s.6). Executive power is vested in the President (s.5), who exercises it directly or through the Vice-President and Ministers, who together form the Federal Executive Council. Of the options, B is the one that names the three organs: B.\nThe trap is A: the Senate is only one chamber of the legislature, and the Supreme Court is only one court. Option D lists agencies, not the three organs.\nMemory tip: sections 4-5-6 = legislature-executive-judiciary.",
 "|A referendum is:|A military overthrow of government|A direct vote by the electorate on a specific issue|The appointment of a head of state|A type of election for local government": "A referendum is a direct vote in which the electorate decides a particular question, such as approving a new constitution or a change of territory, instead of electing representatives. For example, the 1963 vote in the Mid-West created the Mid-Western Region. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is D: a local government election chooses people; a referendum decides an issue. A plebiscite is a similar popular vote, often on a territorial question (for example, the 1961 Cameroons plebiscites).\nMemory tip: Referendum = the people \"refer\" the question to themselves.",
 "|Which of the following is a function of INEC in Nigeria?|Adjudicating on electoral disputes|Conducting elections|Making election-related laws|Registering political parties only": "The Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC), set up in 1998 and named in section 153 of the 1999 Constitution, conducts elections. It also registers voters and political parties, monitors party finances and fixes constituencies. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps: deciding electoral disputes is the work of election tribunals and courts (A); making election laws is the National Assembly's job (C); and D is wrong because of \"only\", since INEC does far more than register parties.\nMemory tip: INEC runs elections; tribunals judge them; the National Assembly makes the rules.",
 "|Bicameralism in Nigeria means the National Assembly consists of:|The House of Representatives and the State Houses of Assembly|The Senate and the House of Representatives|The President and the Senate|The Governor and State Assembly": "Bicameral means \"two chambers.\" Nigeria's National Assembly has the Senate (109 members: three per state plus one for the FCT) and the House of Representatives (360 members). So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: State Houses of Assembly are separate state legislatures, and each one is unicameral (a single chamber). The President and Governors belong to the executive, not the legislature.\nMemory tip: \"Bi\" = two: Senate + House of Reps = 109 + 360 = 469 lawmakers.",
 "|The theory of the social contract argues that:|Might is always right|Citizens obey only when forced to|Government derives its legitimacy from the consent of the governed|Kings rule by divine right": "Social contract theorists, namely Thomas Hobbes (\"Leviathan\", 1651), John Locke (\"Two Treatises\", 1689) and Jean-Jacques Rousseau (\"The Social Contract\", 1762), argued that people left the \"state of nature\" by agreeing to form a government. Government's authority therefore rests on the consent of the governed: C.\nThe trap is D: rule by divine right is a rival theory, which says kings get their power from God and not from the people. \"Might is right\" belongs to the force theory.\nMemory tip: Contract = agreement = consent.",
 "|Which of the following distinguishes public administration from political science?|Public administration deals with implementation of policy; political science focuses on policy formulation and political theory|They are the same subject|Public administration deals with military affairs|Political science is concerned only with elections": "Political science studies power, the state, political institutions, ideas and behaviour, including how policies are made. Public administration is the branch that deals with carrying out government policy through the civil service and public agencies. So A gives the correct distinction: A.\nThe trap is B: the two subjects are related (public administration grew out of political science), but they are not the same. Options C and D shrink each subject to one narrow topic.\nMemory tip: Political science asks \"what should government do?\"; public administration asks \"how is it done?\"",
 "|What is gerrymandering?|Electoral malpractice of ballot-box snatching|Manipulation of constituency boundaries to favour a particular party|Using military force in elections|Rigging through multiple voting": "Gerrymandering means drawing constituency boundaries in odd shapes to help one party win more seats. The name comes from Governor Elbridge Gerry of Massachusetts, whose 1812 district map looked like a salamander. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps (ballot-box snatching, multiple voting, use of force) are all real electoral malpractices, but they happen on election day. Gerrymandering happens earlier, on the map.\nMemory tip: Gerry + salamander: gerrymandering is a map trick.",
 "|The first military coup in Nigeria occurred in:|1960|1963|1966|1967": "Nigeria's first military coup took place on 15 January 1966. Young officers, often called the \"five majors\" and including Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu and Emmanuel Ifeajuna, killed Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa, Sir Ahmadu Bello and Chief S.L. Akintola. Major-General J.T.U. Aguiyi-Ironsi then took power. So the answer is 1966: C.\nThe trap is 1967: that is the year the civil war began (6 July 1967) and the 12 states were created (27 May 1967). The second coup also came in 1966 (29 July), when Gowon took over.\nMemory tip: 1966 = two coups (January and July); 1967 = war and 12 states.",
 "|Under the Nigerian Constitution, the tenure of the President is:|4 years, renewable once|5 years, renewable once|4 years, renewable twice|6 years, not renewable": "Under section 135 of the 1999 Constitution the President holds office for four years, and under section 137 a person cannot be elected President more than twice. So the tenure is 4 years, renewable once: A.\nThe trap is B: five-year terms exist in some other countries, and some Nigerians have proposed a single longer term, but the Nigerian Constitution says four years. State Governors also serve four years, renewable once (ss.180 and 182).\nMemory tip: \"4 + 4 and no more.\"",
 "|Colonialism in Africa was justified by Europeans on the basis of:|Resource sharing|The 'White Man's Burden' — a self-imposed duty to 'civilise' Africans|African invitation|UN mandate": "Europeans defended colonial rule with the claim that they had a duty to \"civilise\" Africans and Asians, the \"White Man's Burden\", a phrase from Rudyard Kipling's 1899 poem. Behind the claim were economic motives: raw materials, markets and investment. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps: colonial rule was imposed by conquest and treaties signed under pressure, not by any \"African invitation\"; and the UN was created only in 1945, long after Africa was partitioned at the Berlin Conference of 1884–85.\nMemory tip: The excuse was \"civilising\"; the real aim was resources and markets.",
 "|Absolute sovereignty was advocated by:|John Locke|Montesquieu|Jean Bodin|Aristotle": "The French writer Jean Bodin, in \"Six Books of the Commonwealth\" (1576), was the first to describe sovereignty as absolute, perpetual and indivisible power to make law. Thomas Hobbes later took a similar view. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap is John Locke: Locke argued for limited government with rights the ruler must respect, the opposite of absolute sovereignty. Montesquieu wanted power divided, not absolute.\nMemory tip: Bodin = Boundless sovereignty; John Austin later developed the legal theory of sovereignty.",
 "|The practice of delegating power from a higher to a lower level of government is called:|Devolution|Federalism|Confederation|Secession": "Devolution is the transfer of powers from a central (higher) government to regional or local (lower) governments, while the centre keeps ultimate sovereignty and can take the powers back. The UK's devolution to Scotland and Wales in 1998–99 is an example. So A is correct: A.\nThe trap is B: in federalism the constitution itself shares power, so the lower units do not owe their powers to the centre's gift. Confederation is the reverse (states give powers to the centre), and secession means breaking away.\nMemory tip: Devolution = power handed Down by the centre.",
 "|Which of the following factors does NOT promote national integration?|Common language|Tribalism|Shared history|Sporting activities": "National integration is the process of uniting different groups into one nation with a shared identity. A common language, a shared history and national sports (for example, supporting the Super Eagles together) all bring people closer. Tribalism, putting loyalty to one's ethnic group above the nation, divides people. So tribalism does NOT promote integration: B.\nStudents sometimes doubt D, but sport is a well-known unifying force in Nigeria. The NYSC (1973) and Unity Schools (from 1966) were also created to promote integration.\nMemory tip: In a NOT question, look for the one divisive option.",
 "|An embargo is a government order that:|Permits free trade|Restricts or prohibits trade with a specific country|Increases import tariffs|Promotes foreign investment": "An embargo is an official order that bans or restricts trade with a particular country, often as a political sanction. Many countries placed arms and oil embargoes on apartheid South Africa, for example. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is C: raising import tariffs makes trade dearer but does not stop it. A tariff is a tax; an embargo is a ban.\nMemory tip: Embargo = Bar on trade.",
 "|The principle of judicial review means courts can:|Make new laws|Review and invalidate laws that conflict with the constitution|Sentence criminals without trial|Create executive orders": "Judicial review is the power of the courts to examine laws and executive actions and to declare void any that go against the constitution. It became established in the USA through Marbury v. Madison (1803). Nigerian courts have the same power under the 1999 Constitution. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: courts interpret and test laws but do not make new laws; law-making belongs to the legislature. Judicial review is one of the judiciary's main checks on the other two organs.\nMemory tip: Judicial review = the court as the \"umpire\" of the constitution.",
 "|OPEC was established to:|Promote democracy in oil-producing countries|Coordinate and unify petroleum policies among member countries|Build oil refineries in Africa|Regulate global energy consumption": "The Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) was founded in Baghdad on 14 September 1960 by Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela. Its aim is to coordinate and unify the petroleum policies of its members and secure fair, stable prices. Its headquarters has been in Vienna, Austria, since 1965. Nigeria joined in 1971. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is D: OPEC manages its members' oil supply but cannot regulate how the world consumes energy.\nMemory tip: OPEC = Baghdad 1960, Vienna headquarters, Nigeria 1971.",
 "|The ombudsman is an official appointed to:|Conduct elections|Investigate citizens' complaints against government maladministration|Manage the economy|Oversee military operations": "An ombudsman is an independent official who investigates citizens' complaints against injustice or maladministration by government officials and agencies. The office began in Sweden in 1809. Nigeria's version is the Public Complaints Commission, set up in 1975. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: conducting elections is the job of the electoral commission (INEC). The ombudsman deals with complaints about the administration.\nMemory tip: Ombudsman = the \"people's complaints officer\"; in Nigeria, the Public Complaints Commission (1975).",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a pillar of democracy?|Free and fair elections|Rule of law|Respect for fundamental rights|Single-party rule": "The pillars of democracy include free and fair elections, rule of law, respect for fundamental rights, an independent judiciary, a free press and competition among political parties. Single-party rule removes the people's choice and is a feature of authoritarian states. So it is NOT a pillar of democracy: D.\nThe trap is to hesitate over B: rule of law is a pillar because it protects everyone, including the government's opponents.\nMemory tip: Democracy needs CHOICE, and one party means no choice.",
 "|The Clifford Constitution of 1922 was significant because it:|Granted Nigeria independence|Introduced elective representation in Nigeria for the first time|Created the First Republic|Divided Nigeria into North and South": "The Clifford Constitution of 1922, named after Governor Sir Hugh Clifford, introduced the elective principle in Nigeria. Four seats in the Legislative Council were elected (three for Lagos and one for Calabar) by adult males earning at least £100 a year. This led Herbert Macaulay to form the NNDP in 1923, Nigeria's first political party. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps: independence came in 1960, the First Republic in 1963, and the North–South split dates from 1900 (the two protectorates), not from 1922.\nMemory tip: Clifford = first Choice (election), 1922.",
 "|Which theory holds that the state originated through force and conquest?|Social contract theory|Force or conquest theory|Divine right theory|Evolutionary theory": "The force (or conquest) theory holds that states began when strong groups conquered and ruled weaker ones, so that the state was born of power, not agreement. So B is correct: B.\nThe other options are rival theories. Social contract says the state came from an agreement among people; divine right says it came from God; evolutionary (natural) theory says it grew slowly from the family to the clan, tribe and state.\nMemory tip: Force = Fight; Contract = Consent; Divine = God; Evolution = Growth.",
 "|The concept of 'balance of power' in international relations refers to:|Equal distribution of economic resources|A system where no single state becomes dominant by ensuring roughly equal power among major states|Equal military spending|UN peacekeeping operations": "Balance of power is the idea that peace is kept when power is shared fairly evenly among the major states or alliances, so that no single state can dominate the others. Weaker states join together against any power that grows too strong. Europe in the 19th century and the Cold War balance between the USA and USSR are examples. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: balance of power concerns military and political strength among states, not equal sharing of wealth.\nMemory tip: Balance of power = a see-saw that no state can tip for good.",
 "|Local government exists at which tier in Nigeria?|First tier|Second tier|Third tier|Fourth tier": "Nigeria has three tiers of government: federal (first), state (second) and local government (third). The 1976 Local Government Reform made local government the third tier of government across the country, and the 1979 and 1999 Constitutions confirmed it. There are 774 local government areas today. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap is B: the second tier is the state government. There is no fourth tier.\nMemory tip: Federal–State–Local = 1–2–3; local government is the government closest to the people.",
 "|The concept of 'rule of law' as developed by A.V. Dicey includes all of the following EXCEPT:|Supremacy of law over arbitrary power|Equality before the law|The constitution is the result of rights of individuals as defined by courts|Laws are made only by the military": "Dicey's rule of law (1885) rests on three ideas: supremacy of law (no arbitrary power), equality before the law, and the principle that the constitution's rules come from court decisions on individual rights. Laws \"made only by the military\" is not part of it; rule by decree is the opposite of the rule of law. So D is the exception: D.\nThe trap is C: its wording sounds odd, but it is Dicey's third principle, that rights in Britain grew out of court judgments.\nMemory tip: Dicey's three S-E-C: Supremacy, Equality, Court-made constitution.",
 "|Which of the following is a characteristic of a unitary state?|Powers are shared between national and regional governments by constitution|There is a single, centralised government from which all authority derives|Different regions have equal constitutional powers to the centre|The military controls regional governments": "In a unitary state there is one central government from which all authority comes. Any regional or local units are created by the centre and can be changed or abolished by it. Britain and France are examples, and Nigeria briefly became unitary under the Unification Decree No. 34 of May 1966. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: sharing powers by the constitution between national and regional governments is the definition of federalism.\nMemory tip: Unitary = one source of power; Federal = shared by the constitution.",
 "|The concept of 'sovereignty' in political science refers to:|The military power of a state|The supreme and ultimate authority to make and enforce law within a territory|The elected president's personal power|The ability to wage war": "Sovereignty is the supreme and final legal authority of a state to make and enforce laws within its territory, without control from outside. Internally, no body is above it; externally, it means independence from other states. Jean Bodin and John Austin are its classic theorists. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps are A and D: military strength and the ability to wage war may help a state defend its sovereignty, but they are not what sovereignty means. Sovereignty belongs to the state, not to one person (C).\nMemory tip: Sovereignty = supreme legal authority, from within and free from outside.",
 "|In Nigeria's 1999 Constitution, the National Assembly consists of:|Only the Senate|Only the House of Representatives|The Senate (109 senators) and the House of Representatives (360 members)|The National Council of State only": "The National Assembly (s.47, 1999 Constitution) has two chambers. The Senate has 109 members: three from each of the 36 states plus one from the FCT. The House of Representatives has 360 members elected from federal constituencies. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap is D: the National Council of State is an advisory body (made up of the President, former Heads of State, Governors and others), not a legislature.\nMemory tip: 36 × 3 + 1 = 109 senators; 360 Reps.",
 "|The Organisation of African Unity (OAU) was transformed into the African Union (AU) in:|1999|2001|2002|2005": "The OAU was founded in Addis Ababa on 25 May 1963. African leaders agreed to replace it with the African Union in the Sirte Declaration (1999). The AU's Constitutive Act was adopted at Lomé in 2000 and came into force in May 2001, and the AU was formally launched at Durban, South Africa, on 9 July 2002. So the answer is 2002: C.\nThe trap is 2001: the treaty came into force that year, but the OAU was not replaced until the Durban launch in 2002. Both bodies have their headquarters in Addis Ababa.\nMemory tip: OAU 1963 → AU 2002; both based in Addis Ababa.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a function of a constitution?|It defines the structure of government|It guarantees fundamental rights|It specifies how elections are conducted|It determines the prices of commodities": "A constitution sets out the structure of government, protects fundamental rights and can lay down how elections are conducted (Nigeria's 1999 Constitution, for example, sets the rules for electing the President). It does not fix prices of goods; that is economic policy, handled by government and markets. So D is NOT a function: D.\nThe trap is C: some students think elections are left entirely to ordinary laws, but constitutions often contain the main electoral rules, with an Electoral Act filling in details.\nMemory tip: Constitutions deal with power and rights, not prices.",
 "|Proportional representation as an electoral system means:|The candidate with the most votes wins regardless of vote share|Seats in parliament are allocated in proportion to the votes each party receives|Only two parties are allowed to contest|Voters rank candidates in order of preference": "Proportional representation gives each party seats in proportion to the votes it wins nationally or regionally, so a party with 40% of the votes gets about 40% of the seats. Small parties and minorities therefore gain representation. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is D: ranking candidates in order of preference is the alternative (preferential) vote. It is used within one form of PR, the single transferable vote, but it is not the definition of PR. Option A describes the simple-majority system.\nMemory tip: PR = Percent of votes → Percent of seats.",
 "|The term 'pressure group' in politics refers to:|A group that contests elections|An organisation that seeks to influence government policy without seeking political power itself|The military junta|A coalition government": "A pressure (interest) group is an organised body that tries to influence government policy for its members or a cause, but does not seek to form a government or put up candidates. Examples in Nigeria are the NLC, the Nigerian Bar Association (NBA), ASUU and NANS. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: contesting elections to win power is what makes a group a political party. That is the key difference between a party and a pressure group.\nMemory tip: Parties want to BE the government; pressure groups want to INFLUENCE the government.",
 "|Which of the following was a major cause of political instability in post-independence Africa?|Too much economic development|Ethnic/tribal politics combined with weak institutions, military interventions, and colonial boundary legacies|Strong democratic traditions|Absence of political parties": "After independence, many African states suffered coups and conflict. The causes were ethnic rivalry in states whose colonial borders grouped rival peoples together, weak institutions, corruption, and repeated military interventions; Nigeria's coups of 1966 and civil war of 1967–70 are examples. So B is correct: B.\nThe other options would make a state more stable, not less. Strong democratic traditions and real economic development reduce instability. Political parties were not absent: many countries had too many ethnically based parties.\nMemory tip: In \"cause of instability\" questions, choose the option that names division and weakness.",
 "|Judicial independence means:|Judges are elected by the people|The judiciary operates free from interference by the executive and legislature in its decisions|Judges can make any law they want|Courts are run by the military": "Judicial independence means judges decide cases according to the law, free from pressure by the executive, the legislature or anyone else. In Nigeria it is protected by appointment on the recommendation of the National Judicial Council, security of tenure, salaries charged on the Consolidated Revenue Fund, and immunity for judicial acts. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: electing judges would tie them to politics. Nigerian judges are appointed, not elected.\nMemory tip: Independent judge = fixed tenure, fixed pay, no fear.",
 "|The principle of 'checks and balances' in government ensures that:|All government officials are paid equally|Each branch of government has powers to limit and oversee the others, preventing abuse of power|The judiciary controls the executive|Only the legislature makes laws": "Checks and balances means each organ of government has some power to limit the others. The President can veto bills; the legislature can override a veto by a two-thirds vote, approve appointments and impeach; the courts can strike down unconstitutional laws. This prevents abuse of power. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is C: the judiciary checks the executive, but the executive and legislature also check the judiciary (appointments, removal of judges), so no single organ controls another.\nMemory tip: Separation of powers divides power; checks and balances makes each part watch the others.",
 "|The ECOWAS (Economic Community of West African States) was established in:|1960|1975|1980|1963": "ECOWAS was established by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975 by 15 West African states, led by Nigeria's General Yakubu Gowon and Togo's General Gnassingbé Eyadéma. Its aim is economic integration. So the year is 1975: B.\nThe traps: 1963 is when the OAU was founded and 1960 is when Nigeria became independent. Membership has changed over time: Cape Verde joined in 1977, Mauritania left in 2000, and Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger left in January 2025, so ECOWAS has 12 members today, with its headquarters in Abuja.\nMemory tip: ECOWAS = Lagos, 28 May 1975.",
 "|Which of the following correctly describes a 'coalition government'?|A government formed by a single dominant party|A government formed by two or more parties joining together to command a parliamentary majority|A military government with civilian advisors|A government with no opposition": "A coalition government is formed when two or more parties combine to get a majority in parliament, usually because no single party has won one. In Nigeria's First Republic, the NPC and NCNC formed a coalition federal government in 1959 under Tafawa Balewa. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: a government of one dominant party is single-party government. A government with no opposition (D) can be a \"government of national unity\", but that is not what defines a coalition.\nMemory tip: Coalition = Combination of parties.",
 "|In Nigeria, the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) is established under which section of the 1999 Constitution?|Section 153|Section 14|Section 36|Section 270": "Section 153 of the 1999 Constitution sets up the Federal Executive Bodies. There are 14 of them, including the Code of Conduct Bureau, the Federal Character Commission and the Independent National Electoral Commission (s.153(1)(f)). Their composition and powers are in the Third Schedule. So A is correct: A.\nThe traps: section 14 states the fundamental objectives (government's duty to promote welfare, federal character); section 36 is the right to fair hearing; section 270 deals with the State High Courts.\nMemory tip: s.153 = the list of federal commissions, from Code of Conduct to Revenue Mobilisation.",
 "|The term 'bicameralism' refers to:|A legislature with one chamber|A legislature divided into two separate houses or chambers|Two competing governments|Two political parties sharing power": "Bicameralism means a legislature has two separate chambers, usually an upper house and a lower house. Nigeria's National Assembly (the Senate and the House of Representatives), the US Congress and the UK Parliament (Lords and Commons) are examples. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is A: a legislature with one chamber is unicameral, like Nigeria's State Houses of Assembly and Ghana's Parliament. \"Two parties sharing power\" is a coalition, not bicameralism.\nMemory tip: Bi = two, as in bicycle (two wheels) and bicameral (two chambers).",
 "|Power differs from influence in that it is|persuasive while influence is directive|coercive while influence is harmful|coercive while influence is persuasive|arrogant while influence is corruptible": "Power is the capacity to make people do what they would not otherwise do, because disobedience brings sanctions: arrest, fines, loss of office, force. Influence is the ability to change people's behaviour by persuasion, example or reputation; they follow willingly and can refuse without punishment. So power is coercive while influence is persuasive: C. The trap: A reverses the pair, giving the persuasive quality to power. B and D attach moral labels (harmful, arrogant, corruptible) that are not part of either definition. Memory tip: Power Pushes (with sanctions), Influence Invites (with persuasion).",
 "|The standing committee of legislature is one|whose members stand while deliberating|that has statutory responsibilities|that performs ad hoc functions|that has all legislators as members": "Legislatures do most detailed work in committees. A standing committee is permanent: it is created by the standing orders (rules) of the house, lasts for the whole life of the legislature and has fixed, statutory responsibilities, such as the committees on finance, defence or public accounts: B. The trap: C describes an ad hoc (select) committee, set up for one task and dissolved when it reports. D describes the Committee of the Whole House, where all members sit as a committee. A is a pun on \"standing\". Memory tip: standing = permanent and statutory; ad hoc = one job, then gone.",
 "|Where the constitution is supreme, unconstitutional acts of the executive and the legislature can be checked by the courts through|recall|judicial review|vote of no confidence|impeachment": "When the constitution is supreme, every law and executive act must agree with it. Judicial review is the power of the courts to examine such acts and declare them null and void if they break the constitution. It was established in the USA in Marbury v. Madison (1803) and is used by Nigerian courts too: B. The trap: impeachment (D) also checks the executive, but it is done by the legislature, not the courts, and it removes a person rather than voiding an act. Recall (A) is done by voters; a vote of no confidence (C) is done by parliament. Memory tip: courts check by REVIEW; legislatures check by IMPEACHMENT; voters check by RECALL.",
 "|The central decision-making organ of a confederation is made up of|technocrats appointed by the units|politicians elected from the confederal constituencies|politicians nominated by governments of member states|representatives of pressure groups": "A confederation is a loose union of sovereign states that keep most power and create a weak centre for limited common purposes (examples: the USA under the Articles of Confederation, 1781–1789; Senegambia, 1982–1989). Because the member states remain sovereign, the central body is made up of delegates nominated by the member governments, who act on their governments' instructions: C. The trap: B describes a federation, where the people elect central representatives directly. Technocrats (A) and pressure groups (D) do not make up the decision-making organ. Memory tip: in a confederation, the centre belongs to the states; in a federation, power is shared under a constitution.",
 "|Which of the following is true of a parliamentary system of government?|Clear separation of government organs|Strict operation of bicameral legislature|Removal of government by impeachment|Adherence to majority rule": "In a parliamentary system (Britain, Nigeria 1960–1966), the executive comes out of the legislature. The party or coalition with a majority of seats forms the government, its leader becomes Prime Minister, and the government survives only while the majority supports it. The whole system rests on majority rule: D. The trap: A is the mark of the presidential system; a parliamentary system has fusion of powers, not separation. Removal by impeachment (C) is also presidential; a parliamentary government falls by a vote of no confidence. Many parliamentary systems are unicameral, so B is wrong. Memory tip: parliamentary = fusion + majority + no-confidence vote; presidential = separation + fixed term + impeachment.",
 "|A major feature of authoritarianism is that government is|consensual|personalized|centralized|decentralized": "Authoritarianism is rule in which power is held by one leader or a small group who are not accountable to the people, tolerate little opposition and ignore constitutional limits. Because institutions are weak, government revolves around the ruler's person: decisions reflect his wishes, loyalty to him matters more than law, and the state is identified with him. That is personalized government: B. The trap: C, centralized, is tempting because authoritarian rulers concentrate power, but centralization alone is also a feature of democratic unitary states such as Britain, so it does not mark out authoritarianism. Consensual (A) describes democracy. Memory tip: authoritarianism = rule by persons, not by law.",
 "|The central point of capitalism, as expounded by Karl Marx, is that|capitalists' profit is the surplus value obtained from workers' labour|workers are inherently incapable of being owners of their labour|capitalists shall always increase workers' earning capacity through wages|capitalists shall always readily consent to workers' welfare demands": "Karl Marx (Das Kapital, 1867) taught that labour creates value. A worker produces goods worth more than the wages he is paid; the difference is surplus value, which the capitalist keeps as profit. For Marx this is the root of exploitation and class conflict between the bourgeoisie (owners) and the proletariat (workers): A. The trap: C and D describe a kindly capitalism that Marx rejected; he argued capitalists keep wages as low as possible. B misstates his view, since Marx believed workers would one day own the means of production. Memory tip: Marx's formula: value of goods minus wages = surplus value = profit.",
 "|A constitution that requires a plebiscite or a referendum to be amended is|rigid|unwritten|flexible|written": "A rigid constitution is one that cannot be amended by an ordinary act of the legislature; it needs a special procedure, such as a two-thirds majority, approval by the state assemblies, or a referendum (plebiscite) of the people: A. The USA and Nigeria have rigid constitutions. The trap: D, written, is tempting because most rigid constitutions are written, but a written constitution can still be flexible if it is easy to change. The question asks about the amendment procedure, which is what rigid and flexible describe. Memory tip: rigid or flexible = how it is changed; written or unwritten = the form it takes.",
 "|An important function of a constitution is that it|provides a framework for the study of government|facilitates cross-fertilization of ideas of governance|serves as the fountainhead of authority for the exercise of power|promotes citizen participation in government and administration": "A constitution is the fundamental law that creates the organs of government, shares out their powers and limits them. Every authority (President, legislature, courts, states) gets its power from the constitution and must act within it, so the constitution is the fountainhead (source) of authority: C. The trap: A sounds sensible, because students do study constitutions, but that is a by-product, not a function. D is a possible effect, and B is not a function at all. Memory tip: ask \"where does this official's power come from?\"; the answer is always the constitution.",
 "|When a bill passed by the legislature is vetoed by the executive, the action underscores the principle of|probity and accountability|separation of power|collective responsibility|checks and balances": "Checks and balances means each arm of government has some power to restrain the others so that none becomes too powerful. The executive's veto checks the legislature, and the legislature can in turn override the veto by a two-thirds majority (in Nigeria and the USA): D. The trap: B, separation of powers, divides functions among the three arms (making, executing, interpreting laws), but the veto is one arm stepping into another's work, which is exactly what checks and balances allows. Collective responsibility (C) belongs to a cabinet in a parliamentary system. Memory tip: separation = who does what; checks and balances = who stops whom.",
 "|In the legislative process, a bill is a|motion accepted for debate|motion rejected after debate|proposal before the legislature|law passed by the legislature": "A bill is a draft of a proposed law brought before the legislature. It goes through first reading, second reading (debate on principles), committee stage, report stage and third reading in each house, and becomes an act (a law) only after it is passed and receives the executive's assent: C. The trap: D describes an act, not a bill; once passed and signed, it is no longer a bill. A and B describe a motion, which is a proposal for the house to take a decision, not a draft law. Memory tip: bill = draft law going in; act = law coming out.",
 "|One of the advantages of bicameral over unicameral legislature is that it|is cheap to maintain|promotes social equality|takes less time for bills to be passed|prevents the passage of ill-considered bills": "A bicameral legislature has two chambers (for example, Nigeria's Senate and House of Representatives). Every bill must pass both, so the second chamber revises the work of the first and can delay or stop hasty, ill-considered bills: D. It also allows equal representation of the federating units in a federal state. The trap: A and C are actually advantages of a unicameral legislature, which is cheaper and passes bills faster. B has nothing to do with the number of chambers. Memory tip: two houses = a second look; one house = cheaper and quicker.",
 "|The fundamental rights of citizens include rights to|free education, employment and freedom of thought|life, speech and association|life, liberty and property|association, property and social security": "Fundamental rights are the basic rights written into the constitution and enforceable in court. The classic trio, from John Locke, is life, liberty and property, and all three are protected in Chapter IV of Nigeria's constitution: C. Option B (life, speech, association) is also accepted, because the rights to life, freedom of expression and freedom of association are all in Chapter IV too. The trap: A and D include free education, employment and social security, which are in Chapter II (Fundamental Objectives and Directive Principles of State Policy); they are goals of the state and cannot be enforced in court. Memory tip: Chapter IV = rights you can sue for; Chapter II = policy goals you cannot.",
 "|The manipulation of boundaries of constituencies in order to win more seats is called|devolution|rigging|gerrymandering|delimitation": "Gerrymandering is the unfair drawing of constituency boundaries to favour one party, for example by packing opponents into a few seats or spreading them thinly. The word comes from Elbridge Gerry, governor of Massachusetts, whose 1812 district was shaped like a salamander: C. The trap: D, delimitation, is the lawful, neutral process of dividing a country into constituencies (done by INEC in Nigeria). Gerrymandering is delimitation abused. Rigging (B) is electoral fraud on voting or counting. Devolution (A) is the transfer of power to lower levels of government. Memory tip: Gerry + salamander = gerrymander, a boundary drawn into a strange shape for gain.",
 "|One argument against a multi-party system is the|encouragement of opposition and instability|banning of interest groups|inability to attract foreign assistance|high cost of conducting elections": "A multi-party system has three or more parties competing for power. Its critics point to its costs: many parties mean many campaigns, candidates, ballot papers, run-off elections and subsidies, which makes elections very expensive for the state and the parties: D. It can also produce weak coalitions and ethnic parties. The trap: A is tempting, but the encouragement of opposition is an argument for multi-partyism, since a strong opposition keeps government accountable, so the option mixes a merit with a demerit. Multi-partyism does not ban interest groups (B) or stop foreign aid (C). Memory tip: more parties, more choice, but more cost.",
 "|Associational interest groups are organized to|further the interests of members|specifically lobby the government|support the government|achieve goals affecting other associations": "Associational interest groups are formally organised, with constitutions, officers and members, to promote the specific interests of their members. Examples: the Nigerian Labour Congress, the Nigerian Bar Association, the Nigerian Medical Association, and the Manufacturers Association of Nigeria. They use lobbying, strikes and publicity as means: A. The trap: B, lobbying, is one method they use, not the reason they exist, and many do not lobby only government. C is wrong because they often oppose government. Memory tip: group types: associational (formal, members' interests), institutional (inside government), non-associational (informal kin or ethnic), anomic (riots).",
 "|Public opinion is view that is|held by the majority|active in the public realm|widely publicized|no longer a secret": "Public opinion is the collective view held by the majority of people in a society on a public issue at a particular time, for example on fuel prices or a new law. Governments measure it through elections, opinion polls, the media and protests: A. The trap: C, widely publicized, describes how an opinion spreads, not whose it is; a view pushed by the media may still be held by only a few. B and D are vague and do not define the term. Memory tip: public opinion = what most people think about a public issue now.",
 "|The political neutrality of civil servants implies that they|are not allowed to join any organization or group|have no dealings with politicians|are not allowed to be involved in partisan politics|are not allowed to vote": "Political neutrality means civil servants serve whichever government is in power with equal loyalty. To make this possible they may not join political parties, campaign, stand for election or publicly support a party while in service; they must stay out of partisan politics: C. They keep their right to vote, because voting is secret and does not show partisanship. The trap: D goes too far, since civil servants are citizens and may vote. A is also too wide: they may join professional bodies and unions. Memory tip: neutral civil servants vote quietly but never campaign loudly.",
 "|The idea of making the civil service permanent, neutral and anonymous is to|enhance efficiency in administration|ensure loyalty and support|prevent opposition to government|make civil servants a functional elite": "Three principles of the civil service: permanence (officials stay when governments change, keeping experience and continuity), neutrality (they serve any party in power) and anonymity (ministers, not officials, take public credit or blame). Together they let the service give frank advice and carry out policy professionally, without fear or favour, making administration efficient: A. The trap: B, loyalty and support, sounds right, but neutrality means loyalty to the state and the government of the day, not personal or party support. C and D are not aims of the principles. Memory tip: P-N-A (Permanent, Neutral, Anonymous) = an efficient, steady service.",
 "|The western zone of the Sokoto caliphate was administered from|Kebbi|Ilorin|Bida|Gwandu": "After the Fulani jihad (1804), Usman dan Fodio divided the Sokoto caliphate in about 1812 into two parts for easier administration. The eastern half, with Sokoto as its centre, went to his son Muhammad Bello; the western half, administered from Gwandu, went to his brother Abdullahi: D. Western emirates such as Ilorin and Nupe (Bida) owed allegiance to Gwandu. The trap: Ilorin (B) and Bida (C) were emirates under the Gwandu zone, not its headquarters. Kebbi (A) was a Hausa kingdom in the area, not the zone's headquarters. Memory tip: Bello in the East at Sokoto; Abdullahi in the West at Gwandu.",
 "|Some pre-colonial Nigerian societies are described as stateless because|they had no formal governmental institutions|they had no definite political boundaries|their population was too small|they were not independent": "Stateless (acephalous, \"headless\") societies had no central government, no king or chief with overall authority, and no formal institutions such as a standing army or a bureaucracy. Authority was spread among family heads, elders' councils, age grades, title holders, oracles and village assemblies. Most pre-colonial Igbo and Tiv communities were like this: A. The trap: B is wrong because these communities did have known territories. C is wrong because some were very populous. D is wrong because they were independent communities. \"Stateless\" refers to the absence of centralised government, not of land, people or independence. Memory tip: stateless = headless (acephalous), not lawless.",
 "|The method used by the British to facilitate the administration of Southern Nigeria was|persuasion|dialogue|divide and rule|trade concession": "In Southern Nigeria the British kept the Colony of Lagos, the Western and Eastern provinces and many ethnic groups apart, encouraged rivalry among them, and later ruled North and South differently. This divide and rule policy stopped the people from uniting against colonial authority: C. The trap: A and B (persuasion, dialogue) do not describe colonial rule, which rested on conquest and force (for example the 1897 expedition against Benin). D, trade concession, describes the earlier Royal Niger Company era of trade, not a method of administration. Memory tip: keep them divided and they cannot unite: divide and rule.",
 "|A major function of the Warrant chiefs was to|prevent tribal wars|supervise native courts and markets|stop ritual killings|take charge of local administration": "Indirect rule worked in the North because emirs already had authority. In the East, where most communities had no kings, the British created warrant chiefs: local men given a \"warrant\" to act as the colonial government's agents in local administration. They collected taxes, kept order, provided labour and sat in native courts: D. Their unpopularity helped cause the 1929 Aba Women's Riot. The trap: B is one of their duties, but the question asks for the major function, which is the whole local administration. A and C were not their main job. Memory tip: Warrant chief = a Created chief for the stateless East.",
 "|After 1945, the demand of African nationalists changed from reform to independence because|colonial rule became less oppressive|colonial rule was in disarray|the second world war boosted their morale|the second world war enhanced colonial rule": "Before 1945 Nigerian nationalists mostly asked for reforms within colonial rule: more seats in the Legislative Council, more jobs for Africans. After the Second World War (1939–1945) they demanded independence. Ex-servicemen had seen that white men could be defeated, the Atlantic Charter (1941) promised self-determination, and India's independence (1947) showed it could be done. The war boosted their morale and confidence: C. The trap: B is tempting, but Britain's colonial administration was still in firm control, not in disarray. A and D would have reduced, not increased, the demand. Memory tip: WWII = Waking up: soldiers returned, morale rose, demands grew.",
 "|When Nigeria achieved independence in 1960, the Head of State was the|President|Prime Minister|Governor-General|Queen of England": "At independence on 1 October 1960, Nigeria was a dominion within the Commonwealth. The British monarch, Queen Elizabeth II, remained Head of State (the paper calls her the Queen of England), and she was represented in Nigeria by a Governor-General (first Sir James Robertson, then Nnamdi Azikiwe from November 1960). Real power lay with the Prime Minister, Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa: D. The trap: C, Governor-General, only represented the Head of State. B, Prime Minister, was Head of Government. A: Nigeria had no President until it became a republic on 1 October 1963. Memory tip: 1960 = Queen (via Governor-General); 1963 = President Azikiwe.",
 "|The central legislature of Nigeria became bicameral in|1963|1951|1959|1960": "Before independence the federal legislature had only a House of Representatives. Constitutional changes made at the end of 1959 created a Senate representing the regions, and the Senate first sat in January 1960, with Nnamdi Azikiwe as its first President. So the Parliament of independent Nigeria in 1960 had two houses: D. The trap: B, 1951, is when the Northern and Western regional legislatures became bicameral (Houses of Chiefs), not the central legislature. 1963 is the year Nigeria became a republic. 1959 is when the Senate was provided for, but it did not sit until 1960. Memory tip: regions got two houses in 1951; the centre got its Senate in 1960.",
 "|Which of these constitutions recognized local government as a third tier of government in Nigeria?|1946 constitution|1960 constitution|1963 constitution|1979 constitution": "The 1976 local government reform under Obasanjo created a uniform system of elected councils. The 1979 constitution then entrenched it: section 7 guaranteed democratically elected local government councils, and the councils got a share of federal revenue. Local government thus became the third tier of government after the federal and state tiers: D. The 1999 constitution kept this. The trap: the 1960 and 1963 constitutions (B, C) left local government entirely to the regions. The 1946 Richards constitution (A) dealt with native authorities under colonial rule. Memory tip: 1976 reform, then 1979 entrenchment; the third tier is a 1970s creation.",
 "|Under the 1963 constitution, items not listed in the exclusive and concurrent lists were within the exclusive competence of the|Executive|Federal parliament|Regional legislatures|Judiciary": "The 1960 and 1963 constitutions split legislative powers into three groups. The exclusive list (defence, currency, foreign affairs) was for the federal parliament alone. The concurrent list (higher education, industrial development) was shared, with federal law prevailing where they clashed. Everything on neither list, the residual matters (such as chieftaincy and local government), belonged to the regional legislatures: C. The trap: B, the federal parliament, controlled the exclusive list. In the USA too, residual powers stay with the states. The executive and judiciary (A, D) do not make laws. Memory tip: Exclusive = federal; Concurrent = both; Residual = regions/states.",
 "|Before Nigeria became a republic, the highest body charged with the administration of justice was the|Privy Council|High Court|Supreme Court|Court of Appeal": "Until 1963 the final court of appeal for Nigeria was the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council in London. Appeals went from the Federal Supreme Court to the Privy Council: A. When Nigeria became a republic on 1 October 1963, appeals to the Privy Council were abolished and the Supreme Court of Nigeria became the highest court. The trap: C, Supreme Court, is the right answer after 1963, not before. The Court of Appeal (D) was created only in 1976 (as the Federal Court of Appeal). Memory tip: before the republic, London had the last word; after 1963, Lagos did.",
 "|The equivalent of a commissioner at the local government level is the|executive chairman|secretary|councillor|supervisory councillor": "At state level the governor runs ministries through commissioners. At local government level the executive chairman runs departments through supervisory councillors, who are appointed by the chairman to head areas such as health, education or works and sit in the executive committee: D. The trap: A matches the governor, not the commissioner, so the chairman is the governor's equivalent. C, councillor, is an elected member of the legislative council, who corresponds to a member of the state House of Assembly. B is the secretary to the local government, like the secretary to the state government. Memory tip: Governor : Commissioner = Chairman : Supervisory Councillor.",
 "|The Independent National Electoral Commission has the power to prepare and maintain the register of|political parties|constituencies|voters|electoral candidates": "The Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC), set up in 1998, organises federal and state elections, registers political parties and monitors their finances, and prepares and keeps the register of voters, which lists everyone entitled to vote: C. The trap: A, political parties, are registered by INEC, but the phrase \"prepare and maintain the register\" is used for voters. Constituencies (B) are delimited, not registered, and candidates (D) are nominated by parties. Memory tip: INEC keeps the voters' register; no registration, no vote.",
 "|The President of Nigeria is advised on the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the country by the|National Security Council|National Defence Council|Council of State|Federal Executive Council": "The constitution sets up several federal advisory councils. The National Defence Council advises the President on matters relating to the defence of the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Nigeria: B. The National Security Council advises on internal public security matters and the agencies that handle them. The trap: A sounds similar; remember that defence means guarding the country's borders against outside threats, while security means internal order. The Council of State (C) advises on matters such as the prerogative of mercy and national population censuses. The Federal Executive Council (D) is the cabinet. Memory tip: Defence = borders and sovereignty; Security = internal order.",
 "|The Nigerian Youth Movement collapsed as a result of|its failure to win election|shortage of funds to run its affairs|the harassments of its leadership by government|the breakup of its leadership": "The Nigerian Youth Movement, founded in 1934 (as the Lagos Youth Movement), was Nigeria's first truly national party. In 1941 a vacancy arose for a Legislative Council seat. The movement chose Ernest Ikoli over Samuel Akisanya; Azikiwe backed Akisanya, and he and many Ijebu and Igbo members left. This breakup of the leadership, along ethnic lines, ruined the NYM: D. The trap: A is wrong because the NYM had won the 1938 Lagos elections. Shortage of funds (B) and harassment (C) were not the cause. Memory tip: 1941 Ikoli–Akisanya crisis = the first ethnic split in Nigerian nationalism.",
 "|The first restructuring of the Nigeria Federation took place with the|creation of the Mid-West Region in 1963|abolition of federalism in 1966|military counter-coup of 1966|creation of states in 1967": "The federation began in 1954 with three regions (North, West, East). The first change to these units came on 9 August 1963, when the Mid-West Region was carved out of the Western Region after a referendum, making four regions: A. The trap: D, the creation of 12 states by Gowon in May 1967, was the biggest restructuring, but it came four years after the Mid-West. B, Ironsi's Unification Decree (May 1966), ended federalism briefly; it was not the first restructuring. C was a change of regime, not of structure. This is a corrected answer: some would choose 1967 as the main restructuring, but the question asks for the first. Memory tip: 3 regions (1954), 4 (1963), 12 states (1967), 19 (1976), 36 (1996).",
 "|The land use decree of 1978 vested the ownership of land in Nigeria in the|local chiefs|local governments|state governments|federal government": "Before 1978, land in Nigeria was held under many different customary and statutory systems. The Land Use Decree of 1978 (Obasanjo regime) vested all land in each state in the governor of that state, to hold in trust for the use and common benefit of all Nigerians. Local governments control land in rural areas under the governor: C. The law still applies today as the Land Use Act, entrenched in the constitution. The trap: D, the federal government, owns only land it acquires for federal purposes. A, local chiefs, lost their control under the decree. Memory tip: Land Use Act: the Governor holds the land in Trust.",
 "|The main source of financing local government in Nigeria is|internal revenue generation|statutory revenue allocation|special state grants|grants-in-aid": "Local governments in Nigeria raise some money internally (rates, fees, licences, market dues), but this is small. Their main income is statutory allocation: their constitutional share of the Federation Account, which they get monthly, plus a share of VAT: B. The trap: A is how local governments should ideally be funded, but it is the weakest source in practice. Grants-in-aid (D) and special state grants (C) are occasional. Memory tip: local government money comes mostly from Abuja, by statutory allocation.",
 "|The most remarkable legacy of the 1976 Local Government Reform in Nigeria was the introduction of|the office of sole administrators|caretaker management committees|uniformity in structure and functions|the third tier of government": "Before 1976 Nigeria had many different local systems: native authorities in the North, councils on the British model in the South, and other arrangements. The 1976 reform under Obasanjo set up one uniform, single-tier, multi-purpose local government system across the country, with similar structures and functions, elected councils and a population of 150,000 to 800,000 per council: C. Option D is also accepted: the reform guidelines first called local government the third tier of government, and the 1979 constitution entrenched this. The trap: A and B (sole administrators, caretaker committees) were features of military rule, not legacies of the reform. Memory tip: 1976 = one pattern for all of Nigeria.",
 "|The Murtala/Obasanjo regime in Nigeria increased the number of states from|4 to 12|12 to 19|19 to 21|30 to 36": "In 1967 Gowon created 12 states from the four regions. On 3 February 1976 the Murtala Muhammed government (with Obasanjo as Chief of Staff, Supreme Headquarters) created seven more, making 19 states: B. The trap: A, 4 to 12, was Gowon's 1967 change. C, 19 to 21, was Babangida's 1987 change, and Babangida went on to 30 in 1991. D, 30 to 36, was Abacha's 1996 change. Nigeria has had 36 states since 1996. Memory tip: Gowon 12, Murtala 19, Babangida 21 and 30, Abacha 36.",
 "|A major factor that influenced the formulation of Nigeria's foreign policy in the First Republic was|geographical location|the colonial legacy|economic consideration|the parliamentary system": "The First Republic (1960–1966) under Prime Minister Balewa kept a strongly pro-British, pro-Western foreign policy. That came from the colonial legacy: Nigeria's leaders were trained in Britain, its economy and trade were tied to Britain, it joined the Commonwealth, and it signed the Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact (1960–1962): B. The trap: C, economic consideration, mattered, but the economic ties themselves came from colonial rule. Geographical location (A) and the parliamentary system (D) did not shape the policy in the same way. Memory tip: Balewa's foreign policy looked toward London because of the colonial legacy.",
 "|Nigeria's departure from pro-west policy during the Murtala Muhammed regime was as a result of|the economic interests of the west in Nigeria|Nigeria's increased international influence|the growing trade between Nigeria and the East|Nigeria's concern for decolonization in Africa": "Gowon's and Balewa's foreign policies were cautious and pro-Western. When Murtala Muhammed came to power in July 1975, he made Africa the centrepiece of foreign policy and the liberation of Africa from colonial and white-minority rule a priority. Over Angola in 1975, he defied the USA, recognised the MPLA and gave it aid, and he used oil wealth to support liberation movements in Southern Africa: D. The trap: B, increased international influence, was a result of the new policy, not its cause. A and C are not correct. Memory tip: Murtala's message on Angola: \"Africa has come of age\".",
 "|During the Civil War, the major power that expressed moral support for Biafra's self-determination was|France|China|the United States|Great Britain": "During the Nigerian Civil War (1967–1970), Britain and the Soviet Union supported the federal government, and the USA stayed officially neutral. France, under President Charles de Gaulle, publicly declared sympathy for Biafra's right to self-determination in 1968 and supplied arms through Gabon and Côte d'Ivoire: A. Only four African states (Tanzania, Gabon, Côte d'Ivoire, Zambia) and Haiti recognised Biafra. The trap: D, Great Britain, supplied arms to the federal side. China (B) gave Biafra only some verbal support. Memory tip: France for Biafra; Britain and USSR for Lagos.",
 "|The Barclays Bank and the British Petroleum Company in Nigeria were nationalized in the late 1970s for transacting business with|France|South Africa|Portugal|Libya": "Nigeria fought apartheid with economic weapons. In 1978 the Obasanjo government took over Barclays Bank's Nigerian operation (it became Union Bank) and in July 1979 nationalised British Petroleum's Nigerian assets, both because the companies did business with apartheid South Africa: B. The BP takeover was also meant to pressure Britain over Rhodesia. The trap: Portugal (C) had been a colonial target until 1975, and Libya (D) and France (A) are not the reason. Memory tip: Barclays to Union Bank, BP to African Petroleum; both punished for South Africa links.",
 "|South-South cooperation is a major policy plan on which Nigeria bases her relations with|developed countries|countries of the southern hemisphere|developing countries|member countries of OAU": "\"South\" in international politics means the developing countries of Africa, Asia and Latin America, as opposed to the industrialised \"North\" (Brandt Report, 1980). South-South cooperation is cooperation among developing countries in trade, technology and aid, for example Nigeria's Technical Aid Corps: C. The trap: B takes \"South\" literally. Nigeria and most of the developing world, such as India, lie north of the equator; the term is a political label, not a hemisphere. Developed countries (A) are the North, and D is too narrow. Memory tip: North = rich, South = developing; South-South = developing helping developing.",
 "|Nigeria's recognition of the Popular Movement for the Liberation of Angola to lead the country was made under|General Yakubu Gowon|General Murtala Muhammed|General Muhammadu Buhari|General Ibrahim Babangida": "In 1975 Angola won independence from Portugal amid a civil war between three movements: the MPLA, the FNLA and UNITA. The USA and South Africa backed the MPLA's rivals. On 25 November 1975 the Murtala Muhammed government recognised the MPLA government and gave it money, swinging African opinion behind it: B. The trap: A, Gowon, was overthrown in July 1975, before the recognition. Buhari (C) and Babangida (D) came in the 1980s. Memory tip: Murtala's short rule (1975–1976) = Angola, the MPLA, and Africa as the centrepiece.",
 "|The first Nigerian Permanent Representative to the United Nations was|Alhaji Yusuf Maitama Sule|General Joseph Garba|Professor Ibrahim Gambari|Jaja Wachuku": "Nigeria joined the United Nations on 7 October 1960, a week after independence, and its first Permanent Representative (ambassador) to the UN was Jaja Wachuku (1960–1961), who had been the first Nigerian Speaker of the House of Representatives and went on to become Minister of Foreign Affairs: D. (The original paper's options were all wrong, so we rebuilt option D.) The trap: Maitama Sule (A), Joseph Garba (B) and Ibrahim Gambari (C) are all famous Nigerian UN ambassadors, but they served from 1979 onward, and Simeon Adebo came after Wachuku (1962–67). Memory tip: Nigeria's \"first\" at the UN came straight after independence: Wachuku in 1960.",
 "|In 1981, Nigeria participated in an Organization of African Unity peace-keeping force to replace Libyan force in|Somalia|Chad|Ethiopia|Zaire": "In 1980–1981 Libya had troops in Chad supporting Goukouni Oueddei's government. When they withdrew in November 1981, the OAU sent an Inter-African Force to keep the peace in Chad. Nigeria supplied the largest contingent, along with Zaire and Senegal. It was the OAU's first peacekeeping force: B. Nigeria had earlier hosted the Kano and Lagos peace accords on Chad (1979). The trap: Zaire (D) contributed troops; it was not where they were sent. Somalia (A) and Ethiopia (C) were not OAU peacekeeping sites in 1981. Memory tip: Chad is Nigeria's north-eastern neighbour; the 1981 OAU force went there after Libya left.",
 "|Nigeria's withdrawal from the Edinburgh Commonwealth Games in July 1986 was in protest against British|support to UNITA rebels in Angola|supply of arms to Rhodesia|failure to impose sanctions on South Africa|negative utterances on Nigeria": "In 1985–1986 Commonwealth leaders wanted economic sanctions against apartheid South Africa, but Britain under Margaret Thatcher refused. In protest, Nigeria led about 32 Commonwealth countries in boycotting the 1986 Commonwealth Games in Edinburgh: C. The trap: B, arms to Rhodesia, was a 1960s–1970s issue; Rhodesia became Zimbabwe in 1980. A, support to UNITA, was mainly an American and South African policy. D is not the reason. Memory tip: 1976 Montreal Olympics boycott over a New Zealand rugby tour of South Africa; 1986 Edinburgh boycott over Thatcher's refusal of sanctions; both against apartheid.",
 "|The major demand of the Third World countries on the United Nations in recent times is the|expansion of the permanent membership of the Security Council|post of the Secretary-General of the organization|withdrawal of the veto power from the Security Council|enforcement of resolutions on the superpowers": "The Security Council has five permanent members with a veto (USA, Russia, China, Britain, France), fixed in 1945 when most of Africa and Asia were colonies. Developing countries, now the majority of UN members, demand expansion of the permanent membership so that Africa, Latin America and more of Asia are represented, and Nigeria is a candidate: A. The demand remains unmet, and the permanent five are unchanged today. The trap: C, withdrawing the veto, is raised by some, but expansion is the main demand. The Secretary-General's post (B) already rotates among regions. Memory tip: P5 set in 1945; Africa wants a permanent seat at the top table.",
 "|Which of the following is the function of the Council of Ministers of the O.A.U.?|Co-ordinating the general policy of the organization|Directing the finances of the organization|Preparing the agenda of the organization's meetings|Reviewing the functions and activities of other organs of the organization": "The OAU (1963) had four main organs: the Assembly of Heads of State and Government (supreme), the Council of Ministers (foreign ministers), the General Secretariat, and the Commission of Mediation, Conciliation and Arbitration. The Council of Ministers prepared the conferences of the Assembly, including their agenda, carried out its decisions and approved the budget: C. The trap: A, co-ordinating general policy, belonged to the Assembly, the supreme organ. In 2002 the OAU became the African Union, where the Executive Council of ministers now does this work. Memory tip: Ministers prepare; Heads of State decide.",
 "|The Economic Community of West African States has made impressive progress in the area of|free movement of persons and right of residence|increased trade among members|political integration of the region|providing financial aid to its members": "ECOWAS was founded on 28 May 1975 by the Treaty of Lagos. Its 1979 Protocol on Free Movement of Persons, Right of Residence and Establishment lets community citizens enter any member state without a visa, and later stay and work there. This has been its most visible success, together with the ECOWAS passport and peacekeeping by ECOMOG: A. The trap: B, increased trade, was the founders' main goal, but trade among members has stayed low (about a tenth of their total trade), because they export similar raw materials. This answer is a correction of a debatable point. Memory tip: ECOWAS = free movement, without a visa, across West Africa.",
 "|Delegated legislation becomes unavoidable when|legislators cannot reach a consensus|issues under consideration are personal|issues under consideration are technical|legislators have to proceed on a recess": "Delegated legislation is law made by a body other than the legislature (a minister, ministry, agency or local council) under powers given by a parent act. It becomes unavoidable because modern law involves many technical details, such as drug standards, road safety rules or electricity tariffs, that legislators lack the expertise and time to work out. Parliament passes the broad act and leaves the experts to fill in the details: C. The trap: D sounds plausible, but a recess only pauses law-making; emergency powers, not delegated legislation, deal with that. A and B are not reasons at all. Memory tip: delegated legislation = Details by experts; the legislature sets the frame.",
 "|One major disadvantage of public opinion is that|the critics of government policies are always harassed|a vocal minority claims to represent the majority|gossip and rumours thrive|leaders are unnecessarily criticized": "Public opinion is the view held by most people on a public issue. Its great weakness is that it is hard to measure accurately. A small, well-organised, noisy group (a vocal minority) can dominate the media and protests and claim to speak for \"the people\", while the silent majority may think differently. Government may then act on a false picture: B. The trap: C, gossip and rumours, can colour opinion, but it is a feature of poor information, not the main defect of public opinion itself. A and D describe abuses by or against leaders, not a disadvantage of public opinion. Memory tip: loud is not the same as many.",
 "|Citizenship in a modern state expresses the status of a person who possesses|full political rights|some religious rights|social rights only|exclusive economic rights": "Citizenship is the full legal membership of a state. A citizen enjoys full civil and political rights: to vote and be voted for, to hold public office, to a passport and to live in the country. In return he owes duties such as obeying laws and paying taxes. Aliens may enjoy some civil rights, but not full political rights: A. The trap: C and D say \"only\" and \"exclusive\", which are too narrow; citizens have social and economic rights too, but these are not what mark citizenship out. B is wrong because religious freedom is usually given to everyone, citizen or not. Memory tip: citizen = full political membership; alien = guest without the vote.",
 "|Communism is a system which recognizes|class stratification|the existence of the state|the existence of the individual|the ability of the individual": "Karl Marx described communism as the final stage of society, in which classes have disappeared, the state has \"withered away\" and goods are shared by the rule \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his needs\". Everyone contributes to the common good according to what he is able to do, so communism recognizes the ability of the individual: D. The trap: C, \"the existence of the individual\", sounds humane, but it says nothing special, since every system recognizes that individuals exist. A and B describe exactly what communism aims to end: class stratification and the state. This verdict differs from the official answer key, which gives C. Memory tip: communism = ability in, needs out.",
 "|The delineation of constituencies is a major duty of the|national assembly|political parties|boundary commission|electoral commission": "Delineation (delimitation) of constituencies means dividing a country into electoral areas of roughly equal population, each returning one representative. In Nigeria this is a duty of the electoral commission: today INEC reviews the boundaries under the 1999 Constitution, usually after a census: D. In other countries, such as Britain, an independent boundary commission does it, and Nigeria itself used a Constituency Delimitation Commission in the 1950s, so C is also accepted. The trap: A, the National Assembly, only approves INEC's proposals; it does not draw the lines. Political parties (B) must never draw boundaries, or they would gerrymander. Memory tip: neutral umpires draw the lines; players never do.",
 "|The structure of the civil service is based on|lateral organization|merit system|patronage system|hierarchical organization": "The civil service is organised as a hierarchy, like a pyramid. At the top of each ministry is the Permanent Secretary (head of the ministry's service), then directors, deputy directors and so on down to junior staff. Orders flow down and responsibility flows up, each officer answering to the one directly above him: D. The trap: B, merit system, is the basis of recruitment and promotion (appointment by qualification and examination), not the shape of the structure. C, patronage, is the opposite of merit, and A, lateral organization, would mean no ranks at all. Memory tip: recruitment = merit; structure = hierarchy (the pyramid).",
 "|A common feature of a multi-party system is that government is formed by|the major political parties|all the registered political parties|a coalition of political parties|the party with the highest votes": "In a multi-party system several strong parties compete. Seats are usually shared so widely that no single party wins an outright majority in parliament. To form a government, two or more parties must agree to work together and share ministerial posts: a coalition government. Examples are Italy, Israel and Nigeria's First Republic, where the NPC and NCNC formed a coalition at the centre in 1959: C. The trap: D, the party with the highest votes, governs alone only if it also has a majority of seats, which is typical of a two-party system. B is impossible in practice. Memory tip: many parties, many partners: multi-party means coalition.",
 "|To qualify for absorption into the administrative cadre of the civil service in Nigeria, an applicant must be|knowledgeable in civil service rules|holder of a first university degree|specifically trained in public administration|a senior civil servant": "The Nigerian civil service has several cadres: administrative (senior policy officers), professional (doctors, engineers), executive, clerical and so on. The administrative cadre advises on and shapes policy, so entry requires at least a first university degree or its equivalent; the subject can be any discipline, and recruits then learn on the job: B. The trap: C, specially trained in public administration, sounds right but is not required; graduates of history, law or economics are all admitted. A comes after entry, not before. D is wrong because the cadre takes fresh graduates, not only senior officers. Memory tip: administrative cadre = degree at the door.",
 "|The final interpretation of the provisions of a federal constitution is vested in the|head of state|council of state|highest legislative body|highest court of the land": "A federal constitution divides powers between the centre and the units, so disputes over who may do what are certain. The final word on what the constitution means must belong to a neutral umpire: the highest court of the land. In Nigeria that is the Supreme Court, whose interpretation binds every government and court: D. The trap: C, the highest legislative body, makes laws but cannot be the final judge of them, otherwise it could give itself any power it liked. A, the head of state, and B, the council of state, are executive or advisory bodies. Memory tip: legislature makes, executive executes, Supreme Court has the final say on meaning.",
 "|A major issue that distinguishes pressure groups from political parties is|membership drive|the objective|the voting pattern|ideology": "Political parties and pressure groups both organise people to influence government, but their objectives differ. A political party aims to capture and control government by putting up candidates for elections. A pressure group, such as the NLC or the NBA, does not seek to rule; it only tries to influence the policies of whoever is in power in favour of its members' interests: B. The trap: D, ideology, is tempting, but both parties and pressure groups may or may not have an ideology. A membership drive is done by both. Memory tip: party wants the seat; pressure group wants the ear of whoever holds the seat.",
 "|Proportional representation is a system of allocating seats in the legislature based on|gender participation in politics|an area's involvement in politics|contribution to the national economy|total votes in an election": "Proportional representation (PR) gives each party a share of seats roughly equal to its share of the total votes cast. If a party wins 30% of the votes, it gets about 30% of the seats, usually through party lists in large multi-member constituencies: D. This contrasts with the first-past-the-post system, where the candidate with the most votes in each constituency wins and many votes are \"wasted\". The trap: B mixes it up with the idea of geographical representation; A and C describe quotas or special representation, not PR. Memory tip: PR = Proportion of votes = Proportion of seats.",
 "|The application of the principle of separation of powers seems impracticable because power is|delegated|centralized|fused|separated": "Separation of powers (Montesquieu, 1748) means legislative, executive and judicial powers should be held by different people. In practice no modern government can keep them fully apart: the executive makes delegated laws, the legislature confirms appointments, and in the parliamentary system the ministers are members of the legislature. Powers overlap, or are fused, which makes strict separation impracticable: C. The trap: B, centralized, describes a unitary state, which can still separate the three organs. A, delegated, is one example of overlap but not the general reason. Memory tip: pure separation is a theory; fusion and overlap are the practice.",
 "|One major factor that differentiates the presidential from the parliamentary system is|separation of powers|judicial independence|passage of bills|party system": "The key difference between the two systems is how the executive relates to the legislature. In the presidential system (USA, Nigeria since 1979) the president is elected separately, cannot be a member of the legislature and serves a fixed term: separation of powers. In the parliamentary system (Britain, Nigeria 1960–1966) the Prime Minister and ministers come from and sit in parliament: fusion of powers: A. The trap: B, judicial independence, is found in both systems. Both systems pass bills (C) and both have party systems (D). Memory tip: presidential = separate; parliamentary = fused.",
 "|A major consequence of proportional representation is that it|reduces the chances of political instability|favours the developments of a two-party system|discourages voting along ethnic lines|encourages the proliferation of parties": "Because proportional representation gives seats even to parties with a small share of the vote, it pays to form new parties: a party with 5% of the votes can still win about 5% of the seats. So PR encourages the growth of many parties (proliferation): D. That often produces coalition governments, which can be unstable, so A is the opposite of what usually happens. The trap: B, a two-party system, is linked with first-past-the-post (Duverger's law), not PR. C is wrong because PR can allow ethnic parties to win seats. Memory tip: PR multiplies parties; first-past-the-post squeezes them to two.",
 "|Oligarchy is a form of government which|enhances the interest of the ruling few|enhances the electoral chances of the majority|disregards the views of the minority|protects the interest of the common people": "Oligarchy (Greek oligoi, \"few\") is government by a small group of people, often the rich, a military clique or a few powerful families. Aristotle called it the corrupt form of aristocracy because the few rule mainly in their own interest rather than for the good of all: A. The trap: C, disregarding the views of the minority, is wrong, since the ruling few are the minority; it is the majority whose views are ignored. B and D describe democracy. Memory tip: aristocracy = the best few for all; oligarchy = the few for themselves.",
 "|The absence of the rule of law in government will bring about|human rights abuse|treasonable offences|corrupt practices|political apathy": "The rule of law (A. V. Dicey) means that the law is supreme, everyone is equal before it, and no one may be punished except for a breach of law proved in court. Where it is absent, rulers act arbitrarily: people are detained without trial, denied fair hearing, and lose their freedom of speech and property. The direct result is abuse of human rights: A. The trap: C, corrupt practices, often flourish too, but the rule of law exists first of all to protect individual rights. Treasonable offences (B) are crimes by citizens, and apathy (D) has many causes. Memory tip: no rule of law, no rights.",
 "|Centralization of power is the basic feature of|federalism|a confederation|a presidential system|a unitary government": "In a unitary system (Britain, France, Ghana), all governmental power is concentrated (centralized) in one central government. Any regional or local bodies exist only because the centre created them and can be changed or abolished by it: D. The trap: A, federalism, divides power between the centre and the units under the constitution, which is the opposite of centralization. B, a confederation, leaves most power with the member states. C, a presidential system, describes the relationship between executive and legislature, not between centre and units. Memory tip: unitary = one centre holds all; federal = shared; confederal = units hold most.",
 "|A law passed by the legislature and assented to by the executive is|an act|a presidential proclamation|a decree|a legislative order": "A bill becomes an act (a statute) once it has passed through the legislature and received the assent of the executive (the President or Governor in Nigeria): A. The trap: C, a decree, is a law made by a military government, which has no legislature, so it never goes through this process; edicts were the state-level version under military rule. B, a presidential proclamation, is an executive announcement, and D, a legislative order, is not a standard term for law. Memory tip: legislature + assent = act; military council = decree.",
 "|One of the features of a sovereign state is that it|has the capacity to defend itself from external aggression|has a large number of soldiers|practices the presidential system of government|is not indebted to other countries": "A sovereign state has supreme power within its territory and is independent of outside control. To keep that independence, it must be able to defend itself against external aggression; a state that cannot protect its borders soon loses its sovereignty in practice: A. The trap: B, a large number of soldiers, is not required, since small states such as Gambia are sovereign. C is wrong because sovereignty has nothing to do with presidential or parliamentary government. D is wrong because many sovereign states, Nigeria included, borrow from others. Memory tip: sovereignty = supreme inside, independent outside.",
 "|A major way of maintaining confidence in the electoral process is by ensuring that|electoral officers are regularly trained|elections are conducted in a free and fair atmosphere|unlimited franchise is observed|elections are conducted as and when due": "People trust an electoral process when they believe their votes count. That requires elections conducted in a free atmosphere (no intimidation, bribery or violence) and a fair one (equal rules for all candidates, honest counting, a neutral electoral body): B. The trap: D, elections held as and when due, matters too, but a rigged election held on schedule still destroys confidence. A, trained officers, helps achieve free and fair elections but is only a means. C, unlimited franchise, does not exist anywhere; age and citizenship always limit the vote. Memory tip: confidence comes from FREE (no fear) and FAIR (no fraud).",
 "|The principle of checks and balances reinforces separation of power in order to|protect the powers of the executive|make the legislature more powerful|prevent an unconstitutional change of government|prevent the emergence of dictatorship": "Separation of powers gives each organ its own work. Checks and balances add the means for each to restrain the others: the President can veto bills, the legislature can override vetoes, approve appointments and impeach, and the courts can strike down unconstitutional acts. The aim is to stop power from gathering in one person or organ, which is the road to dictatorship: D. The trap: C sounds close, but checks and balances work inside the constitution; they cannot stop a military coup. A and B are wrong because the system makes no organ supreme. Memory tip: check each organ, and no dictator can grow.",
 "|Legislative control of public corporations in Nigeria is effected through|the dissolution of their managements|the reorganization of their boards|acts of the National Assembly|bye-laws": "Public corporations, such as the NNPC or the old NEPA, are created by acts of the legislature. The legislature controls them through those acts: it can amend or repeal the enabling act, approve their budgets, require annual reports, and investigate them through committees such as the Public Accounts Committee: C. The trap: A and B, dissolving managements and reorganizing boards, are done by the executive (the supervising minister), not the legislature. D, bye-laws, are made by local governments. Memory tip: the legislature controls corporations through the law that gave them life.",
 "|The Ibadan Conference of 1950 was convened to discuss the provision of the|Macpherson Constitution|Clifford Constitution|Lyttleton Constitution|Richards Constitution": "The Richards Constitution (1946) was unpopular because it was imposed without consulting Nigerians. Governor John Macpherson therefore organised wide consultation, from village meetings up to the General Conference at Ibadan in January 1950, which discussed proposals for a new constitution. The result was the Macpherson Constitution of 1951: A. The trap: D, Richards, was the constitution being reviewed, not the one being prepared. Clifford (B) dated from 1922, and Lyttleton (C) came from the London and Lagos conferences of 1953–1954. Memory tip: Macpherson = the first constitution made after consulting Nigerians, at Ibadan, 1950.",
 "|The fees collected by local governments at motor parks represent|levies|fines|income tax|user charge": "Local governments raise revenue from rates, taxes, licences, fees and levies. Charges collected from drivers and transporters at motor parks are classed as levies: the Taxes and Levies (Approved List for Collection) Act of 1998 names \"motor park levies\" among the items local governments may collect: A. The trap: D, user charge, is tempting because drivers pay for using the park, and economists might use that term, but in Nigerian law and in JAMB's syllabus these are levies. B, fines, are penalties for offences. C, income tax, is levied by the states and the federal government on earnings. Memory tip: motor park = levy, market stall = rate or fee, offence = fine.",
 "|The first Nigerian constitution to provide for the fundamental objectives and directive principles of state policy was the|1979 Constitution|1989 Constitution|1999 Constitution|1960 Constitution": "The Fundamental Objectives and Directive Principles of State Policy are goals the government must work towards, such as social justice, free education where practicable and national unity. They first appeared in Chapter II of the 1979 Constitution, drafted by the Constitution Drafting Committee of 1975–1976, and were repeated in the 1989 and 1999 constitutions: A. Unlike fundamental rights (Chapter IV), they cannot be enforced in court. The paper misprinted \"directive\" as \"derivative\". The trap: C, 1999, is the current constitution, so students think of it first, but it copied 1979. D, 1960, had fundamental rights but no directive principles. Memory tip: 1979 gave Chapter II.",
 "|Under the 1999 Constitution of Nigeria, the power to create local government is vested in the|state assembly|office of the deputy governor|presidency|national assembly": "Section 8(3) of the 1999 Constitution lets a State House of Assembly create a new local government area. The bill needs support from at least two-thirds of the representatives of the area in the House and in the area's local councils, a referendum approved by two-thirds of the people of the area, and a two-thirds majority of the whole House. The National Assembly then amends the First Schedule to list the new area: A. The trap: D, the National Assembly, only lists the area afterwards; it does not start or create it. B and C are executive offices with no such power. This is still the law today. Memory tip: states create LGAs; the centre only lists them.",
 "|The body set up to review the revenue allocation formula in 1980 was the|Udoji Commission|Okigbo Commission|Dina Commission|Adebo Commission": "Revenue allocation has been reviewed many times. In 1979, President Shagari set up a commission headed by Dr Pius Okigbo; it reported in 1980 and recommended sharing the Federation Account mainly among the federal, state and local governments. Its work led to the Revenue Allocation Act of 1981: B. The trap: C, Dina (1968), came earlier and was rejected; Udoji (A, 1972–1974) reviewed public service salaries and structure, and Adebo (D, 1970–1971) reviewed wages and salaries. Today the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC) reviews the formula. Memory tip: Okigbo = the 1980 allocation review; Udoji and Adebo = pay reviews.",
 "|In pre-colonial Igbo society, decisions on public issues were reached mainly through|the oracles|consensus|imposition|majority votes": "Pre-colonial Igbo society was largely acephalous (\"headless\"): there was no central king ruling over all Igbo land. Each village was governed by the village assembly of adult males, the council of elders (ofo holders), age grades and title societies such as Ozo. Public issues were debated openly until general agreement was reached; decisions were by consensus: B. The trap: A, the oracles (such as the Arochukwu oracle), were consulted for difficult disputes and judgements, but they were not the normal way of deciding public matters. C, imposition, describes a centralised monarchy such as an emirate. Memory tip: Igbo enwe eze (the Igbo have no king): talk until everyone agrees.",
 "|The main duty of the Local Government Service Commission is to|supervise and manage the affairs of the local governments|create job opportunities at the local level|handle requests for the creation of more local governments|conduct elections into local councils": "Each state has a Local Government Service Commission that manages the local government service. It appoints, posts, promotes and disciplines senior local government staff, and so supervises how the councils are run: A. The trap: D is tempting because it sounds like a \"commission\" duty, but elections into local councils are conducted by the State Independent Electoral Commission (SIEC). C belongs to the State House of Assembly (see section 8 of the 1999 Constitution), and B, creating jobs, is a general aim of all governments. Memory tip: Service Commission = staff and management; Electoral Commission = elections.",
 "|The constitution that introduced restricted franchise into Nigerian politics was the|Independence Constitution|Lyttleton Constitution|Richards Constitution|Clifford Constitution": "The Clifford Constitution of 1922, under Governor Hugh Clifford, introduced the elective principle in Nigeria: four elected members in the Legislative Council (three for Lagos, one for Calabar). But only adult males resident in those towns with a gross annual income of at least £100 could vote, which was a restricted franchise: D. The trap: C, Richards (1946), and B, Lyttleton (1954), kept or widened voting but did not introduce it. A, the Independence Constitution of 1960, provided for universal adult suffrage in the South. Memory tip: Clifford 1922 = first election, Lagos and Calabar, £100 rule.",
 "|In Nigeria, the Council of State was first created by|General Murtala Muhammed|General Olusegun Obasanjo|Major-General Aguiyi Ironsi|General Yakubu Gowon": "After Murtala Muhammed took power in July 1975, the Constitution (Basic Provisions) Decree of 1975 set up three bodies: the Supreme Military Council (the highest), the Federal Executive Council and the National Council of States, made up of the state military governors. That was the first council of states in Nigeria: A. The 1979 Constitution later created the Council of State, which the 1999 Constitution keeps, made up of the President, former Presidents, former Chief Justices, state governors and others. The trap: D, Gowon, ruled through the Supreme Military Council, on which the governors sat, without a separate council of states. B, Obasanjo, continued Murtala's arrangement. Memory tip: Murtala 1975: SMC, FEC and the National Council of States.",
 "|The leading agent in the expansion of British influence in Northern Nigeria in the 1890s was the|British Consul in Calabar|Royal West African Frontier Force|British Consul in Lagos|Royal Niger Company": "In the 1880s–1890s British interests on the Niger and Benue were advanced by George Goldie's Royal Niger Company, which received a royal charter in 1886. It signed treaties with northern rulers, kept French and German rivals out, maintained its own constabulary and administered the area until its charter was revoked in 1899; on 1 January 1900 the British government took over and proclaimed the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria under Lugard: D. The trap: B, the West African Frontier Force, was formed by Lugard in 1897–1898 and did conquer the north after 1900, but the company led the earlier expansion. Consuls (A, C) worked on the coast. Memory tip: in the north, trade came before the flag.",
 "|The two military coups that toppled civilian regimes in Nigeria were those of|January 1966 and December 1983|July 1966 and August 1985|January 1966 and July 1975|February 1966 and December 1983": "Only two Nigerian coups overthrew elected civilian governments. On 15 January 1966 young officers led by Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu ended the First Republic, killing Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa; Ironsi took over. On 31 December 1983 Buhari's coup ended Shagari's Second Republic: A. The trap: the other coups toppled military regimes: July 1966 (Ironsi to Gowon), July 1975 (Gowon to Murtala) and August 1985 (Buhari to Babangida). D's \"February 1966\" is a wrong date: no coup took place then (the failed Dimka coup of February 1976 killed Murtala). Memory tip: civilian regimes fell in January 1966 and December 1983: start of one year, end of another.",
 "|The main function of the Code of Conduct Bureau is to|ensure accountability in government business|give the judiciary more power to discipline erring judges|protect public officers from the press|give the police more powers to make arrests": "The Code of Conduct Bureau, created by the 1979 Constitution and retained in the 1999 Constitution (Fifth Schedule), enforces the code of conduct for public officers. It receives and checks the asset declarations public officers must make, and refers breaches (such as false declarations or conflicts of interest) to the Code of Conduct Tribunal. Its purpose is honesty and accountability in public business: A. The trap: B is wrong because discipline of judges belongs to the National Judicial Council. C and D are not its functions; the Bureau protects the public from corrupt officers, not officers from the press. Memory tip: Code of Conduct = declare your assets, account for your office.",
 "|The emirate system of administration can be likened to a|confederal system of government|unitary system of government|federal system of government|constitutional monarchy": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirates, power was centralised in the emir. He appointed and could remove the district heads (hakimai) and village heads, controlled the courts through the alkalai he appointed, and collected taxes. All authority flowed from one centre, as in a unitary system of government: B. The trap: D, constitutional monarchy, is tempting because the emir is a monarch, but a constitutional monarch only reigns while others rule, whereas the emir governed with near-absolute power, limited mainly by Islamic law. A and C wrongly suggest shared or divided power within the emirate. Memory tip: emirate = one ruler, one centre = unitary.",
 "|The abolition of the state ministries of local government in 1989 entails that local governments|are equal to the states|have more control over their funds|no longer have anything to do with state governments|are no longer subordinate to state governments": "Under the Babangida government's reforms of 1988–1989 the state ministries of local government, which had supervised and often controlled councils, were abolished, and federal allocations were paid to local governments directly. The aim was to make local government a tier of government in its own right, no longer subordinate to the states: D. The trap: A goes too far: local governments did not become equal to states. C is also too extreme, since states still had some role. Today states again have ministries of local government, though a 2024 Supreme Court ruling requires federal allocations to be paid to councils directly. Memory tip: abolishing the supervisor = freeing the supervised.",
 "|The main legislative body in Nigeria between 1966 and 1975 was the|Supreme Military Council|Armed Forces Ruling Council|Provisional Ruling Council|National Security Council": "From January 1966 until July 1975, under Ironsi and then Gowon, the highest ruling and law-making body was the Supreme Military Council (SMC); it made laws by decree. The SMC continued under Murtala and Obasanjo (to 1979) and under Buhari (1984–1985): A. The trap: the other bodies came later: the Armed Forces Ruling Council (B) under Babangida (1985–1993), the Provisional Ruling Council (C) under Abacha and Abubakar (1993–1999). The National Security Council (D) is an advisory body for security matters. Memory tip: SMC (1966–79, 1984–85) → AFRC (Babangida) → PRC (Abacha, Abubakar).",
 "|Nigeria formally became a federation in|1954|1963|1914|1946": "Nigeria became a federation under the Lyttleton Constitution of 1954, named after the Colonial Secretary Oliver Lyttleton. It divided powers between the central government and the Northern, Eastern and Western Regions, with exclusive and concurrent lists, and made Lagos a Federal Territory: A. The trap: B, 1963, is when Nigeria became a republic and the Mid-West Region was created. C, 1914, was amalgamation into one colony, which was unitary. D, 1946, was the Richards Constitution, which created the regions but kept a unitary government. Memory tip: 1914 amalgamation, 1946 regions, 1954 federation, 1960 independence, 1963 republic.",
 "|The United Nations charter is an instrument that|sets out the rights and obligations of member states|provides for funding of member states|facilitates the resolution of disputes among member states|determines the admission of member states": "The Charter of the United Nations, signed in San Francisco on 26 June 1945, is the founding treaty of the UN. It sets out the purposes and principles of the organisation, establishes its six principal organs, and lays down the rights and obligations of member states, such as sovereign equality, peaceful settlement of disputes and not using force against other states: A. The trap: C, resolving disputes, is one function of the UN under the Charter, not what the Charter as a whole is. D, admission of members, is decided by the General Assembly on the Security Council's recommendation. B is not a Charter function. Memory tip: the Charter is the UN's constitution: rights and duties of members.",
 "|The most active organ of the Economic Community of West African States is the|Tribunal of the Community|Technical and Specialized Commissions|Authority of Heads of State and Government|Council of Ministers": "The Authority of Heads of State and Government is the supreme institution of ECOWAS. It meets regularly, sets the Community's direction, takes all major decisions (on trade, free movement, peacekeeping through ECOMOG, sanctions) and appoints top officials, so it is the most active and powerful organ: C. The trap: D, the Council of Ministers, prepares work for the Authority and advises it, but it does not take the final decisions. The Tribunal (now the Community Court of Justice, A) and the Technical and Specialized Commissions (B, misprinted on the paper) have narrower roles. Memory tip: in ECOWAS, the heads hold the power: the Authority decides.",
 "|The group of states that conceived the idea of the United Nations Organization included|the USSR, Italy, the UK and China|Italy, the UK, France and Japan|the USA, the USSR, the UK and China|France, the USA, Canada and Japan": "The UN was planned by the main Allied powers of the Second World War. The USA, the USSR, the UK and China signed the Moscow Declaration (1943), calling for a general international organisation, and drafted its plan at Dumbarton Oaks, Washington (1944). The Yalta Conference (1945) settled the veto, and the San Francisco Conference adopted the Charter in June 1945: C. The trap: options A, B and D include Italy or Japan, which were Axis powers, enemies of the Allies, and could not have conceived the UN. France joined the Big Four later as a permanent member. Memory tip: the founders were the Big Four Allies: USA, USSR, UK, China.",
 "|The impact of the Commonwealth of Nations is felt most in the area of|diplomatic cooperation|economic cooperation|cultural cooperation|military cooperation": "The Commonwealth is a voluntary association of Britain and its former colonies. It has no military alliance and only limited diplomatic weight, and its members' cultures differ widely. Its practical value to members has been mainly economic: trade preferences (the old Commonwealth preference system), aid, and technical assistance through the Commonwealth Fund for Technical Co-operation: B. The trap: C, cultural cooperation, is tempting because of the Commonwealth Games, the English language and scholarships, but these are by-products; the Commonwealth's main funds and programmes are economic. D is wrong: there is no Commonwealth army. Memory tip: Commonwealth = common WEALTH, so economics first.",
 "|The greatest criticism of the Security Council of the UNO is that it|has no standing army|is not representative enough|undermines the General Assembly|has exclusive veto power": "The UN Security Council has 15 members, five of them permanent: the USA, the UK, France, Russia (formerly the USSR) and China. Only these five hold the veto, so any one of them can block a decision even if all others agree. This has often paralysed the Council and is its most criticised feature: D. This is still true today. The trap: B, not representative enough, is also a strong criticism (no permanent seat for Africa or Latin America), but much of that complaint concerns who holds the veto. A, no standing army, is a weakness of the UN, not a criticism of the Council itself. Memory tip: five vetoes, one \"no\" stops all.",
 "|Which of the following is a founding member of OPEC?|Nigeria|Indonesia|Venezuela|Algeria": "OPEC (Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries) was founded at the Baghdad Conference in September 1960 by five countries: Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela: C. Venezuela's oil minister Juan Pablo Pérez Alfonzo was one of the founding fathers. The trap: the others joined later: Indonesia (B) in 1962, Algeria (D) in 1969 and Nigeria (A) in 1971. Indonesia has since left the organisation. Memory tip: OPEC's founding five: \"I I K S V\" (Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, Venezuela), the only non-Middle-East founder being Venezuela.",
 "|Professor Ibrahim Gambari is the Special Assistant to the United Nations Secretary-General on|African affairs|political and social matters|the Economic Commission of Africa|security matters": "Professor Ibrahim Agboola Gambari, a Nigerian scholar and diplomat, was Nigeria's Foreign Minister (1984–1985) and Permanent Representative to the UN (1990–1999). When this paper was set he was UN Under-Secretary-General and Special Adviser on Africa to Secretary-General Kofi Annan: A. The trap: C is wrong because the Economic Commission for Africa is a separate UN body with its own Executive Secretary. Since then Gambari has held other posts: Under-Secretary-General for Political Affairs (2005–2007), head of the UN–AU mission in Darfur, and Chief of Staff to President Buhari (2020–2023). Memory tip: Gambari = Nigeria's man for Africa at the UN.",
 "|Who among the following served as Secretary-General of OPEC?|Jibril Aminu|Aret Adams|Dan Etete|Rilwanu Lukman": "Chief Rilwanu Lukman, a mining engineer and Nigeria's petroleum minister in several governments, served as Secretary-General of OPEC from 1995 to 2000, after earlier serving as President of the OPEC Conference: D. Later, another Nigerian, Mohammed Sanusi Barkindo, was OPEC Secretary-General from 2016 to 2022. The trap: Jibril Aminu (A, petroleum minister 1989–1992), Dan Etete (C, petroleum minister 1995–1998) and Aret Adams (B, an NNPC chief executive) held top Nigerian oil posts but were never OPEC Secretary-General. Memory tip: Lukman led OPEC's secretariat in Vienna (1995–2000); Barkindo followed later.",
 "|Nigeria's active role in the liberation of some countries in Southern Africa earned her|Chairmanship of the Eminent Persons|Membership of SADC|Giant of Africa|the status of the frontline state": "The frontline states were the African countries bordering apartheid South Africa and white-ruled Rhodesia (Angola, Botswana, Mozambique, Tanzania, Zambia, later Zimbabwe), which supported the liberation movements. Nigeria was far away, but it gave so much money, diplomatic pressure and support to liberation in Angola, Zimbabwe, Namibia and South Africa (for example, nationalising BP in 1979) that it was called an honorary frontline state: D. The trap: C, \"Giant of Africa\", is a nickname from Nigeria's size and population, not from its liberation role. Nigeria is not in SADC (B). Memory tip: far from the front, but a frontline state in spirit.",
 "|Which of these international finance agencies is Nigeria a member of?|London Club|The Infrastructural Development Fund|The Paris Club|The International Monetary Fund": "The International Monetary Fund (IMF), set up at Bretton Woods in 1944, lends to member countries with balance-of-payments problems and advises on economic policy. Nigeria joined in 1961, soon after independence, and is still a member: D. The trap: the London Club (A) and the Paris Club (C) are groups of creditors (commercial banks and creditor governments) to which Nigeria owed debts; Nigeria negotiated with them as a debtor, but was never a member. Nigeria paid off its Paris Club debt in 2006. Memory tip: Nigeria is IN the IMF but only OWED the Clubs.",
 "|Nigeria's membership of the ECOWAS is informed by her desire to|develop a market in the sub-region|form sub-regional high command|become a sub-regional power|promote economic integration": "ECOWAS was created by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975, with Nigeria (under Gowon) and Togo leading the effort. Its main aim is economic integration of West Africa: free movement of goods, people and services, a common external tariff and eventually a single market and currency. Nigeria joined to promote that integration: D. The trap: A, developing a market, is part of integration but narrower, and B and C are not the purposes of ECOWAS; Nigeria's foreign policy stresses cooperation, not domination. Note that in January 2025 Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger left ECOWAS. Memory tip: ECOWAS = Economic Community: economics first.",
 "|An attribute that Nigeria shares with most nonaligned countries is|her large population|the state of her economy|her heterogeneous population|her large size": "The Non-Aligned Movement (founded in Belgrade, 1961) brought together states that refused to join either Cold War bloc. Most of its members, like Nigeria, were newly independent, developing countries whose economies depended on exporting primary products and which needed aid and investment. That shared state of the economy is what Nigeria has in common with most of them: B. The trap: A and D, large population and large size, set Nigeria apart from most non-aligned states, many of which are small. C, a heterogeneous population, is common but not the defining shared attribute. Memory tip: non-aligned = developing, the \"Third World\".",
 "|One of the duties of the legislature is to|exercise oversight|implement laws|promulgate decrees|adjudicate disputes": "The legislature has three big jobs: making laws, controlling public money and overseeing the executive. Oversight means watching how the executive carries out laws and spends money, through committee hearings, questions to ministers, investigations, and approval of appointments and budgets. So one duty of the legislature is to exercise oversight: A. The trap: implementing laws (B) is the executive's job, adjudicating disputes (D) is the judiciary's, and promulgating decrees (C) is what military governments do in place of a legislature. Memory tip: legislature MAKES and WATCHES, executive DOES, judiciary JUDGES.",
 "|The best form of government for a heterogeneous society is a|quasi-federal system|confederal system|unitary system|federal system": "A heterogeneous society contains many ethnic, language and religious groups. Federalism divides power between a central government and regional (state) governments under a written constitution, so each group can manage its own local affairs while all share common services such as defence and currency. That is why Nigeria, India and Switzerland are federations: D. The trap: a confederal system (B) also allows diversity, but its centre is so weak that it often breaks up; a unitary system (C) concentrates power at the centre and can make minorities fear domination. Memory tip: many peoples, one country = federalism (\"unity in diversity\").",
 "|Removal by impeachment applies to the position of a|chief judge|president|Prime Minister|cabinet minister": "Impeachment is the process by which the legislature charges and removes an executive president (and in Nigeria, the vice-president, governors and deputy governors) for gross misconduct. In Nigeria's 1999 Constitution it is in section 143 for the president: a notice signed by a third of the members, investigation by a panel, and a two-thirds vote of each house: B. The trap: a Prime Minister (C) and his cabinet ministers (D) are removed by a vote of no confidence in parliament; a chief judge (A) is removed by the legislature's address or by the National Judicial Council, not by impeachment. Memory tip: President = Impeachment; Prime Minister = No confidence.",
 "|Government by the wealthy is known as|oligarchy|aristocracy|plutocracy|democracy": "Plutocracy comes from the Greek \"ploutos\", meaning wealth: it is government by the rich, where money buys power and policy serves the wealthy: C. The trap: oligarchy (A) is rule by a small group, which is often rich but is defined by its small size, not its wealth. Aristocracy (B) is rule by a noble or \"best\" class, usually by birth. Democracy (D) is rule by the people. Memory tip: PLUTO-cracy = rule by PLUTO, the Greek god of riches.",
 "|The independence of the judiciary can be undermined through the|payment of the salaries of judicial officers by government|appointment of the minister of justice as the Attorney-General|politicization of the appointments of judges|confirmation of the appointment of judges by the legislature": "Judicial independence means judges decide cases without fear or favour, free from pressure by the executive, the legislature or parties. It is protected by appointment on merit, security of tenure and salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund. When appointments are politicized, that is, judges are picked for party loyalty, they may feel indebted to politicians and the courts lose impartiality: C. The trap: A sounds dangerous, but government must pay judges; what protects them is that their pay is fixed by law and cannot be cut. D (legislative confirmation) is actually a safeguard against bad appointments. Memory tip: merit appointment, secure tenure, guaranteed pay = independent judges.",
 "|The unrestrained power of the state over its citizens is underlined by|self-determination|patriotism|sovereignty|nationalism": "Sovereignty is the supreme, absolute and final legal power of the state over all persons and things within its territory, and its independence from outside control. Jean Bodin (1576) called it absolute and indivisible, and John Austin described the sovereign as the one habitually obeyed. This unlimited power over citizens is exactly what sovereignty expresses: C. The trap: self-determination (A) is the right of a people to choose their own government; nationalism (D) and patriotism (B) are feelings of loyalty to the nation, not the state's legal power. Memory tip: Sovereignty = Supreme power inside, independence outside.",
 "|In a parliamentary system, the functions of the head of state and the head of government are vested in|the ministerial council|an individual|the inner cabinet|two different individuals": "A parliamentary system has a dual executive. The head of state is ceremonial: a monarch (as in Britain) or a figurehead president or Governor-General (as in Nigeria's First Republic, Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe). The head of government holds real power: the Prime Minister (Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa). So the two roles are in two different individuals: D. The trap: B, \"an individual\", is the presidential system, where one person (for example, Nigeria's executive president since 1979) is both head of state and head of government. Memory tip: parliamentary = two heads (ceremonial + real); presidential = one head does both.",
 "|A collegial executive is a government in which power is vested in a|committee|monarch|parliament|president": "A collegial executive is a plural executive: power is held jointly by a committee or council whose members are equals, and decisions are taken collectively. The best example is Switzerland's Federal Council of seven members, who rotate the largely ceremonial presidency yearly: A. The trap: a parliament (C) is a legislature, not an executive; a monarch (B) or president (D) is a single-person executive. Memory tip: collegial = colleagues = a committee running the country together (think of the Swiss Seven).",
 "|A common feature of government is|the making of public policy|the separation of powers|the independence of the judiciary|a written constitution": "Government is the machinery through which the state's will is formulated and carried out, so every government, democratic or military, federal or unitary, makes and implements public policy: A. The trap: separation of powers (B), judicial independence (C) and a written constitution (D) are features of particular systems only. Britain has no single written constitution, parliamentary systems fuse powers, and military regimes suspend judicial independence. Memory tip: a \"common feature\" must be true of ALL governments, so choose the function, not a structure.",
 "|A meeting of the legislature is usually brought to an end with|a prorogation|a dissolution|suspension|an adjournment": "Learn the three ways a legislature stops meeting. Adjournment ends a single sitting (or a short break) and the house resumes on a set date. Prorogation ends a whole session, cancelling unfinished business until the next session. Dissolution ends the life of the legislature, leading to fresh elections. A meeting is usually ended by adjournment: D. The trap: prorogation (A) sounds formal, but it closes a session of many meetings, not one meeting. Memory tip: Adjourn = a day; Prorogue = a session; Dissolve = the whole parliament.",
 "|The civil service embraces all workers in|public and private companies|all private corporations|public corporations|government ministries": "The civil service is the body of permanent, salaried officials who advise ministers and carry out government policy in the ministries and departments. Civil servants are non-partisan, anonymous and keep their jobs when governments change. So it embraces the workers in government ministries: D. The paper printed \"government ministers\", which is a printing slip: ministers are political heads appointed by the president and leave with the government. The trap: public corporations (C) such as NNPC have their own staff, who are public servants but not civil servants, and private companies (A, B) are outside government. Memory tip: civil service = ministRIES (staff), not ministERS (politicians).",
 "|The citizenship of a country could be acquired through|registration and arbitration|presidential proclamation|birth and naturalization|parliamentary legislation": "Citizenship is full legal membership of a state. Under Nigeria's constitution (sections 25–27) it is acquired by birth, by registration (for example, a foreign woman married to a Nigerian) and by naturalization (for foreigners who have lived in Nigeria for the required period, 15 years). Option C names two genuine ways: C. The trap: A starts well with registration but adds \"arbitration\", which is a way of settling disputes, not of gaining citizenship. Presidential proclamations (B) and ordinary legislation (D) do not confer citizenship on individuals. Memory tip: B-R-N: Birth, Registration, Naturalization.",
 "|What distinguishes a political party from other social institutions is the desire to|promote the interest of party members|influence the international community on local issues|influence government policies in certain directions|win elections and form a government": "A political party is an organised group of people with similar views who seek to win elections and control the government, to put their programme into action. That aim, capturing power through elections, is what sets parties apart from all other groups: D. The trap: C, influencing government policy, is the aim of pressure groups, which do not want to form a government themselves. Promoting members' interests (A) is true of unions and clubs too. Memory tip: parties want to BE the government; pressure groups want to PUSH the government.",
 "|Capitalism is a system of economic organization based on|very fair distribution of the means of production|a mixed economy that takes all interest into consideration|trading among people who own and control their items of trade|individual ownership of the means of production": "Capitalism (laissez-faire), explained by Adam Smith in \"The Wealth of Nations\" (1776), is an economic system in which land, factories and capital are owned by private individuals and firms, who produce for profit in a free market with little state control: D. The trap: B describes a mixed economy (like Nigeria's), where public and private ownership exist side by side; A is the ideal claimed by socialism. C describes trade only, not who owns the means of production. Memory tip: capitalism = Private ownership + Profit + free market.",
 "|Socialism is a mode of production based on|national ownership of the means of production|mixed ownership of the means of production|state ownership of the means of production|collective ownership of the means of production": "Socialism places the main means of production (land, factories, mines, banks) under social control, which in practice means the state owns and runs them for the benefit of everyone, with central planning and fairer distribution: C. This one is debatable: many textbooks define socialism as \"collective or state ownership\", so D (collective ownership) is also accepted. The trap: B, mixed ownership, describes a mixed economy, not socialism. Note also that Marx saw socialism as the transition stage before communism, a classless, stateless society. Memory tip: socialism = society (through the state) owns; capitalism = individuals own.",
 "|Constitutional disputes in states with written constitutions are resolved by the|legislature|ombudsman|electorate|judiciary": "In a state with a written constitution, the constitution is supreme, and someone neutral must say what it means when governments or citizens disagree. That is the judiciary: through judicial review the courts interpret the constitution and settle disputes, for example between the federal government and the states (in Nigeria, the Supreme Court has original jurisdiction in such cases): D. The trap: the legislature (A) makes and amends laws but cannot be judge in its own cause; an ombudsman (B) handles complaints of maladministration only; the electorate (C) votes but does not decide legal disputes. Memory tip: constitutional quarrel? Go to court.",
 "|Election as a political process is significant because it|facilitates constitutional change of government|is associated with campaigns for public office|facilitates the recall process|enables citizens to vote": "Elections give the people a peaceful, lawful way to choose their leaders and to replace a government they no longer want, at regular intervals. This orderly, constitutional change of government is their main significance: A. The trap: D, \"enables citizens to vote\", only restates what an election is; it does not say why it matters. Campaigns (B) are part of an election, not its purpose, and recall (C) is a separate device for removing an elected representative. Memory tip: ballots replace bullets: elections change governments by votes, not by force.",
 "|Public opinion becomes politically relevant when it|aggregates views and interests|is in support of government|influences the decision of government|criticizes people in power": "Public opinion is the collective views of a significant part of the people on public issues, expressed through the media, polls, protests and votes. It becomes politically relevant only when it influences government decisions, for example when public outcry leads to a policy reversal: C. The trap: A, \"aggregates views and interests\", describes what parties and pressure groups do; it says nothing about political effect. Opinion need not support (B) or criticize (D) government to matter. Memory tip: public opinion matters when it MOVES government.",
 "|The operation of the rule of law is undermined by the|conspiracy by the two houses of the legislature to impeach the president|inability of the press to discharge its responsibilities|unfriendly attitude of pressure groups|existence of administrative tribunals and special immunities": "A.V. Dicey's rule of law means the supremacy of the law, equality before the law, and rights protected by ordinary courts. Administrative tribunals let officials decide some cases outside the ordinary courts, and special immunities (for the president, governors, diplomats or legislators in debate) put some people beyond the reach of ordinary law. Both break equality before the law, so they undermine the rule of law: D. The trap: impeachment (A) is itself a lawful constitutional process, not a breach of the rule of law. Weak press (B) or hostile pressure groups (C) are political problems, not limits on the law itself. Memory tip: immunity = some people above the law = rule of law limited.",
 "|Associations whose main interest is to influence public policies without having to capture power are|political parties|communal groups|pressure groups|trade unions": "Pressure groups (interest groups) are organised groups that try to influence government policy in favour of their members or cause, by lobbying, petitions, strikes or protests, but do not field candidates to capture power. Examples include the NLC, NBA, NMA and ASUU: C. The trap: political parties (A) do want to capture power. Trade unions (D) are one type of pressure group, so C is the general and complete answer. Communal groups (B) are based on community ties. Memory tip: pressure groups seek INFLUENCE; parties seek POWER.",
 "|One of the legacies of pre-colonial Nigeria destroyed by the British was the|peace and harmony in the land|nation's farmland|education of the local people|indigenous cultures of the people": "Colonial rule imposed Western religion, education, language, law and dress, and often looked down on African ways. Over time many indigenous cultural institutions (traditional religion, festivals, languages, customary values and systems of government) were weakened or destroyed: D. The trap: A, \"peace and harmony\", sounds right, but pre-colonial Nigeria had wars and slave raids too, and colonial rule actually stopped many inter-group wars. C is wrong because the British introduced Western education. Memory tip: colonialism changed the CULTURE: religion, language, values.",
 "|The smooth operation of the civil service in Nigeria is mostly hampered by|debt burden and redundancy|poor infrastructure|inadequate training of personnel|corruption and inefficiency": "The Nigerian civil service has long been criticised for corruption (bribery, ghost workers, embezzlement) and inefficiency (delay, red tape, poor productivity). Successive reforms, such as the Udoji Commission (1974) and the 1988 Civil Service Reform, tried to tackle these: D. The trap: inadequate training (C) and poor infrastructure (B) are real problems, but they are lesser ones that are often blamed on the same root of corruption and poor management. Memory tip: \"C and I\" (Corruption and Inefficiency) cripple the Civil service.",
 "|The type of government operated in Nigeria between October 1st 1979 and December 31st 1983 is called|presidential system of government|collegial system of government|unitary system of government|parliamentary system of government": "1 October 1979 to 31 December 1983 is the Second Republic. The 1979 Constitution, drafted by the Constitution Drafting Committee chaired by Chief F.R.A. Williams, replaced the British-style parliamentary system with an American-style presidential system. Alhaji Shehu Shagari was the Executive President until the Buhari coup of 31 December 1983: A. The trap: D, parliamentary, was the First Republic system (1960–66) under Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa. Nigeria was a federation, not unitary (C). Memory tip: First Republic = Parliamentary (Balewa); Second Republic = Presidential (Shagari).",
 "|In the pre-colonial Igbo society, the maintenance of peace and order was the function of the|assembly of Ezes|age-grades|assembly of title holders|clan elders": "Pre-colonial Igbo society was acephalous (without central kings) and governed by village assemblies, elders (the Ofo holders), title societies (Ozo), the Umuada and age-grades. Age-grades, groups of men born within a few years of each other, acted as the police and labour force: they kept law and order, enforced the decisions of the assembly and did public works: B. The trap: clan elders (D) made decisions and settled disputes, but the enforcement of peace and order fell to the age-grades. Memory tip: Igbo: elders decide, age-grades enforce.",
 "|The expenditure of public funds by the executive in Nigeria is controlled by the|president|ministry of finance|judiciary|legislature": "The power of the purse belongs to the legislature. The executive cannot spend public money unless the legislature has approved it in an appropriation act (the budget) or a supplementary appropriation. The legislature also checks spending afterwards through the Public Accounts Committees and the Auditor-General's reports: D. The trap: the president (A) proposes the budget and the ministry of finance (B) manages it, but neither controls it; both are part of the executive being controlled. Memory tip: no Appropriation Act, no spending: the legislature holds the purse.",
 "|The 1979 Constitution established the|Federal Civil Defence Corps|National Human Rights Commission|Federal Road Safety Commission|National Population Commission": "The 1979 Constitution set up federal executive bodies such as the Federal Electoral Commission (FEDECO), the Federal Civil Service Commission, the National Population Commission and the Code of Conduct Bureau: D. The other options came later: the Federal Road Safety Commission was created in 1988, the National Human Rights Commission in 1995 and the Nigeria Security and Civil Defence Corps was given legal backing by an act in 2003. The trap: the human rights commission sounds like a constitutional body, but the 1979 Constitution protected rights through Chapter IV and the courts. Memory tip: 1979 counted heads: the Population Commission.",
 "|The Eastern and Western regions of Nigeria achieved the status of self-government in|1959|1950|1955|1957": "After the 1957 London Constitutional Conference, the Western and Eastern Regions became self-governing in 1957 (the West under Chief Obafemi Awolowo, the East under Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe), and the Northern Region followed on 15 March 1959. The federation became independent on 1 October 1960: D. The trap: 1959 (A) is the North's date; 1953 is the year of Anthony Enahoro's self-government motion, and many students confuse the two. Memory tip: 1957 West and East, 1959 North, 1960 Nigeria.",
 "|The Arthur Richards Constitution was designed to last for|six years|twelve years|five years|nine years": "The Richards Constitution (proclaimed 1946, in force 1 January 1947, under Governor Sir Arthur Richards) introduced regionalism, with three regions and Regional Houses of Assembly. It was designed to last nine years, with reviews every three years: D. Because of nationalist protests over lack of consultation, it was replaced early by the Macpherson Constitution of 1951. The trap: six years (A) is a tempting guess, but the plan was three periods of three years. Memory tip: Richards = 3 regions × 3 years = 9 years.",
 "|The 1976 reforms have been most beneficial to the|Nigeria Police|civil service|federal government|local government": "The 1976 Local Government Reform under the Murtala/Obasanjo regime introduced a uniform, multi-purpose, single-tier local government system for the whole country, with elected councillors, defined functions and statutory grants from the federal and state governments. It was later entrenched in the 1979 Constitution as the third tier of government. The main beneficiary was therefore local government itself: D. The trap: the federal government (C) and civil service (B) were not the reform's targets. Memory tip: 1976 = birth of modern, uniform local government.",
 "|A problem of Nigerian federalism that was resolved by the Supreme Court between the federal government and the littoral states centred on resource|maximization|control|derivation|generation": "In the resource control case (Attorney-General of the Federation v. Attorney-General of Abia State and 35 others, April 2002), the federal government asked the Supreme Court who owns the oil in Nigeria's offshore waters. The Court held that the seaward boundary of the littoral (coastal) states is the low-water mark, so offshore resources belong to the federation, not the states (the onshore/offshore dichotomy): B. The trap: derivation (C) was the revenue principle behind the quarrel, but the question asks what the dispute was about: control of the resources. The National Assembly later abolished the dichotomy for derivation purposes in 2004. Memory tip: coastal states wanted to CONTROL their oil.",
 "|One of the reasons advanced for the overthrow of the Gowon Regime was its failure to|promote some officers of the armed forces|honour the promise to hand over power|create new states|try politicians in detention": "General Yakubu Gowon had promised in 1970 a nine-point programme ending with a return to civil rule in 1976. On 1 October 1974 he announced that 1976 was \"unrealistic\" and the handover was postponed indefinitely. This broken promise, along with corruption and inefficiency, was cited when Brigadier Murtala Muhammed's coup removed him on 29 July 1975: B. The trap: C is wrong because Gowon created 12 states in 1967, and the demand for more states was not his downfall's main reason. Memory tip: Gowon's broken 1976 promise = Murtala's 1975 coup.",
 "|In the first republic, politics in the Northern region was dominated by the|NPC|NCNC|NEPU|UMBC": "The First Republic was regional politics: each region was dominated by one party. The North by the Northern People's Congress (NPC) under Sir Ahmadu Bello, the Sardauna of Sokoto; the East by the NCNC under Azikiwe and later Michael Okpara; the West by the Action Group under Awolowo: A. The trap: NEPU (C), led by Mallam Aminu Kano, and the UMBC (D), led by Joseph Tarka in the Middle Belt, were northern opposition parties that never controlled the region. Memory tip: N for North, N for NPC.",
 "|The Bureau for Public Enterprises is charged with the responsibility for|eradicating poverty|generating opportunities|providing employment opportunities|privatization and commercialization": "Privatization sells government shares in public enterprises to private investors; commercialization makes public enterprises run like businesses that cover their costs. The Technical Committee on Privatisation and Commercialisation (TCPC, 1988) began the programme, and the Bureau of Public Enterprises (BPE, 1993) took it over; since 1999 the BPE acts as secretariat of the National Council on Privatisation: D. The trap: poverty eradication (A) was the job of NAPEP, and employment schemes (C) belonged to bodies like the NDE. Memory tip: BPE = Bureau for Privatising Enterprises.",
 "|The military head of state during the Third Republic was|General Ibrahim Babangida|General Murtala Muhammed|General Sani Abacha|General Olusegun Obasanjo": "General Ibrahim Babangida (1985–93) ran a long transition programme. Under the 1989 Constitution, elected local councils, state governors and assemblies (1991–92) and a National Assembly (1992) took office while he remained military president: this half-civilian arrangement is called the Third Republic. It collapsed after the 12 June 1993 election was annulled: A. The trap: Sani Abacha (C) seized power in November 1993 and dissolved the Third Republic's institutions; Murtala and Obasanjo (B, D) ruled in 1975–79, before the Second Republic. Memory tip: IBB built the Third Republic; Abacha buried it.",
 "|The most important challenge facing the Fourth Republic is|the need to develop Nigeria|how to develop Nigeria's social institution|how to deal with the military|the successful conduct of the 2003 elections": "The Fourth Republic began on 29 May 1999 with President Olusegun Obasanjo. When this paper was set, the major challenge was the 2003 general elections: no civilian government had ever successfully conducted an election and handed over to a civilian successor (the 1983 elections led to a coup). Conducting the 2003 elections successfully would show democracy was taking root: D. The trap: A (development) is always a challenge, but it is too general to be the special test of that moment. Today the 2003 elections are history, and the Fourth Republic has had several transitions, including the first defeat of a sitting president in 2015. Memory tip: 2003 = the first civilian-to-civilian test.",
 "|Financial allocation to local government by the federal or the state government to supplement the cost of a project is called|revenue allocation|matching grant|statutory allocation|reimbursement": "Grants are money transferred from a higher to a lower government. A matching grant is given on condition that the receiving government also contributes, and it is tied to a specific project, so the higher government \"matches\" or supplements the local government's own spending on that project: B. The trap: statutory allocation (C) is the local governments' regular share of the Federation Account fixed by law, not tied to a project. Revenue allocation (A) is the general sharing of national revenue. Memory tip: MATCHING grant = you pay part, we MATCH it, for one project.",
 "|Nigeria's influence in OPEC is determined by the|sizes of her refineries|accessibility of her oil fields|low sulphur content of her crude|volume of her oil revenue": "OPEC members carry weight in proportion to their oil: production quotas are set by output, and members with large output and revenue have more say. Nigeria, Africa's largest oil producer and an OPEC member since 1971, draws its influence from the volume of oil it produces and earns from: D. The trap: C is true but irrelevant; Nigeria's low-sulphur \"sweet\" crude (Bonny Light) earns a higher price, but quality does not give a vote. Refinery size (A) has nothing to do with OPEC, which deals in crude oil. Memory tip: in OPEC, barrels mean influence.",
 "|The activities of Nigeria in the international community are primarily influenced by|military power|diplomacy|propaganda|national interest": "Foreign policy is the set of goals a country pursues in its dealings with other states, and every country's first goal is its national interest: security, territorial integrity, economic development and prestige. Nigeria's Afrocentric policy, ECOWAS role and peacekeeping are all justified by its national interest: D. The trap: diplomacy (B) and propaganda (C) are tools for achieving foreign policy goals, not what drives them; military power (A) is one capability among many. Memory tip: national interest is the WHY; diplomacy is the HOW.",
 "|The dispute between Nigeria and Cameroon is over|trade|exploration rights|fishing rights|territory": "The Nigeria–Cameroon dispute was a boundary and territorial dispute, centred on the oil-rich Bakassi Peninsula and parts of the Lake Chad area, with clashes in the 1980s and 1990s. Cameroon took the case to the International Court of Justice in 1994; on 10 October 2002 the ICJ awarded Bakassi to Cameroon. Under the Greentree Agreement (2006), Nigeria completed the handover on 14 August 2008: D. The trap: oil exploration (B) and fishing (C) rights explain why the land mattered, but the dispute itself was over who owns the territory. Memory tip: Bakassi = boundary.",
 "|Nigeria's high standing in the UN General Assembly is underscored by her|financial contribution|military strength|successes in UN elective offices|contribution to global peace": "Nigeria's prestige at the UN comes mainly from its contribution to world peace. It has sent troops to UN peacekeeping missions since the Congo in 1960, including Lebanon, Sierra Leone, Liberia, Sudan and many more, and it was a leading voice against colonialism and apartheid: D. The trap: C, successes in UN elective offices, is tempting because Nigeria has served several terms on the Security Council and held the General Assembly presidency (Joseph Garba, 1989; Tijjani Muhammad-Bande, 2019), but those elections reflect the respect Nigeria earned through its peace role. Memory tip: Nigeria's UN badge is the blue helmet.",
 "|Nigeria's Non-Alignment policy is constrained by her|membership of the UNO|Afrocentric posture|membership of ECOWAS|strong ties with Western powers": "Non-alignment means not joining either the Western (US-led) or Eastern (Soviet-led) bloc during the Cold War. Nigeria joined the Non-Aligned Movement, but in practice its colonial ties, trade, aid, education and the short-lived Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact (1960–62) kept it close to Britain and the West, especially under Balewa. These strong Western ties constrained genuine non-alignment: D. The trap: membership of the UN (A) and ECOWAS (C) does not conflict with non-alignment; the Afrocentric posture (B) is actually part of it. Memory tip: you can't be neutral while tied to one side.",
 "|The main constraint on Nigeria–Francophone West African cooperation is|ideological differences|cultural differences|poor road network|economic dependence": "Most of Nigeria's neighbours are French-speaking (Benin, Niger, Chad, Cameroon). They remain closely linked to France through the CFA franc (backed by the French Treasury), aid, trade and defence agreements, and France long encouraged rival groupings such as the CEAO (1973). This economic dependence on France limits how far they cooperate with Nigeria: D. This one is debatable: some books stress ideological or cultural differences, but Nigeria and these states were all broadly pro-Western, so ideology was not the real barrier. The trap: cultural differences (B), mainly language, are real but secondary. Memory tip: the CFA franc ties Francophone hands.",
 "|Nigeria's relations with Britain were at a very low ebb under the|Buhari Regime|Gowon Regime|Shagari Regime|Babangida Regime": "In July 1984, under General Muhammadu Buhari, agents tried to kidnap former Shagari minister Umaru Dikko in London and fly him to Nigeria in a crate. Britain expelled Nigerian diplomats, Nigeria retaliated, and both withdrew their High Commissioners; relations stayed cold for about two years: A. The trap: relations were also bad under Murtala/Obasanjo (BP nationalised in 1979) and later under Abacha (1995 Commonwealth suspension), but neither is an option here; Gowon, Shagari and Babangida kept fairly good ties with Britain. Memory tip: Buhari + Dikko crate = low ebb with London.",
 "|The ECOWAS Treaty was reviewed in 1991 to|accommodate the interest of France|mobilize ECOMOG|accommodate extra sub-regional interest|make it responsive to new challenges": "ECOWAS was founded by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975. By the early 1990s the world had changed: the Cold War had ended, the Liberian civil war needed a regional response, and economic integration was lagging. At the 1991 Abuja summit, ECOWAS set up a committee of eminent persons, chaired by Yakubu Gowon, to review the treaty, and the Revised Treaty was signed at Cotonou in July 1993 to make ECOWAS responsive to these new challenges: D. The trap: B, mobilizing ECOMOG, is tempting, but ECOMOG was deployed in Liberia in 1990, before the review. Memory tip: Lagos 1975 founded; review 1991, signed Cotonou 1993.",
 "|The headquarters of the International Court of Justice is in|Paris|The Hague|London|Washington DC": "The International Court of Justice (ICJ) is one of the six principal organs of the United Nations. It sits at the Peace Palace in The Hague, Netherlands, and has 15 judges elected for nine-year terms. It settles legal disputes between states, for example Nigeria v. Cameroon over Bakassi (2002): B. The trap: Paris (A) hosts UNESCO, London (C) the Commonwealth Secretariat and Washington DC (D) the IMF and World Bank. Memory tip: the ICJ is the only principal UN organ not in New York; it is in The Hague.",
 "|The independent African countries that did not sign the OAU Charter on May 25, 1963 were|Morocco and Angola|Togo and Sierra Leone|Chad and the Gambia|Togo and Morocco": "The OAU was founded in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, on 25 May 1963 (now Africa Day) by the 32 then-independent African states. Thirty signed the Charter that day. Togo was not represented because the new government that followed the assassination of President Sylvanus Olympio (January 1963) had not been recognised, and Morocco stayed away over its claim to Mauritania; both signed later in 1963. So the question means the states that did not sign that day, Togo and Morocco: D. The paper left out the word \"not\". The trap: Angola (A) and the Gambia (C) were not yet independent (1975 and 1965). Memory tip: Togo and Morocco came Late (T-M-L).",
 "|The tenure of the President of the UN Security Council is|two years|one year|one month|six months": "The Security Council has 15 members: five permanent (USA, UK, France, Russia, China) with veto power, and ten non-permanent members elected for two years. Its presidency rotates every month among all 15 members in the English alphabetical order of their names: C. The trap: two years (A) is the term of a non-permanent member, not the president's tenure; one year (B) is the term of the President of the General Assembly. Memory tip: Security Council president = one Month; General Assembly president = one year.",
 "|The majority of the OPEC members are from|Asia|Latin America|the Middle East|Africa": "OPEC was founded in Baghdad in 1960 by Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela, and is based in Vienna. When this paper was set (2003) it had 11 members, six of them in the Middle East (Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, UAE), with Algeria, Libya and Nigeria from Africa, Venezuela from Latin America and Indonesia from Asia. So the majority were Middle Eastern: C. Today this has changed: after Qatar, Ecuador and Angola left and Gabon, Equatorial Guinea and Congo joined, Africa has six of the 12 members and the Middle East five. Memory tip: in 2003 OPEC was \"Gulf first\".",
 "|The non-British colony which is a member of the Commonwealth is|Guinea-Bissau|Mozambique|Rwanda|Eritrea": "The Commonwealth is mainly made up of former British territories, but countries with no British colonial past may join. Mozambique, a former Portuguese colony, became the first such member in November 1995, as a reward for its support for the struggle against apartheid in neighbouring South Africa: B. The trap: Guinea-Bissau (A) was also Portuguese, and Eritrea (D) was Italian, then Ethiopian, but neither joined. Today the answer would be broader: Rwanda (Belgian) joined in 2009, and Gabon and Togo (French) in 2022. Memory tip: Mozambique was the first non-British member (1995).",
 "|The major problem of the ECOWAS is lack of|a common Customs Union|a common currency|uniform ideology|commitment by members": "ECOWAS aims at a single West African market, but its members use many currencies: the CFA franc (shared by the francophone states), the naira, the cedi, the leone, the dalasi and others, which are not freely convertible. This makes trade and payments between members difficult and costly. The plan for a single currency, the Eco, has been postponed many times: B. The trap: lack of commitment (D) is also often blamed, and a customs union (A) was slow to come (the Common External Tariff began in 2015), but the missing common currency is the classic textbook answer. Memory tip: one region, many monies = ECOWAS's headache.",
 "|In a democracy, sovereign authority is exercised by the|people|executive|electorate|legislature": "In a democracy, ultimate sovereignty belongs to the people as a whole. But not everyone can use it directly: children, aliens and others cannot vote. The part of the people who actually exercise that sovereign power, by choosing representatives in elections, voting in referendums and removing leaders at the polls, is the electorate, that is, all citizens qualified and registered to vote: C. The trap: A, \"people\", is where sovereignty resides, not who exercises it in practice. B and D, the executive and legislature, exercise power only as agents of the electorate. Memory tip: the people own sovereignty; the electorate uses it at the ballot box.",
 "|The agent through which the state undertakes political socialization is the|school|family|peer group|pressure group": "Political socialisation is the process by which people learn political values, beliefs and attitudes. Many agents do it: family, peers, religion, media, schools. But the state directly controls only some of them. Through schools the state sets the curriculum (civic education, history, government), makes pupils recite the national anthem and pledge, and teaches national symbols and loyalty. So the school is the agent through which the state undertakes socialisation: A. The trap: B, the family, is the first and most powerful agent, but it is private, not an instrument of the state. Peer groups and pressure groups are also outside state control. Memory tip: state socialises through the classroom; parents socialise through the home.",
 "|Unicameral legislature is a common feature of|presidentialism|parliamentarism|unitarism|federalism": "A unicameral legislature has only one chamber. Federations usually need two chambers: one representing the people by population and one representing the federating units equally (as Nigeria's Senate gives each state three senators). A unitary state has no federating units to protect, so a single chamber is common and cheaper; examples include Ghana, Denmark and New Zealand: C. The trap: D, federalism, is the system that most typically produces a bicameral legislature, the opposite of the question. Presidential and parliamentary systems can have one or two chambers, so they are not the defining link. Memory tip: one centre, one chamber; many states, two chambers.",
 "|Independence of the judiciary is pertinent because it accords the judiciary the power to|determine a fixed term of office for the judges|dismiss any judge who has breached the judicial code of conduct|enable the judge to try and decide cases without bias|determine a fixed salary for judges": "Judicial independence means judges are free from control or pressure by the executive, the legislature, parties or private interests. It is secured by security of tenure, salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund, appointment through bodies like the Judicial Service Commission, and immunity for judicial acts. The purpose of all these is that judges can try and decide cases impartially, without fear or favour: C. The trap: A and D, fixed tenure and fixed salary, are means of securing independence, not the reason it matters; and judges do not fix them for themselves. B, dismissing judges, belongs to other bodies (the NJC and executive). Memory tip: independence is the means; fair judgment is the end.",
 "|In a unitary system of government, power is concentrated at the centre|without devolution|with devolution|with residual functions|without residual functions": "In a unitary state all governmental power is held by the central government. Any local or regional units are created by the centre, exercise only the powers it gives them, and can be abolished by it. Because there is no constitutional sharing of powers, the residual functions (anything not listed elsewhere) also stay with the centre. So power is concentrated at the centre with residual functions: C. The trap: A, \"without devolution\", sounds right, but many unitary states do devolve power, as Britain does to Scotland and Wales; devolved power can simply be taken back. Memory tip: in a unitary state the centre keeps everything, even the leftovers.",
 "|In a confederation, the constituency that a member of legislature represents is a|senatorial district|parliamentary constituency|nation-state|region": "A confederation is a loose union of sovereign states that keep their independence and give a weak central body limited tasks, such as defence or trade. Examples are the USA under the Articles of Confederation (1781–1789) and the Senegambia Confederation (1982–1989). Members of the confederal assembly are delegates sent by each member state and answer to it, so the constituency each represents is a whole nation-state: C. The trap: D, \"region\", is the word for a federating unit, as in Nigeria's Northern, Eastern and Western Regions; regions are not sovereign. A and B are electoral districts within one country. Memory tip: confederation = club of sovereign states; each delegate speaks for a state.",
 "|In a constitutional monarchy, the authority to remove the head of state is exercised by the|legislature|head of government|cabinet|prime minister": "In a constitutional monarchy, such as Britain, the monarch is head of state but reigns under law made by parliament. Since the Glorious Revolution of 1688 and the Act of Settlement 1701, parliament decides who may hold the throne. In 1936 Parliament passed His Majesty's Declaration of Abdication Act to give effect to Edward VIII's abdication. So the authority to remove the head of state lies with the legislature: A. The trap: D, the prime minister, is powerful but is the monarch's own appointee and cannot remove the monarch by himself; B and C are the same executive. Memory tip: parliament made the crown's rules, so parliament can take the crown.",
 "|In a parliamentary system of government, a vote of no confidence leads to the resignation of|the entire cabinet|an individual minister|the entire parliament|the prime minister": "In a parliamentary system the cabinet stays in office only while it keeps the confidence of the legislature. Ministers are collectively responsible: they stand or fall together. When parliament passes a vote of no confidence in the government, the prime minister and the entire cabinet must resign, or the prime minister asks for parliament to be dissolved and an election held: A. The trap: D, the prime minister alone, is wrong because collective responsibility brings down the whole team, not just its leader. B fits a censure of one minister (individual responsibility), and C confuses dissolution with resignation. Memory tip: one boat, one crew: no confidence sinks the whole cabinet.",
 "|In a presidential system of government, the president checks the legislature through|executive order|executive review|exercise of veto|legislative order": "Checks and balances let each organ of government limit the others. In a presidential system such as the USA or Nigeria, a bill passed by the legislature needs the president's assent. The president can withhold assent, which is a veto, sending the bill back; in Nigeria the National Assembly can override it by a two-thirds majority in each house. So the president checks the legislature through the exercise of veto: C. The trap: A, executive orders, are directives to the executive branch, not a check on the legislature. B, review of laws, is the courts' power of judicial review. Memory tip: Veto = Very powerful \"no\" to the legislature.",
 "|The economic basis of feudalism is|capital|agriculture|slavery|trade": "Feudalism was the system of medieval Europe (and similar systems elsewhere). The king granted land, called fiefs, to lords; lords granted it to vassals and knights in return for military service; serfs worked the land and gave part of their crops and labour. Land and farming were the basis of wealth and power: B. The trap: A, capital, is the basis of capitalism, the system that replaced feudalism. Trade existed but was not the basis, and slavery was the basis of the earlier slave economies (as Marx's stages show: slavery, feudalism, capitalism). Memory tip: feudal = fief = farmland.",
 "|The creation of a classless society is the ultimate aim of|communism|capitalism|socialism|fascism": "Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels taught that history is a story of class struggle. Capitalism would be overthrown by the workers; socialism would follow as a transition stage; and finally communism would arrive, a society with no classes, no private ownership of the means of production, and no state, because the state would \"wither away\": A. The trap: C, socialism, also attacks class inequality, but in Marxist theory it is only the stage on the way, still with a state and some differences in reward. Capitalism accepts classes, and fascism glorifies the state. Memory tip: communism is the communal end of the road; socialism is the road.",
 "|One of the sources of a constitution is|constitutional law|common law|corporate law|statutory law": "Sources of a constitution are where its rules come from: historical documents, conventions, judicial precedents, common law, statutes (acts of parliament), and the works of learned writers. Common law, the body of rules developed by judges over centuries, gives Britain many rules on individual liberty and the royal prerogative: B. Statutory law, option D, is also a source, since acts such as the Bill of Rights 1689 shape the constitution, so both are accepted. The trap: A, constitutional law, is the constitution itself, not a source of it; C, corporate law, governs companies. Memory tip: a constitution comes from Courts (common law), Conventions and Codified statutes.",
 "|The fundamental assumption on which the idea of the rule of law is based is|rationality of human beings|equality of human beings|love for social justice|supremacy of the constitution": "The rule of law, explained by A. V. Dicey, means that the law is supreme: government acts only under law, everyone is equal before the law, and rights are protected by the courts. Its fundamental assumption is the supremacy of the law, which in a country with a written constitution, like Nigeria, means the supremacy of the constitution over every person and organ of government: D. The trap: B, equality of human beings, sounds like Dicey's \"equality before the law\", but that is equality of treatment by the courts, not a claim that all humans are equal; and it follows from the supremacy of law. Memory tip: law on top first; equality comes from it.",
 "|The principle of separation of powers was made popular by|John Locke|Baron de Montesquieu|Thomas Hobbes|Niccolo Machiavelli": "Separation of powers divides government into three organs (legislature, executive and judiciary), each with its own functions and personnel, to prevent tyranny. Although John Locke wrote of separate legislative and executive powers, it was the French writer Charles-Louis de Secondat, Baron de Montesquieu, who popularised the full three-way doctrine in The Spirit of the Laws (1748), drawing on Britain: B. The trap: A, Locke, is famous for the social contract and natural rights (life, liberty, property). Hobbes wrote Leviathan (1651), defending absolute sovereignty, and Machiavelli wrote The Prince (1513). Memory tip: Montesquieu = Many organs, three separate powers.",
 "|Delegated legislation refers to the laws made by|the legislature|military governments|civilian governments|non-legislative bodies": "Delegated (subsidiary) legislation is law made by persons or bodies other than the legislature, under authority given by an act of the legislature. Examples are regulations made by ministers, rules made by agencies like NAFDAC, and bye-laws made by local councils. These are non-legislative bodies acting on delegated power: D. The trap: A is the opposite, since laws made by the legislature itself are primary legislation. B and C describe who is in government, not who makes delegated laws; military decrees are primary law-making by a military regime, not delegation. Memory tip: delegated = done by others on parliament's behalf.",
 "|Laws made by state governments are known as|edicts|acts|decrees|bye-laws": "Laws in Nigeria take names from their maker. During military rule, the Federal Military Government made decrees, state military governors made edicts, and local governments make bye-laws. So the laws made by state governments were known as edicts: A. Today, under civilian rule, state Houses of Assembly pass bills that become \"Laws\" of the state. The trap: C, decrees, are federal military laws; B, acts, are laws passed by the National Assembly; D, bye-laws, are local government rules. Memory tip: Federal Decrees, State Edicts, Local Bye-laws: D-E-B from top to bottom.",
 "|The law of libel limits a citizen's right to freedom of|association|movement|worship|expression": "Libel is a defamatory statement in permanent form (writing, print, broadcast or online) that damages a person's reputation; slander is the spoken form. Section 39 of the 1999 Constitution guarantees freedom of expression and the press, but section 45 allows laws limiting rights to protect the reputation and rights of others. The law of libel is one such limit on freedom of expression: D. The trap: none of the other freedoms is about what you say or publish. Association is about joining groups, movement about travel, and worship about religion. Memory tip: libel = label someone falsely in print; free speech has limits.",
 "|The first franchise in the history of the democratic process is|female franchise|male franchise|universal franchise|property franchise": "Franchise means the right to vote. It did not begin universal. In ancient Athens only free adult males who were citizens could vote, and in Britain and Nigeria the first voters were men only: B. Option D is also accepted, because those early male voters usually also needed property or income: Nigeria's first elections under the 1922 Clifford Constitution were for adult males in Lagos and Calabar earning at least £100 a year. The trap: A, female franchise, came much later (Britain 1918 and 1928; Northern Nigerian women only in 1976), and C, universal adult suffrage, was the last stage. Memory tip: men and money first, women later, everyone last.",
 "|An interest group that admits members and conducts its affairs according to stated rules is described as|institutional|organizational|associational|non-associational": "Gabriel Almond classified interest groups into four types. Associational groups are formally organised, have written rules, officers and open membership, and pursue stated interests: trade unions, the Nigerian Bar Association, the Nigerian Medical Association, MAN: C. The trap: A, institutional groups, exist inside formal institutions such as the army, the civil service or churches. D, non-associational groups, are informal groups based on kinship, ethnicity or religion with no formal rules. Anomic groups are spontaneous, like riots. Memory tip: associational = association with a constitution and membership card.",
 "|A major influence on the formulation of public opinion is|public journals|peer groups|the family|the mass media": "Public opinion is the views held by a large part of the population on public issues. It is shaped by family, schools, religion, peer groups and leaders, but the major influence is the mass media: radio, television, newspapers and now the internet and social media. They reach millions at once, set the agenda of what people discuss and frame how issues are seen: D. The trap: B and C, peer groups and the family, influence individuals strongly but reach only a few people each; A, public journals, are a small, specialist part of the media. Memory tip: mass media shapes mass opinion.",
 "|The highest grade in the civil service is known as the|technical cadre|administrative cadre|executive cadre|clerical cadre": "The civil service is divided into classes or cadres. The administrative cadre is the highest: it includes permanent secretaries and directors, advises ministers, formulates policy and coordinates ministries. Below it are the executive cadre, which carries out policy and manages routine work; the professional and technical cadres (engineers, doctors, technicians); and the clerical cadre, which keeps records and does routine office work: B. The trap: A, the technical cadre, contains highly qualified experts, but they serve within ministries headed by the administrative class. Memory tip: Administrative At the top, then Executive, then Clerical: A-E-C.",
 "|The pre-colonial Yoruba political system as a whole can best be described as a|confederation of monarchies and chiefdoms|federation of chiefdoms and localities|highly centralized kingdom|confederation of chiefdoms and localities": "Pre-colonial Yorubaland was not one state but many kingdoms: Oyo, Ife, Ijebu, Egba, Ekiti, Ijesa, Ondo and others. Each had its own oba and chiefs, and they were loosely linked by common descent from Oduduwa and respect for the Ooni of Ife as spiritual head. This makes the Yoruba system as a whole a confederation of monarchies and chiefdoms: A. The trap: C, \"highly centralized kingdom\", fits the Hausa-Fulani emirates better; even the Oyo Empire's Alaafin was checked by the Oyo Mesi and Ogboni. D leaves out the monarchies, the heart of the system. Memory tip: Yoruba = many crowns under one ancestor.",
 "|The major motivation of British colonization of Nigeria was to|spread religion|satisfy British economic interests|westernize Nigerians|protect Nigeria from external attack": "Britain's colonisation of Nigeria was driven above all by economic interests. The Industrial Revolution created demand for raw materials such as palm oil, cocoa, groundnuts, cotton and tin, and for markets for British manufactured goods. Companies like the Royal Niger Company led the way, and the colonial state built railways to export produce: B. The trap: A and C, spreading Christianity and \"civilising\" Nigerians, were real activities of missionaries and were used to justify colonial rule, but they were not the main motive of the British state. D is false: Britain itself was the external power. Memory tip: colonialism followed the money: Trade first, Flag later.",
 "|The Native Authority system was most effective and successful in|Western Nigeria|Mid-Western Nigeria|Northern Nigeria|Eastern Nigeria": "Lord Lugard introduced indirect rule, ruling through traditional rulers organised as Native Authorities. It succeeded best in Northern Nigeria because the emirates already had centralised authority, strong emirs, an organised tax system and Islamic courts. In the West it worked less well because obas were checked by chiefs, and in the East it failed because there were no chiefs, leading to warrant chiefs and the Aba Women's Riot of 1929: C. The trap: A, Western Nigeria, had kings, so it is tempting, but their power was limited. Memory tip: indirect rule needs a ready-made ruler: the North had emirs.",
 "|The process of nationalism was accelerated by|rapid economic development|the coming of Christian missionaries|the signing of the Atlantic Charter|improvement in warfare tactics": "The Atlantic Charter was signed by Winston Churchill and Franklin Roosevelt in August 1941. It declared the right of all peoples to choose their own form of government. When Churchill said it applied only to Nazi-occupied Europe, African nationalists such as Nnamdi Azikiwe (who wrote \"The Atlantic Charter and British West Africa\" in 1943) used it to demand self-government, accelerating nationalism: C. The trap: B, Christian missionaries, helped indirectly by spreading Western education, which produced the educated elite; but the question asks what accelerated the process. A is wrong because colonial economies remained underdeveloped. Memory tip: the Atlantic Charter crossed the Atlantic to Africa.",
 "|A common feature of the earlier political parties in Nigeria was that they|started as socio-cultural organizations|were formed by the government|were non-elitist in nature|were backed by the colonialists": "Most of Nigeria's early major parties grew out of cultural and ethnic associations. The Action Group (1951) came from Egbe Omo Oduduwa, a Yoruba cultural society; the Northern People's Congress grew from Jam'iyyar Mutanen Arewa; and the NCNC was closely tied to the Igbo State Union. This gave them regional and ethnic bases: A. The trap: D, \"backed by the colonialists\", is wrong since these parties fought for self-government. B is wrong because they were formed by private citizens, and C is wrong because they were led by the educated elite. Memory tip: culture first, party later: Egbe became AG, Jam'iyya became NPC.",
 "|Under the 1999 Constitution, the power to declare war is vested in the|legislature|executive|National Council of States|National Security Council": "Under section 5(4) of the 1999 Constitution, the President, as the head of the executive and Commander-in-Chief, has the power to declare a state of war between Nigeria and another country, but only with the sanction of a resolution of both Houses of the National Assembly sitting in joint session. So the power is vested in the executive, checked by the legislature: B. The trap: A, the legislature, gives the approval but does not itself declare war. C, the National Council of States, and D, the National Security Council, are advisory bodies. Memory tip: the President declares; the Assembly approves.",
 "|A distinguishing feature of the 1979 Constitution was the|departure from the parliamentary to the presidential system|preservation and entrenchment of republicanism|introduction of unicameralism into Nigeria|introduction of a federal structure": "The 1979 Constitution, drafted by the Constitution Drafting Committee under Chief Rotimi Williams (1975–76), began the Second Republic. Its main innovation was replacing the Westminster parliamentary system of 1960–66 with an American-style executive presidency, with separation of powers and a President who is both head of state and head of government: A. The trap: B, republicanism, was introduced in 1963, not 1979. D is wrong because Nigeria became federal in 1954 (Lyttelton Constitution). C is wrong since the National Assembly had two chambers. Memory tip: 1979 = presidency; 1963 = republic; 1954 = federation.",
 "|The two chambers of elected national representatives in Nigeria are called|the parliament|the senate|House of Assembly|the National Assembly": "Nigeria's federal legislature is the National Assembly, established by section 47 of the 1999 Constitution. It has two chambers: the Senate, with 109 senators (three from each of the 36 states and one from the FCT), and the House of Representatives, with 360 members. Together the two chambers are called the National Assembly: D. The trap: A, \"parliament\", was the name under the 1960 and 1963 parliamentary system; B, the Senate, is only one of the two chambers. C, House of Assembly, is the state legislature. Memory tip: National Assembly = Senate + House of Reps.",
 "|Judicial administration in respect of the national code of conduct lies with the|Judicial Service Commission|Code of Conduct Tribunal|Public Complaints Commission|Code of Conduct Bureau": "The Code of Conduct for public officers (Fifth Schedule of the 1999 Constitution) requires them to declare their assets and forbids things such as foreign bank accounts. Two bodies enforce it. The Code of Conduct Bureau receives and keeps asset declarations, investigates complaints and refers breaches. The Code of Conduct Tribunal is a court-like body that tries cases and imposes penalties such as removal from office or disqualification. Judicial administration therefore lies with the Tribunal: B. The trap: D, the Bureau, is the administrative and investigating body, not a judicial one. A, the JSC, deals with judges' appointments. Memory tip: Bureau = books (declarations); Tribunal = trial.",
 "|In the Second Republic, the ruling National Party of Nigeria formed an alliance with the|Unity Party of Nigeria|Nigeria People's Party|Great Nigeria People's Party|Nigeria Advance Party": "In the 1979 elections, Shehu Shagari's National Party of Nigeria won the presidency but lacked a majority in the National Assembly. It formed an accord with Nnamdi Azikiwe's Nigeria People's Party (NPP) to secure a working majority, and NPP members got ministerial posts. The accord collapsed in 1981: B. The trap: A, the UPN of Obafemi Awolowo, was the main opposition party. C, the GNPP of Waziri Ibrahim, was also in opposition, and D, the Nigeria Advance Party of Tunji Braithwaite, was registered only in 1982. Memory tip: NPN + NPP = the \"P-P\" accord of 1979.",
 "|The relationship between the tiers of government in Nigeria can be described as one of|independent co-existence|coordinate and independent jurisdiction|voluntary subordination|superior-subordinate co-existence": "Nigeria has three tiers of government: federal, state and local. In practice the federal government dominates (it controls most revenue and the police), and local governments are placed under the states, which create them, fund them and often dissolve their councils. So the relationship is superior-subordinate co-existence: D. Option B is also accepted: in theory, and in the constitution's division of powers between exclusive and concurrent lists, the federal and state governments are coordinate and independent, which is K. C. Wheare's classic definition of federalism. The trap: C, \"voluntary\" subordination, is wrong because subordination is imposed, not chosen. Memory tip: in theory side by side; in practice top-down.",
 "|The 1976 Local Government Reforms in Nigeria transformed the relationship between states and local government into one of|master and servant|partnership and cooperation|equality|subordination": "The 1976 Local Government Reform under the Murtala/Obasanjo regime made local government uniform across Nigeria and recognised it as the third tier of government. Councils got defined functions, elected councillors, a statutory share of federal revenue and protection in the 1979 Constitution. The aim was to change the relationship between states and local governments from master and servant to partnership and cooperation: B. The trap: A, master and servant, describes what existed before 1976, when states controlled councils at will. C is too strong, since states still oversee local governments. Memory tip: 1976 made local government a partner, not a servant.",
 "|Government-owned companies operating in the economic sector are referred to as|public utilities|public enterprises|public investments|public services": "Public enterprises are businesses owned wholly or mainly by government that produce goods and services, often for sale. They include public corporations such as NNPC, the old NEPA and the Nigerian Railway Corporation, and government-owned companies. Since the question speaks of companies operating in the economic sector, the general name is public enterprises: B. The trap: A, public utilities, are one kind of public enterprise providing essential services like water and electricity, so the term is too narrow. C is not a technical term, and D, public services, refers to the civil service. Memory tip: enterprise = business; public enterprise = government business.",
 "|The difference between commercialized and privatized companies is that in the former|private ownership is dominant|public ownership is dominant|government subsidizes costs|profit motive is recessive": "Commercialisation and privatisation are both reforms of public enterprises, pursued in Nigeria from 1988 (Technical Committee on Privatisation and Commercialisation) and later by the Bureau of Public Enterprises. A commercialised enterprise stays owned by government but is run on business lines, charging economic prices and making profits without subsidies. A privatised enterprise has its shares sold to private investors. So in a commercialised company public ownership is dominant: B. The trap: A describes the privatised company, the latter not the former. C and D are wrong because commercialisation ends subsidies and makes profit important. Memory tip: commercialised = government-owned but business-run; privatised = privately owned.",
 "|The body responsible for running the personnel affairs of senior local government staff in Nigeria is the|Local Government Council|State Civil Service Commission|Senior Staff Commission|Local Government Service Commission": "Each state has a Local Government Service Commission that appoints, posts, promotes and disciplines senior staff of local governments, such as those on higher grade levels and heads of departments. It was introduced with the 1976 reform to give local government a professional, unified career service. Junior staff are handled by the councils themselves: D. The trap: B, the State Civil Service Commission, handles state civil servants, not council staff. A, the Local Government Council, deals with junior staff. C is not a real body. Memory tip: local government staff = Local Government Service Commission: the name tells you.",
 "|The immediate cause of the January 15, 1966 military coup in Nigeria was the|Kano Riots|Tiv Riots|election crisis in the Western Region|crisis over the population census": "The first military coup in Nigeria took place on 15 January 1966, led by Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu and others. Its immediate cause was the crisis after the rigged Western Region election of October 1965, which led to widespread violence, arson and killing (\"Operation Wetie\") and a breakdown of law and order: C. The trap: D, the 1962–63 census crisis, and the 1964 federal election crisis were remote causes that built up tension. B, the Tiv riots (1960 and 1964), and A, the Kano riots (1953), were earlier crises too. Memory tip: Wild Wild West 1965 triggered January 1966.",
 "|Nigeria demonstrated her commitment to the policy of non-alignment during the regime of|Muhammadu Buhari|Johnson Aguiyi-Ironsi|Murtala Muhammed|Ibrahim Babangida": "Non-alignment means refusing to join either the Western (US-led) or Eastern (Soviet-led) bloc in the Cold War. Nigeria joined the Non-Aligned Movement, but under Balewa leaned towards the West. The clearest demonstration came under Murtala Muhammed (1975–76). In 1975 he recognised the MPLA government in Angola, which the US opposed, and rejected American pressure, saying \"Africa has come of age\": C. The trap: A, Buhari (1984–85), was known for discipline at home and expelling illegal aliens; B, Ironsi, ruled only six months; D, Babangida, was known for economic diplomacy. Memory tip: Murtala Muhammed made Nigeria's non-alignment real.",
 "|Nigeria's support for the South-South Cooperation is based on her desire to|promote economic understanding in the Third World|counter the political and military domination by major powers|assert her leadership role in Africa|promote her non-aligned policy": "South-South Cooperation means cooperation among developing countries of Africa, Asia and Latin America (the \"South\") in trade, technology and development, rather than depending on the industrialised North. Nigeria supports it to promote economic understanding and cooperation within the Third World: A. Babangida's government, for example, hosted South-South meetings and set up the Technical Aid Corps (1987) to share expertise with other developing countries. The trap: B, countering great power domination, is closer to non-alignment, which is political. C and D are separate aims of Nigerian foreign policy. Memory tip: South-South = economic solidarity among developing nations.",
 "|Nigeria's relation with black political communities outside Africa is built on|economic considerations|shared political aspirations|perceived cultural affinities|expectations of political support from them": "Nigeria sees herself as a leader of the black race. Her ties with black communities outside Africa, in the Caribbean, Brazil, the USA and Britain, rest on shared African descent and cultural heritage. Nigeria hosted the Second World Black and African Festival of Arts and Culture (FESTAC '77) in Lagos in 1977 to celebrate this link: C. The trap: A, economic considerations, matter in Nigeria's trade with many countries, but not in this special relationship; B and D are not the basis since these communities are not states. Memory tip: FESTAC = family ties of the black world, culture first.",
 "|The one-time president of the United Nations General Assembly was|Maitama Sule|Joseph Garba|Ibrahim Gambari|Arthur Mbanefo": "The President of the UN General Assembly is elected each year to preside over its sessions. Major-General Joseph Nanven Garba, Nigeria's Permanent Representative to the UN (1984–1989), was elected President of the 44th session (1989–1990): B. Since the paper was set, another Nigerian, Professor Tijjani Muhammad-Bande, presided over the 74th session (2019–2020). The trap: A, Maitama Sule, was also Nigeria's UN ambassador and chaired the UN Special Committee against Apartheid, but he did not preside over the Assembly. C, Ibrahim Gambari, was later a UN Under-Secretary-General. Memory tip: Garba guarded the gavel in 1989.",
 "|The leaders who spearheaded the transformation of the Organization of African Unity into the African Union are from|South Africa, Libya and Zambia|Nigeria, Libya and South Africa|Nigeria, Liberia and Kenya|Algeria, Libya and Morocco": "The Organisation of African Unity (founded in 1963) was replaced by the African Union. The process began with the Sirte Declaration of 9 September 1999, at a summit hosted by Muammar Gaddafi of Libya; the Constitutive Act was adopted at Lome in 2000; and the AU was launched in Durban in 2002. The leaders who drove it were Gaddafi, Olusegun Obasanjo of Nigeria and Thabo Mbeki of South Africa: B. The trap: A swaps Nigeria for Zambia; D includes Morocco, which had left the OAU in 1984 and rejoined the AU only in 2017. Memory tip: the AU was built by L-N-S: Libya, Nigeria, South Africa.",
 "|The countries in which Nigeria participated in the ECOMOG peace-keeping operations were|Liberia and Guinea|Sierra Leone and Cote d'Ivoire|Senegal and Cote d'Ivoire|Liberia and Sierra Leone": "ECOMOG, the ECOWAS Ceasefire Monitoring Group, was ECOWAS's peacekeeping force. Nigeria supplied most of its soldiers and money. It was first deployed in Liberia in 1990 during the civil war, and later in Sierra Leone (1997–2000), where it restored the elected President Kabbah: D. The trap: A and B each contain one right country with one wrong one; Guinea and Côte d'Ivoire contributed troops or hosted refugees but were not where Nigeria's main ECOMOG operations took place. ECOMOG also served briefly in Guinea-Bissau (1998–99). Memory tip: ECOMOG's twin missions were Liberia and Sierra Leone.",
 "|The permanent members of the Security Council of the United Nations are|Britain, Japan, Australia, Germany and the United States|Germany, France, Poland, Hungary and China|the United States, Russia, France, Britain and China|the United States, Russia, France, Britain and Japan": "The UN Security Council has 15 members: 10 non-permanent members elected for two years, and 5 permanent members with veto power. The five permanent members are the United States, Russia (which took the Soviet Union's seat in 1991), France, Britain and China: C. This is still true today. The trap: D swaps China for Japan; Japan and Germany are major economic powers and have campaigned for permanent seats, but they were defeated powers in the Second World War and have none. A and B include countries that never held permanent seats. Memory tip: the P5 are the five big Allied winners of World War Two.",
 "|A specialized agency of the United Nations Organization is the|World Health Organization|General Assembly|International Court of Justice|International Olympic Committee": "UN specialised agencies are independent international organisations linked to the UN by agreements, each working in a particular field: WHO (health), FAO (food), UNESCO (education and culture), ILO (labour), IMF and the World Bank. So the WHO is a specialised agency: A. The trap: B and C, the General Assembly and the International Court of Justice, are principal organs of the UN, not specialised agencies; the other principal organs are the Security Council, ECOSOC, the Secretariat and the Trusteeship Council. D, the IOC, is a private sports body. Memory tip: agencies have a specialty (health, food, labour); organs are the UN's own body parts.",
 "|With the admission of Asian and African countries to the Commonwealth, the Queen of England became the|head of state of these countries|head of government of these countries|patron of the Commonwealth|head of the Commonwealth": "The Commonwealth was once made of Britain and dominions that all shared the British monarch as head of state. When India wanted to become a republic yet stay in, the London Declaration of 1949 created the title \"Head of the Commonwealth\", a symbolic head of the whole association. As Asian and African republics joined, the Queen became the Head of the Commonwealth rather than head of state of each: D. (The paper printed this option as \"chairperson of the Commonwealth\"; the correct title is Head.) Since 2022, King Charles III has been Head of the Commonwealth. The trap: A is true only for realms like Jamaica, not republics such as Nigeria since 1963. C, patron, is not her title. Memory tip: Head of the Commonwealth, not head of every state.",
 "|A major problem of the defunct Organization of African Unity was|ideological differences|language barrier|inadequate resources|cultural diversity": "The OAU (1963–2002) achieved much in fighting colonialism and apartheid, but it was weakened by ideological differences among its members. From the start, the radical Casablanca group (Nkrumah's Ghana, Guinea, Egypt) wanted immediate political union, while the moderate Monrovia group (including Nigeria) preferred gradual cooperation; later, Cold War divisions between pro-East and pro-West states split members on issues like Angola: A. The trap: C, inadequate resources, was also a real problem, but the main one was disagreement on goals. B and D, language and cultural diversity, were managed through common working languages. Memory tip: the OAU split over ideas before money.",
 "|The Lagos Treaty of May 28th 1975 led to the formation of the|Economic Commission for Africa|Economic Community of West African States|Lagos Plan of Action|African Economic Summit": "The Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) was founded by the Treaty of Lagos, signed on 28 May 1975 by 15 West African heads of state. General Yakubu Gowon of Nigeria and President Gnassingbe Eyadema of Togo led the effort: B. The trap: C, the Lagos Plan of Action, is also a \"Lagos\" document, but it was adopted by the OAU in 1980 for African economic development. A, the UN Economic Commission for Africa, was set up in 1958 in Addis Ababa. D is not a treaty body. Memory tip: Lagos 1975 = ECOWAS; Lagos 1980 = Plan of Action.",
 "|The current Executive Secretary of the ECOWAS is|Lansana Kouyate|Abubakar Ouattara|Mohammed Ibn Chambas|Abass Bundu": "In 2004, the Executive Secretary of ECOWAS was Dr Mohamed Ibn Chambas of Ghana, who took office in 2002 after Lansana Kouyaté of Guinea (1997–2002): C. In 2007 the Secretariat became the ECOWAS Commission, and Chambas became its first President. Today the head is the President of the ECOWAS Commission, Omar Alieu Touray of The Gambia (since 2022). The trap: A, Kouyaté, was the immediate predecessor, so he is tempting for anyone with older notes; D, Abass Bundu of Sierra Leone, served 1989–1993. Memory tip: \"current\" means current when the paper was set; check the year of the paper.",
 "|The responsibility for admitting new members to the OPEC rests with the|Board of Governors|Conference|Secretariat|Summit": "OPEC (founded in Baghdad in 1960; Nigeria joined in 1971) has three main organs. The Conference of oil ministers is the supreme authority: it sets policy, approves the budget and admits new members. Admission needs a three-fourths majority of full members, including all five founder members (Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, Venezuela): B. The trap: A, the Board of Governors, directs management and implements Conference decisions; C, the Secretariat in Vienna, does the administrative work under the Secretary General. D, summit, is not an OPEC organ. Memory tip: the Conference is the Club's committee that lets people in.",
 "|The ultimate authority in a state is|legitimacy|influence|power|sovereignty": "Sovereignty is the supreme, final and unlimited legal authority of the state. Inside its borders no person or body is above it, and outside it is independent of control by other states. Jean Bodin (1576) called it absolute and indivisible, and John Austin defined the sovereign as the one whom others habitually obey. So the ultimate authority in a state is sovereignty: D. The trap: power (C) is the ability to make others obey, which even a robber has; legitimacy (A) is the people's acceptance that a ruler has the right to rule; influence (B) is persuasion without command. Memory tip: Sovereignty = Supreme authority inside, independence outside.",
 "|The study of government essentially facilitates the understanding of the|organization of the executive arm of government|observance of fundamental human rights|functioning of the entire social formation|governance of human societies": "Government as an academic subject studies how human societies are governed: what the state is, how its institutions are organised, how power is won and used, and how rulers and citizens relate through rights, duties, parties and elections. That broad aim is the governance of human societies: D. The trap: A (the executive) and B (human rights) are real topics in Government, but each is only one part of the subject, not its whole purpose. C, the \"entire social formation\", is the wider field of sociology and political economy. Memory tip: when asked for the PURPOSE of a subject, choose the broadest correct option, not one chapter of it.",
 "|The most important aspect of political participation in a democracy is|registration with a political party|the observance of electoral processes|voting in elections|attending political rallies": "Political participation means the ways citizens take part in choosing and influencing their rulers: voting, joining parties, attending rallies, contesting elections, lobbying. In a democracy the most important and most widespread form is voting, because the ballot is how the people actually choose and remove governments, and it gives each adult an equal say (\"one man, one vote\"): C. The trap: registering with a party (A) and attending rallies (D) are forms of participation, but many citizens never do them and they do not by themselves decide who rules; observing electoral processes (B) is what monitors do. Memory tip: in a democracy, power flows through the ballot box.",
 "|A major flaw in a liberal democracy is|its emphasis on political rights over economic rights|its emphasis on collective ownership|the limitation of the freedom of expression|that it promotes political instability": "Liberal democracy (Britain, the USA, Nigeria) protects political and civil rights: the vote, free speech, free association and fair trials. But it leaves the economy largely to private ownership and the free market. Socialist critics say this is its major flaw: everyone has an equal vote, yet wealth and economic opportunity stay very unequal, so the rich dominate politics. It stresses political rights over economic rights: A. The trap: B (collective ownership) belongs to socialism, the opposite of liberal democracy, and C is wrong because liberal democracy actually guarantees freedom of expression. Memory tip: liberal democracy = freedom of the ballot, not freedom from want.",
 "|A non-monarchical state can best be described as a|confederation|republic|federation|nation": "A republic is a state whose head of state is not a hereditary king or queen but a person elected or appointed for a fixed term. The word comes from the Latin res publica, \"the public thing\". Nigeria became a republic on 1 October 1963, when a Nigerian president (Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe) replaced the British Queen as head of state: B. The trap: federation (C) and confederation (A) describe how power is shared between centre and regions, not who heads the state; a federation can be a monarchy (Malaysia, Australia). A nation (D) is a people with a shared identity. Memory tip: no crown = republic.",
 "|An elected legislator in a presidential system can lose his seat by|cross-carpeting|recall|impeachment|a vote of no confidence": "Recall is the power of the voters of a constituency to remove their own legislator before the end of his term. It suits the presidential system, where legislators are elected for fixed terms. In Nigeria's 1999 Constitution (section 69), a member can be recalled if more than half of the registered voters of his constituency sign a petition and a majority then vote for recall in a referendum conducted by INEC: B. The trap: impeachment (C) removes an executive (president or governor), and a vote of no confidence (D) removes a parliamentary government. Cross-carpeting (A) is changing parties; it is not the standard device by which voters remove a legislator. Memory tip: voters RECALL legislators; legislators IMPEACH executives.",
 "|In judicial administration, the term 'the bench' refers to the|judges|lawyers|litigants|registrars": "In legal language \"the bench\" means the judges or magistrates who sit to hear and decide cases; the name comes from the bench on which judges once sat. Lawyers who argue cases are called \"the bar\", from the rail that separated them from the court. So the bench refers to the judges: A. The trap: lawyers (B) are the bar, not the bench; litigants (C) are the parties to a case; registrars (D) are court officials who keep records and process documents. Memory tip: judges sit on the BENCH; lawyers stand at the BAR.",
 "|One of the foremost theorists of federalism was|K.C. Wheare|Baron de Montesquieu|A.V. Dicey|Karl Marx": "Professor K.C. Wheare's book \"Federal Government\" (1946) is the classic study of federalism. He defined the federal principle as \"the method of dividing powers so that the general and regional governments are each, within a sphere, coordinate and independent\", and studied the USA, Canada, Australia and Switzerland: A. The trap: Baron de Montesquieu (B) is famous for the separation of powers in \"The Spirit of the Laws\" (1748); A.V. Dicey (C) for the rule of law (1885); Karl Marx (D) for communism and class struggle. Memory tip: Wheare = Where power is shared (federalism); Montesquieu = separation; Dicey = rule of law.",
 "|How does the president relieve a minister of his appointment in a presidential system of government?|By unilateral action|In consultation with the legislature|After serving a full tenure|In consultation with the judiciary": "In a presidential system the president is the chief executive and the ministers are his appointees and assistants, not members of the legislature. In Nigeria the Senate must confirm a ministerial nominee, but once appointed, a minister holds office at the president's pleasure and can be removed by the president alone, at any time, without asking anyone: A. The trap: B is tempting because the legislature takes part in APPOINTING ministers; it has no role in DISMISSING them. Ministers have no fixed tenure (C), and the judiciary (D) plays no part. Memory tip: the Senate says \"yes\" to hire; the president alone says \"go\".",
 "|Under a parliamentary system of government, the cabinet holds office at the pleasure of the|electorate|legislature|opposition|head of state": "In a parliamentary system the Prime Minister and cabinet are members of parliament and are collectively responsible to it. They stay in office only as long as they keep the confidence of the majority in the legislature; if parliament passes a vote of no confidence, the government must resign or call an election. So the cabinet holds office at the pleasure of the legislature: B. The trap: the head of state (D) formally appoints the Prime Minister, but by convention must pick the majority leader and cannot freely remove him. The electorate (A) chooses parliament, not the cabinet directly. Memory tip: parliamentary cabinet = lives and dies by the confidence of parliament.",
 "|Serfs are the dominated class under|fascism|capitalism|feudalism|socialism": "Feudalism was the social and economic system of medieval Europe. The king granted land (fiefs) to lords and nobles, who owed him loyalty and military service. At the bottom were serfs, peasants bound to the land, who worked the lord's fields and paid dues in return for protection and the right to farm a small plot. They could not leave without the lord's permission. So serfs are the dominated class under feudalism: C. The trap: under capitalism (B) the dominated class, in Marxist terms, is the proletariat (wage workers), not serfs. Memory tip: Marx's stages: slaves (slavery), serfs (feudalism), proletariat (capitalism).",
 "|An unwritten constitution is|partially written and based on convention|completely unwritten and based on convention|entirely based on case laws|completely sourced from parliamentary law": "An unwritten constitution is one not contained in a single document. Britain's is the classic example. It is not literally unwritten: parts are written in statutes (Magna Carta 1215, Bill of Rights 1689, Parliament Acts), judicial decisions and authoritative books, while other parts are conventions, unwritten customs such as the monarch always assenting to bills. So it is partially written and based on convention: A. The trap: B, \"completely unwritten\", takes the name too literally. C and D each name only one source. Memory tip: \"unwritten\" really means \"uncodified\": written in many places, not in one book.",
 "|The principle of checks and balances empowers the judiciary to|administer the criminal justice system|apply the law|invalidate the actions of the other arms|abrogate the law": "Checks and balances means each arm of government can limit the others so none becomes tyrannical. The judiciary's check is judicial review: it can declare a law of the legislature or an act of the executive unconstitutional, null and void. The US Supreme Court established this in Marbury v. Madison (1803), and Nigerian courts do it under the 1999 Constitution. So it empowers the judiciary to invalidate the actions of the other arms: C. The trap: applying the law (B) is the judiciary's ordinary function, not a check; abrogating (repealing) laws (D) is for the legislature. Memory tip: checks = each arm can STOP another; the courts stop by striking down.",
 "|The final stage in the process of enacting legislation is|guillotine|notification|assent|final reading": "A bill passes through first reading, second reading (debate on principles), committee stage, report stage and third reading in each house. Even then it is not yet law: it must receive the assent (signature) of the head of state, in Nigeria the president for federal bills (section 58) or the governor for state bills. Assent is the final stage that turns a bill into an Act: C. The trap: the final (third) reading (D) is the last stage inside the legislature, not the last stage of the whole process. A guillotine (A) is a time limit on debate; notification (B) is not a stage. Memory tip: readings in the House, ASSENT in the president's house.",
 "|Citizenship acquired through marriage is referred to as|naturalization|registration|honorary|nationalization": "Nigeria's 1999 Constitution gives three ways to become a citizen: birth (section 25), registration (section 26) and naturalization (section 27). Registration is for a foreign woman married to a Nigerian man, and for some people of Nigerian grandparentage born abroad. So citizenship acquired through marriage is citizenship by registration: B. The trap: naturalization (A) is for foreigners who have lived in Nigeria for a long time (15 years) and meet conditions such as good character; it is not tied to marriage. Honorary citizenship (C) is a symbolic honour, and nationalization (D) is state takeover of private businesses. Memory tip: Birth, Registration (marriage), Naturalization (residence).",
 "|In a first-past-the-post electoral system, a candidate is declared elected when he|obtains a two-thirds majority of the votes cast|records the highest number of the votes cast|scores the aggregate of the opponents' votes|obtains one-third of the votes cast": "First-past-the-post, also called the simple majority or plurality system, is used for single-member constituencies in Britain and in Nigeria's legislative elections. The candidate with the highest number of votes wins, even if he has less than half of the votes cast. If A has 40%, B 35% and C 25%, A wins: B. The trap: a two-thirds majority (A) is needed to amend a constitution or override a veto, not to win an ordinary seat. C describes an absolute majority (more than all opponents together), which some systems require for a run-off. Memory tip: like a race, the first past the post wins, however slow.",
 "|On the basis of its structure, a political party can be classified as indirect if|it campaigns for votes through agents|its membership is acquired through other groups|it controls government indirectly|it contests elections by proxy": "Maurice Duverger classified parties by structure into direct and indirect parties. In a direct party, people join as individuals and pay dues. In an indirect party, people become members through other organisations affiliated to the party, such as trade unions, cooperatives or farmers' associations; the classic example is the British Labour Party, whose early members came mainly through the trade unions. So a party is indirect if its membership is acquired through other groups: B. The trap: A, C and D all play on the word \"indirectly\", but campaigning through agents and contesting by proxy describe methods, not membership structure. Memory tip: indirect party = you join through your union.",
 "|The pressure group that resorts to unconventional methods to achieve its objectives is called|promotional group|interest group|anomic group|institutional group": "Gabriel Almond classified pressure groups into four types: associational (organised bodies like the NLC or NBA), institutional (groups inside government such as the army or civil service), non-associational (based on kinship, ethnicity or religion) and anomic. Anomic groups are spontaneous and unorganised; they spring up suddenly and use unconventional methods such as riots, violent demonstrations and blocking roads: C. The trap: promotional groups (A) campaign for a cause and interest groups (B) defend members' interests; both normally use lawful methods like lobbying. Memory tip: anomic = a-nomos = \"without law\": lawless, riotous action.",
 "|The method used to determine the possible outcome of an electoral contest is|opinion poll|referendum|plebiscite|press review": "An opinion poll asks a carefully chosen sample of voters how they intend to vote or what they think of an issue, then estimates the views of the whole electorate. That is how forecasters predict the likely outcome of an election before it is held: A. The trap: a referendum (B) and a plebiscite (C) are votes in which the whole electorate decides a question directly, for example the 1961 plebiscites in the British Cameroons; they do not forecast an election. A press review (D) only summarises what newspapers say. Memory tip: a poll PREDICTS; a referendum DECIDES. (The paper misprinted option B's letter as \"D\".)",
 "|The major function of the civil service is|advising government and implementing its policies|promoting the interest of civil servants|providing relevant information on government|keeping records of the activities of government": "The civil service is the body of permanent, professional, non-partisan officials in government ministries. Its two main jobs are to advise ministers when policy is being made, giving facts and expert opinion, and then to implement the policies the government decides on. These are its central functions: A. The trap: keeping records (D) and providing information (C) are real but smaller duties that serve the main ones. Promoting the interest of civil servants (B) is the job of their trade unions. Memory tip: civil servants ADVISE before a decision and IMPLEMENT after it.",
 "|Under the emirate system, legislative functions were performed by the|Ulama|Khadi|Emir|Hakimi": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirate system after the 1804 jihad, the Emir held supreme political, religious and judicial authority. He made laws for the emirate within the limits of Islamic (Sharia) law, appointed officials and could remove them, and his court was the highest court. So legislative functions were performed by the Emir: C. The trap: the Ulama (A) were Islamic scholars who advised on the Sharia but did not make law; the Khadi or Alkali (B) were judges; the Hakimi (D) were district heads who collected taxes and kept order. Memory tip: in the emirate, all three powers met in one man: the Emir.",
 "|The pre-colonial Tiv society can best be described as|monarchical|republican|aristocratic|feudalistic": "The pre-colonial Tiv of the Benue valley, like the Igbo, were an acephalous (headless) society: they had no king or central government. Authority was spread among lineage elders and family heads, decisions were reached by consensus in meetings, and age and kinship mattered more than rank. Such a decentralised, participatory society is described as republican: B. The trap: monarchical (A) fits the Hausa-Fulani emirates or the Oyo kingdom; feudalistic (D) fits the emirate system with its land-holding fief-holders; aristocratic (C) means rule by a noble class, which the Tiv lacked. Memory tip: Tiv and Igbo = acephalous = republican; Hausa-Fulani and Oyo = centralised = monarchical.",
 "|The failure of the assimilation policy necessitated the introduction of|indirect rule|direct rule|association|paternalism": "France first ruled its African colonies by assimilation: it tried to turn Africans into Frenchmen by spreading French language, law and culture, and granting French citizenship to the few \"évolués\". It failed: few Africans qualified, it was costly, and Africans resisted losing their cultures. So France adopted association, a policy that respected local customs and institutions while keeping colonies firmly under French control: C. The trap: indirect rule (A) was the British policy, associated with Lord Lugard in Northern Nigeria; direct rule (B) was the method, not the replacement policy. Memory tip: French colonies: Assimilation, then Association (A to A).",
 "|The political party formed in 1923 by Herbert Macaulay was|National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons|Nigerian National Democratic Party|Nigerian Youth Movement|United National Independent Party": "Herbert Macaulay, often called the father of Nigerian nationalism, founded the Nigerian National Democratic Party (NNDP) on 24 June 1923. It was Nigeria's first political party, formed to contest the three Lagos seats in the Legislative Council created by the Clifford Constitution of 1922, which it won repeatedly until 1938: B. The trap: the NCNC (A) was founded in 1944 with Macaulay as its first president and Azikiwe as secretary, so it is linked to Macaulay but came later; the Nigerian Youth Movement (C) began in 1934 and defeated the NNDP in 1938. Memory tip: Clifford 1922 gave elections; Macaulay's NNDP 1923 contested them.",
 "|In the First Republic, the official opposition party was the|Northern People's Congress|Northern Elements Progressive Union|National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons|Action Group": "After the 1959 federal election, the NPC (strongest in the North) formed a coalition government with the NCNC, and Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa became Prime Minister. The third major party, Chief Obafemi Awolowo's Action Group, became the official opposition in the federal House of Representatives, and Awolowo became Leader of the Opposition at independence: D. The trap: the NPC (A) and NCNC (C) were in government, not opposition; NEPU (B), led by Aminu Kano, was a small northern ally of the NCNC. Memory tip: First Republic: NPC + NCNC = government; AG = opposition (Awolowo, leader of the opposition).",
 "|The first federal constitution in Nigeria was the|Clifford Constitution|Macpherson Constitution|Lyttleton Constitution|Independence Constitution": "Nigeria's colonial constitutions moved step by step: Clifford (1922) introduced the elective principle; Richards (1946) introduced regionalism; Macpherson (1951) gave a quasi-federal system; and Lyttleton (1954) established a true federation. The Lyttleton Constitution divided powers between the federal government and three regions (plus the Southern Cameroons) through exclusive, concurrent and residual lists, and made Lagos federal territory: C. The trap: Macpherson (B) is called quasi-federal because the centre could still overrule the regions. The Independence Constitution of 1960 (D) continued an existing federation. Memory tip: C-R-M-L: Clifford elections, Richards regions, Macpherson semi-federal, Lyttleton full federal.",
 "|A distinctive feature of the Richards Constitution was its provision for|official members in the Legislative Council|unofficial members in the Legislative Council|a Legislative Council for the whole of Nigeria|the establishment of local councils": "The Richards Constitution of 1946, under Governor Sir Arthur Richards, created a Legislative Council for the whole of Nigeria, bringing the North into central law-making for the first time (under Clifford the Council legislated only for the South). It also created regional Houses of Assembly for the North, West and East, starting regionalism: C. The trap: official members (A) and unofficial members (B) had both existed in the Legislative Council since 1922, so they were not distinctive. Local councils (D) were not its feature. Memory tip: Richards = whole-country Legislative Council + Regions.",
 "|Under the 1999 Constitution, the appointment of ministers and ambassadors must be ratified by the|House of Representatives|Senate|Presidency|National Council of States": "The 1999 Constitution makes the president nominate ministers (section 147) and ambassadors (section 171), but each appointment must be confirmed by the Senate before the nominee takes office. This is a check by the legislature on the executive. So ministers and ambassadors must be confirmed by the Senate: B. The trap: the House of Representatives (A) is the other chamber, but it does not screen nominees; it has a bigger role on money bills. The National Council of States (D) advises the president on matters such as the prerogative of mercy. Memory tip: Senate Screens and Sanctions appointments.",
 "|The legislature in Nigeria under the 1999 Constitution is composed of|100 senators and 250 members of the House of Representatives|108 senators and 350 members of the House of Representatives|109 senators and 360 members of the House of Representatives|109 senators and 350 members of the House of Representatives": "Nigeria's National Assembly is bicameral. The Senate has 109 members: three senators from each of the 36 states (108) plus one from the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja. The House of Representatives has 360 members elected from single-member constituencies shared out roughly by population: C. The trap: D gets the Senate right but the House wrong (350). B forgets the FCT senator (108). A matches no Nigerian legislature. These numbers have not changed since 1999. Memory tip: 36 states x 3 + 1 = 109 senators; 360 reps, like the 360 degrees of a circle.",
 "|The head of the Nigerian judiciary is the|Attorney-General and Minister of Justice|Chief Justice of the Federation|President of the Court of Appeal|Solicitor-General of the Federation": "The Chief Justice of Nigeria (officially the Chief Justice of the Federation) heads the Supreme Court and the whole Nigerian judiciary. He or she is appointed by the president on the recommendation of the National Judicial Council, subject to Senate confirmation, and chairs the National Judicial Council: B. The trap: the Attorney-General and Minister of Justice (A) is the government's chief law officer and a member of the executive, not the head of the judiciary. The President of the Court of Appeal (C) heads only that court; the Solicitor-General (D) is the top civil servant in the Ministry of Justice. Memory tip: CJN heads the Courts; AGF advises the Government.",
 "|The commission established to handle the electoral activities in the Second Republic of Nigeria was|INEC|NECON|FEDECO|NEC": "The Federal Electoral Commission (FEDECO) was set up by the Murtala/Obasanjo regime in 1976. It registered the five parties of 1978 (NPN, UPN, NPP, GNPP, PRP) and conducted the 1979 and 1983 elections of the Second Republic: C. The trap: INEC (A), the Independent National Electoral Commission, was created in 1998 and has run elections since the Fourth Republic began in 1999. NEC (D), the National Electoral Commission, served the Babangida transition (1987–1993), and NECON (B) served the Abacha era. Memory tip: ECN (First Republic), FEDECO (Second), NEC (Third), NECON (Abacha), INEC (Fourth).",
 "|A major function of the State Independent Electoral Commissions in Nigeria is the|conduct of gubernatorial elections|delineation of electoral constituencies|registration of political parties|conduct of elections into local government offices": "The 1999 Constitution created a State Independent Electoral Commission (SIEC) for each state. Its main job is to organise, conduct and supervise elections into local government councils: chairmen, vice-chairmen and councillors (Third Schedule, Part II): D. The trap: governorship elections (A), the delimitation of constituencies (B) and the registration of political parties (C) are all functions of INEC, the national electoral body. Memory tip: INEC runs elections from the president down to the state assembly; SIEC runs local council elections.",
 "|In Nigeria, promotion of judges is the responsibility of the|Council of Legal Education|Attorney-General and Minister of Justice|Judicial Service Commission|Chief Justice of the Federation": "To keep judges independent, their appointment, promotion and discipline are given to judicial bodies, not to politicians. In Nigeria this is done by the Judicial Service Commissions (the Federal Judicial Service Commission and the state Judicial Service Commissions), working with the National Judicial Council created by the 1999 Constitution: C. The trap: the Chief Justice (D) chairs the National Judicial Council, but promotion is a commission's collective decision, not his personal power; the Attorney-General (B) is a member of the executive; the Council of Legal Education (A) runs the Nigerian Law School. Memory tip: judges are looked after by a JUDICIAL body, not by a politician.",
 "|During the Second Republic, the Great Nigeria People's Party won the governorship election in|Borno and Kaduna States|Gongola and Sokoto States|Borno and Gongola States|Sokoto and Bauchi States": "In the 1979 governorship elections the NPN won seven states, the UPN five, the NPP three, the PRP two (Kano and Kaduna) and the GNPP two: Borno, where Alhaji Mohammed Goni became governor, and Gongola, where Alhaji Abubakar Barde became governor. Both lay in the north-east, the home area of the party's leader, Alhaji Waziri Ibrahim: C. The trap: Kaduna (A) went to the PRP (Balarabe Musa), and Sokoto and Bauchi (B, D) went to the NPN. Memory tip: GNPP's two = the two north-eastern states, Borno and Gongola, home of Waziri Ibrahim.",
 "|In local government administration, Chief-and-Council implies that|all members of the local council are appointed and controlled by the chief|the chief is a member of the local council but he does not exercise veto power|the council operates independently of the chief's authority|members of the local council appoint the chief to office": "In the Native Authorities of colonial Nigeria there were three forms. A Sole Native Authority ruled alone. Under Chief-in-Council the chief had to consult his council but could override its advice. Under Chief-and-Council the chief sat as a member of the council and had to accept its majority decision, so he had no veto: B. The trap: A describes a sole native authority, where the chief controls everything; C is wrong because the chief is still part of the council; D is wrong because the chief was a traditional ruler, not chosen by the council. Memory tip: Chief-IN-Council: the chief is IN charge; Chief-AND-Council: the chief is just one AND the rest outvote him.",
 "|In the present local government structure, traditional rulers play|a judicial role|an advisory role|a legislative role|an executive role": "The 1976 local government reform made local councils the third tier of government, run by elected chairmen and councillors. Traditional rulers were removed from executive and judicial roles and placed in Traditional Councils, whose job is to advise the councils, help with chieftaincy matters and mobilise their people. That arrangement continues today: B. The trap: before the reform, chiefs did play executive and judicial roles (D, A) as Native Authorities and through native courts, so these options describe the old system, not the present one. They make no laws (C). Memory tip: after 1976 the chief became the council's adviser, not its boss.",
 "|The first successful disengagement of the military from politics in Nigeria was carried out by the government of|Murtala/Obasanjo|Buhari/Idiagbon|Ibrahim Babangida|Abdulsalami Abubakar": "General Murtala Mohammed came to power in July 1975 and announced a programme to return Nigeria to civil rule by 1979. After his assassination in February 1976, General Olusegun Obasanjo carried it out: a new constitution, the creation of FEDECO, party registration and elections. On 1 October 1979 he handed over to the elected president, Alhaji Shehu Shagari. This was the first successful military disengagement: A. The trap: Abdulsalami Abubakar (D) also handed over, in 1999, but that was the second. Babangida's transition (C) collapsed with the annulment of the June 1993 election, and Buhari/Idiagbon (B) never handed over. Memory tip: 1979 Obasanjo to Shagari; 1999 Abdulsalami to Obasanjo.",
 "|The atomic bomb tests carried out around the Sahara Desert made Nigeria sever relations with|France|Canada|The United States|Germany": "In February 1960 France exploded its first atomic bomb at Reggane in the Algerian Sahara, and further tests followed. Nigerians feared radioactive fallout on West Africa and saw the tests as contempt for African lives. After another test in December 1960, the Balewa government broke diplomatic relations with France in January 1961 and banned French ships and aircraft: A. The trap: the United States (C) tested its bombs in Nevada and the Pacific, not the Sahara; Canada (B) and Germany (D) carried out no atomic tests. Memory tip: Sahara bomb = France = Nigeria's first big break with a Western power (1961).",
 "|Nigeria supports the South-South cooperation as a foreign policy objective for mutual understanding among|sub-Saharan African countries|developing countries|Asian and sub-Saharan African countries|nations south of the equator": "\"South-South cooperation\" means cooperation among the developing countries of Africa, Asia, Latin America and the Caribbean, often called the global South, in trade, technology, education and diplomacy. The term contrasts with \"North-South\" relations between these poor countries and the rich industrial nations of the North. Nigeria supports it through bodies such as the Non-Aligned Movement, the G-77 and the D-8: B. The trap: D takes \"South\" geographically; in fact Nigeria itself lies north of the equator, and so do India and most of Latin America's partners. A and C are too narrow. Memory tip: in world politics, North = rich, South = developing, whatever the map says.",
 "|An indication that Africa is still the centre-piece of Nigeria's foreign policy was the creation of the|Nigerian Institute of International Affairs|Ministry of Integration and Cooperation in Africa|Nigeria Institute of Policy and Strategic Studies|Ministry of Foreign Affairs": "Since independence Nigeria has called Africa the \"centre-piece\" (cornerstone) of its foreign policy: decolonisation, the fight against apartheid, the OAU, ECOWAS and peacekeeping in Liberia and Sierra Leone. Creating a separate Ministry of Integration and Cooperation in Africa, a whole ministry for African affairs alone, showed that this commitment was still central: B. The trap: the Ministry of Foreign Affairs (D) handles relations with the whole world, so its existence says nothing special about Africa. The Nigerian Institute of International Affairs (A) and the Institute of Policy and Strategic Studies at Kuru (C) are research and training bodies. Memory tip: a ministry with \"Africa\" in its name = Africa as centre-piece.",
 "|The Nigeria-Cameroun crisis over the Bakassi peninsula occurred owing to the interpretation of the treaty of 1913 and the|Maroua Accord|resolution of the OAU|decision of the International Court of Justice|decision of the ECOWAS Tribunal": "The 1913 Anglo-German treaty drew the border between British Nigeria and German Kamerun and placed Bakassi on the Cameroon side. In 1975 General Gowon and President Ahidjo signed the Maroua Accord (Declaration) on the maritime boundary, which later Nigerian governments refused to accept because it was never ratified by the Supreme Military Council. Disputes over these two documents led to clashes and to Cameroon's case at the ICJ: A. The trap: the ICJ decision (C) came in October 2002, as the result of the dispute, not its cause; it gave Bakassi to Cameroon, and Nigeria handed it over in August 2008 under the Greentree Agreement. Memory tip: Bakassi: 1913 treaty + 1975 Maroua, then ICJ 2002, handover 2008.",
 "|Nigeria's voting at the United Nations is guided primarily by|world peace|the cold war|her concern for Africa|her national interests": "Every state's foreign policy, including its voting at the United Nations, is guided above all by its national interest: security, territorial integrity, economic welfare and prestige. Nigeria's support for African liberation, world peace and non-alignment is itself how Nigeria defines and pursues its national interest: D. The trap: concern for Africa (C) is tempting because Africa is the centre-piece of Nigeria's policy, but it is one expression of the national interest, not the primary guide. The Cold War (B) ended in 1991, and Nigeria's policy was non-alignment. Memory tip: in foreign policy, national interest always comes first; the rest are means to it.",
 "|Under the umbrella of the OAU, Nigeria mediated in the dispute between|Kenya and Somalia|Tunisia and Morocco|Ethiopia and Somalia|Zimbabwe and Tanzania": "The Ogaden region, inhabited mainly by Somalis but ruled by Ethiopia, caused a long border dispute and two wars (1964 and 1977–78). The OAU set up a Good Offices Committee to mediate, and Nigeria, a leading OAU member, chaired it: C. The trap: Kenya and Somalia (A) also had a border dispute over Kenya's north-eastern region in the 1960s, but it was eased by the 1967 Arusha memorandum, with Zambia's President Kaunda mediating, not by Nigerian mediation. Tunisia and Morocco (B) and Zimbabwe and Tanzania (D) had no such dispute. Memory tip: Ogaden = Ethiopia v. Somalia = OAU mediation chaired by Nigeria.",
 "|Nigeria is rated in the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries as the|third largest oil producer|fifth largest oil producer|sixth largest oil producer|seventh largest oil producer": "Nigeria joined OPEC in 1971. When this paper was set, it was rated the sixth largest oil producer in the organisation: C. The trap: third (A) or fifth (B) overstate Nigeria's output then; seventh (D) understates it. Rankings change from year to year with output, quotas and membership (Angola left OPEC in 2024, for example), so check current figures, but Nigeria remains one of Africa's largest producers. Memory tip: Nigeria joined OPEC in 1971 and has long sat around sixth in the league.",
 "|Which of the following served as the Secretary-General of the United Nations?|Javier Perez de Cuellar|Mohamed El-Baradei|Ibrahim Gambari|Joseph Garba": "Javier Perez de Cuellar of Peru was the fifth Secretary-General of the United Nations, serving from 1982 to 1991; he helped end the Iran-Iraq war in 1988: A. The trap: the other three were senior international figures but never Secretary-General. Joseph Garba (D), a Nigerian, was President of the UN General Assembly (1989–90); Ibrahim Gambari (C), also Nigerian, was Nigeria's UN ambassador and later a UN Under-Secretary-General; Mohamed ElBaradei (B) of Egypt headed the International Atomic Energy Agency (1997–2009). Memory tip: Secretaries-General: Lie, Hammarskjold, U Thant, Waldheim, Perez de Cuellar, Boutros-Ghali, Annan, Ban, Guterres.",
 "|A Security Council resolution requires the concurrence of|3 of the 5 permanent members|1 of the 5 permanent members|all of the 5 permanent members|9 of the 15 council members": "Article 27 of the UN Charter says decisions of the Security Council on substantive matters need the affirmative votes of nine of its fifteen members, \"including the concurring votes of the permanent members\" (USA, Russia, China, Britain, France). Because each permanent member must concur, any one of them can block a resolution: this is the veto. So a resolution requires the concurrence of all five permanent members: C. The trap: D, nine of fifteen, is also needed, but on its own it is not enough, since nine votes fail if one permanent member says no. (In practice an abstention is not counted as a veto.) Memory tip: 9 votes + 5 \"yes\" from the Big Five (no veto).",
 "|A member of the Commonwealth that still recognizes the British monarch as its Head of State is|Ghana|India|Australia|Singapore": "A Commonwealth realm is a member that keeps the British monarch as its head of state, represented by a Governor-General. Australia is one: it voted in a 1999 referendum to keep the monarchy, and it still does today. The monarch was Queen Elizabeth II when this paper was set and has been King Charles III since September 2022: C. The trap: Ghana (A, a republic since 1960), India (B, since 1950) and Singapore (D, independent republic since 1965) are Commonwealth members, but each has its own president as head of state; they accept the monarch only as symbolic Head of the Commonwealth. Memory tip: realms keep the Crown (Australia, Canada, New Zealand); republics keep only the club.",
 "|A major achievement of the OAU was the|decolonization of Africa|promotion of development|eradication of poverty|establishment of democracy in Africa": "The Organisation of African Unity was founded in Addis Ababa on 25 May 1963. Its greatest success was the decolonisation of Africa: through its Liberation Committee it supported freedom movements until the last colonies became free and apartheid ended in South Africa in 1994: A. The trap: development (B), poverty eradication (C) and democracy (D) were aims the OAU largely failed to achieve; it was criticised as a \"club of heads of state\" that ignored coups and dictatorships under its rule of non-interference. The African Union replaced the OAU in July 2002 (launched in Durban). Memory tip: OAU = liberation success, development failure.",
 "|The power to exercise overall control of the executive functions of ECOWAS is vested in the|Technical and Special Commissions|Council of Ministers|Authority of Heads of State and Government|Secretary-General": "ECOWAS was created by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975. Its highest organ is the Authority of Heads of State and Government, which meets at least once a year, directs and controls the community's executive functions, and takes the final decisions: C. The trap: the Secretary-General (D) headed the Executive Secretariat that carried out decisions day to day; in 2007 it was replaced by the ECOWAS Commission headed by a President. The Council of Ministers (B) advises the Authority and supervises the community under it; the Technical and Special Commissions (A) prepare projects. Memory tip: in every such body, the summit of leaders is the boss.",
 "|One of the organizational weaknesses of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries is its inability to|collect membership dues promptly|punish erring members|assist poor countries|monitor global oil consumption": "OPEC, founded in Baghdad in 1960, tries to stabilise oil prices by setting production quotas for each member. Its key weakness is that it has no power to punish members who produce more than their quota, so cheating on quotas has often pushed prices down and weakened its unity: B. The trap: collecting dues (A) is not OPEC's known problem, assisting poor countries (C) is done through the separate OPEC Fund for International Development, and monitoring global oil consumption (D) is something OPEC actually does in its regular market reports. Memory tip: OPEC can set quotas but cannot enforce them.",
 "|Galadima in the emirate system refers to|a village head|the officer in charge of the army|the administrator of the capital city|a judge": "The Hausa-Fulani emirate was run by the Emir with titled officials, each in charge of one area. The Waziri was the chief adviser (like a prime minister), the Madawaki commanded the army, the Ma'aji kept the treasury, the Alkali judged cases under Sharia law, the Sarkin Ruwa handled water and fishing, and the Galadima administered the capital city. So Galadima refers to the administrator of the capital city: C. The trap: B (the army) is the Madawaki and D (a judge) is the Alkali; a village head (A) is the Mai Anguwa or Dagaci. Memory tip: Galadima guards the capital, Madawaki marches the army, Ma'aji minds the money.",
 "|After the defeat of Germany in World War I, her former colonies were administered under the League of Nations as|occupied territories|protectorates|crown colonies|mandated territories": "After Germany lost World War I, the Treaty of Versailles (1919) took away her colonies. The League of Nations did not hand them over outright: it gave them as \"mandates\" to the victors to govern on its behalf and report to it. Britain and France divided the Cameroons and Togoland; Britain also got Tanganyika. They were mandated territories: D. In 1946 the United Nations replaced the League, and the mandates became trust territories (the paper printed this later name). The trap: protectorates (B) and crown colonies (C) were Britain's own possessions, not held under the League. Memory tip: League = Mandate; UN = Trust.",
 "|Under the 1963 Republican Constitution, the president exercised|judicial powers|nominal powers|concurrent powers|executive powers": "On 1 October 1963 Nigeria became a republic: the British Queen ceased to be head of state and Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe became the first President. But the 1963 Constitution kept the parliamentary (Westminster) system. The President was only a ceremonial head of state with nominal powers; he signed bills and appointed ministers on the Prime Minister's advice. Real executive power lay with Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa and his cabinet. So the President exercised nominal powers: B. The trap: D (executive powers) was true only from the 1979 Constitution, when Nigeria adopted the presidential system. Memory tip: 1963 = republic but ceremonial president; 1979 = executive president.",
 "|A major feature of the policy of deregulation in Nigeria is the|proliferation of public corporations|phenomenal increase in direct foreign investment|enthronement of market forces mechanism|increasing dominance of the economy by the state": "Deregulation means removing government control from parts of the economy, so that prices, exchange rates and entry into businesses (oil marketing, telecoms, banking) are set by supply and demand instead of by government order. Nigeria began it under the 1986 Structural Adjustment Programme. Its main feature is enthroning market forces: C. The trap: B (foreign investment) is a hoped-for result, not the defining feature, and it has not always been \"phenomenal\". A and D describe the opposite: more state enterprises and state control. Memory tip: deregulation = de-control; let the market decide.",
 "|At its inception in 1919, the National Congress of British West Africa was led by|Marcus Garvey|Nnamdi Azikiwe|Casely Hayford|William Du Bois": "The National Congress of British West Africa was the first nationalist movement linking Nigeria, the Gold Coast, Sierra Leone and the Gambia. It was led by J. E. Casely Hayford, a Gold Coast lawyer and writer; its first conference met in Accra in March 1920 after planning from 1919. It demanded elected seats in the colonial councils, and its pressure helped bring the elective principle in Nigeria's 1922 Clifford Constitution. The answer is C. The trap: Marcus Garvey (A) and W. E. B. Du Bois (D) were Pan-Africanists in the Americas, and Azikiwe (B) became prominent only in the 1930s. Memory tip: Casely Hayford's Congress produced Clifford's elective principle.",
 "|The main reason for the poor performance of the Public Complaints Commission in Nigeria is that|its activities are interfered with|it lacks adequate funds to play its roles|people have not been lodging complaints|it lacks the powers to prosecute offenders": "The Public Complaints Commission, set up by the Murtala/Obasanjo regime in 1975, is Nigeria's ombudsman. Any citizen can complain to it free of charge about injustice or abuse by government officials or agencies. But it can only investigate and recommend remedies; it has no power to prosecute offenders or enforce its decisions, so officials often ignore it. That is the main reason for its poor performance: D. The trap: A and B (interference and funds) are real complaints, but the basic weakness is in its legal powers. Memory tip: an ombudsman can bark (investigate and recommend) but cannot bite (prosecute).",
 "|The signing of protectorate treaty was used by the Europeans to penetrate|the Niger Delta|Eastern Nigeria|Northern Nigeria|Western Nigeria": "A treaty of protection was an agreement in which a local ruler accepted British \"protection\", in practice British control. The Royal Niger Company of George Goldie signed hundreds of such treaties with rulers in the North, including Sokoto and Gwandu in 1885, and used them to claim the region at the Berlin Conference; it was a main route of penetration into Northern Nigeria: C. Option A (the Niger Delta) is also true: in 1884 Consul Edward Hewett signed treaties with Delta chiefs, creating the Oil Rivers Protectorate in 1885. This question is debatable, so both A and C are accepted. Memory tip: treaty first, protectorate next, conquest last.",
 "|The chairman of the National Defence Council as provided in the 1999 Constitution is the|Chief of Army Staff|Chief of Defence Staff|President|Minister of Defence": "Section 153 of the 1999 Constitution sets up federal executive bodies, including the National Defence Council and the National Security Council. The Third Schedule says the National Defence Council is made up of the President as chairman, the Vice-President, the Minister of Defence, the Chief of Defence Staff and the service chiefs. It advises the President on defence and the armed forces. The chairman is the President: C. The trap: the Chief of Defence Staff (B) and the Minister of Defence (D) are members, but the President chairs as Commander-in-Chief of the armed forces. Memory tip: the President chairs every top council (Council of State, NDC, NSC).",
 "|Constitutional matters in which the Nigerian federal and state governments exercise joint authority are on the|concurrent list|reserved list|residual list|exclusive list": "Nigeria's constitution divides legislative powers into lists. The Exclusive Legislative List holds matters only the federal government can legislate on (defence, currency, foreign affairs). The Concurrent Legislative List holds matters on which both federal and state governments can make laws (education, agriculture, electricity); if their laws conflict, the federal law prevails. Matters on neither list are residual and belong to the states. So joint authority is on the concurrent list: A. The trap: the exclusive list (D) is federal only, and the residual list (C) belongs to the states. Memory tip: concurrent = \"running together\", both levels share.",
 "|Under colonial administration, what was the status of the inhabitants of the Lagos Crown Colony?|British servants|British citizens|British settlers|British subjects": "Lagos was annexed by Britain in 1861 and became a crown colony: British territory ruled directly by the Crown. People born in a colony were British subjects, owing allegiance to the British monarch. People in the protectorates (the rest of Nigeria) were only \"British protected persons\", because a protectorate was not legally British soil. So the inhabitants of Lagos colony were British subjects: D. The trap: British citizens (B) was a later legal category (British nationality law of 1948 and after) and did not give colonials full rights; servants and settlers are not legal statuses. Memory tip: colony = subject; protectorate = protected person.",
 "|In 1999, the Alliance for Democracy won the governorship election in|four states|five states|seven states|six states": "In the 1999 elections that ended military rule, three parties were registered: PDP, APP and AD. The Alliance for Democracy, built on Afenifere and the Yoruba political tradition of Awolowo, won the governorship in all six South-West states: Lagos (Bola Tinubu), Ogun, Oyo, Osun, Ondo and Ekiti. The answer is six: D. The trap: five (B) is a common slip if you forget Ekiti, created only in 1996. In 2003 the AD lost five of the six states to the PDP, keeping only Lagos; it later faded, and most of it went into the Action Congress (2006) and then the APC (2013). Memory tip: AD 1999 = six South-West states.",
 "|The 1960 Independence Constitution of Nigeria provided for the|Queen as head of state|Governor-General as head of state|Governor-General as head of government|President as head of government": "Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960 as a dominion within the Commonwealth. Under the 1960 Independence Constitution the British monarch, Queen Elizabeth II, remained Queen of Nigeria and head of state. She was represented in Nigeria by the Governor-General (Sir James Robertson, then Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe from November 1960). The Prime Minister, Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, was head of government. So the Queen was head of state: A. The trap: B, because the Governor-General performed the head-of-state duties, but he did so only as the Queen's representative. Memory tip: 1960 = Queen; 1963 = President (republic).",
 "|During colonial administration, the office of the warrant chief was introduced in|Western Nigeria|Northern Nigeria|the Lagos Territory|Eastern Nigeria": "Indirect rule needed traditional rulers to rule through. In the East, the Igbo, Ibibio and their neighbours had no centralised kings; authority was spread among elders, age grades and titled men. So the British created \"warrant chiefs\": men given a warrant (certificate) to sit in the native courts and enforce colonial orders. Many were not traditional leaders, and their corruption fuelled resentment, including the 1929 Aba Women's Riot. The answer is Eastern Nigeria: D. The trap: Northern Nigeria (B) is where indirect rule worked best, through the existing emirs. Memory tip: no kings in the East, so the British made warrant chiefs.",
 "|In the pre-colonial Yoruba political system, the council of chiefs constituted the|ombudsman|executive|judicial arm|legislative arm": "In the Oyo Empire the Alaafin was the king, but his power was checked by the Oyo Mesi, a council of seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun. The Oyo Mesi chose the Alaafin, made laws with him, advised him and, if he became tyrannical, could reject him by presenting an empty calabash or parrot's eggs, forcing him to commit suicide. As law-makers and representatives, they acted as the legislative arm: D. The trap: the Ogboni society also checked the Oyo Mesi and had judicial roles (so C is wrong here), and the executive (B) was the Alaafin himself. Memory tip: Oyo Mesi makes laws; Ogboni balances.",
 "|A major merit of privatization is that it increases|industrial harmony|activities on the stock exchange|public fiscal resources|labour turnover": "Privatization is the sale of government-owned shares in public enterprises to private individuals and firms. In Nigeria (the 1988 Privatization and Commercialization Decree, and the Bureau of Public Enterprises from 1999) many shares were sold to the public through the Nigerian Stock Exchange, which widened share ownership and greatly increased trading on the exchange. So a major merit is more activity on the stock exchange: B. The trap: C (public fiscal resources) sounds right because the sale brings in money, but it is a one-off gain and the government loses the enterprise. Labour turnover (D) is a cost, not a merit. Memory tip: privatize = sell shares = stock exchange.",
 "|The highest decision-making body under the Gowon Regime was the|Provisional Ruling Council|Supreme Military Council|Armed Forces Ruling Council|Federal Executive Council": "Each military regime had its own top ruling body. Gowon (1966–1975) and then Murtala/Obasanjo (1975–1979) ruled through the Supreme Military Council (SMC). Buhari (1984–85) also used the SMC. Babangida (1985–1993) renamed it the Armed Forces Ruling Council (AFRC), and Abacha (1993–98) and Abubakar (1998–99) used the Provisional Ruling Council (PRC). So under Gowon it was the Supreme Military Council: B. The trap: the Federal Executive Council (D) existed under Gowon too, but it was the cabinet below the SMC. Memory tip: SMC for Gowon, AFRC for Babangida, PRC for Abacha.",
 "|In pre-colonial governance, women played prominent roles in the|Tiv society|Igbo society|Yoruba Kingdom|Jukun Kingdom": "Pre-colonial Nigerian women were not all excluded from public life. In Igbo society the umuada (married daughters of a lineage) settled disputes and could sanction men, women's associations controlled markets, and in some towns such as Onitsha the Omu was a female ruler over women's affairs: B. Option C is also true: in the Yoruba kingdoms the Iyalode represented women in the council of chiefs, and palace officials such as the Iya Oba had real power. This question is debatable, so both B and C are accepted. The trap: the Tiv (A) were a strongly male, elder-led society. Memory tip: Igbo umuada, Yoruba Iyalode.",
 "|Before the 1976 reforms, the three-tier local government system was practised by|Mid-Western and Eastern Nigeria|Western and Northern Nigeria|Eastern and Western Nigeria|Northern and Eastern Nigeria": "In the 1950s the Eastern and Western Regions copied the English model of local government. The Eastern Region Local Government Ordinance of 1950 created three tiers: county, district and local councils. The Western Region Local Government Law of 1952 created divisional, district and local councils. The North kept its emir-based Native Authorities. The 1976 reform replaced all of these with one uniform single-tier system. The answer is C. Some books describe the North's Native Authority, district and village councils as tiers, so this question is debatable. The trap: B puts the North in place of the East. Memory tip: East and West copied England; the North kept its emirs.",
 "|In Nigeria, military regimes returned power to civilians through|negotiations|impositions|transition programmes|constitutional conferences": "Nigeria's military rulers did not simply hand over power overnight. They ran \"transition programmes\": timed plans that included drafting a new constitution, lifting the ban on politics, registering parties, a census or voter registration, and staged elections. Obasanjo's programme ended with the 1979 hand-over to Shagari; Babangida's long programme collapsed with the 1993 annulment; and Abubakar's brief one led to the 1999 hand-over to Obasanjo. The answer is C. The trap: constitutional conferences (D) were often one step within a transition, not the whole method. Memory tip: soldiers leave by a timetable: the transition programme.",
 "|The ECOWAS Tribunal is responsible for|interpreting ECOWAS treaties|ratifying international treaties for member states|trying erring member states of ECOWAS|ensuring compliance with global treaties": "ECOWAS was founded by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975. Its revised 1993 Treaty set up a Community Tribunal, now the ECOWAS Community Court of Justice in Abuja (working since 2001). Its main job is to interpret and apply the ECOWAS Treaty and protocols and to settle disputes over them; since 2005 individuals can also bring human-rights cases to it. So it interprets ECOWAS treaties: A. The trap: C (trying erring states) sounds like a court, but the court rules on disputes, it does not put states on trial; ratifying treaties (B) is done by each country itself. Memory tip: treaty court = treaty interpreter.",
 "|Judges of the World Court are appointed to serve for|nine years|four years|ten years|five years": "The World Court is the International Court of Justice at The Hague, the main judicial organ of the UN. It has 15 judges, elected by both the General Assembly and the Security Council (an absolute majority in each). They serve nine-year terms and can be re-elected; a third of them are elected every three years, so the court is never completely renewed at once. No two judges may come from the same country. The answer is nine years: A. The trap: five years (D) is a common term for other offices. Memory tip: 15 judges, 9 years, 1/3 renewed every 3 years.",
 "|The highest decision-making organ of OPEC is the|Special Fund|Board of Governors|Secretariat|Conference": "OPEC (founded in Baghdad in 1960, with headquarters in Vienna) has three main organs. The Conference, made up of the oil ministers of member countries, is the supreme authority: it sets policy and production levels and admits new members. The Board of Governors manages the organisation and carries out Conference decisions, and the Secretariat, headed by the Secretary-General, does the day-to-day work. The answer is the Conference: D. The trap: the Board of Governors (B) sounds senior, but it answers to the Conference. The Special Fund (A) became OFID, OPEC's aid fund. Memory tip: OPEC's Conference of ministers is king.",
 "|The transformation of the OAU to AU is essentially to|enhance Africa's economic development|accelerate Africa's political development|accelerate Africa's technological development|promote Africa's socio-culture advancement": "The OAU (1963) was set up mainly to end colonialism and defend independence. By the 1990s most of Africa was free, so leaders wanted a body to speed up economic integration and development. The Sirte Declaration (1999) and Constitutive Act (2000) created the African Union, launched in Durban in July 2002, building on the 1991 Abuja Treaty for an African Economic Community. Its economic programme is NEPAD. So the change was essentially to enhance Africa's economic development: A. The trap: the AU also has political aims (peace, democracy, and the right to intervene in grave crimes), but the driving purpose was economic. Memory tip: OAU freed Africa; AU builds Africa.",
 "|Diplomatic missions among the Commonwealth member states are referred to as|high commission|legations|embassies|consulates": "Diplomatic missions between Commonwealth countries are called high commissions, and their heads are high commissioners, because the members share a special relationship (historically, the same monarch) rather than being \"foreign\" to one another. So Nigeria has a High Commission in London and in Accra, but an Embassy in Washington. The answer is A. The trap: embassies (C), headed by ambassadors, are used between non-Commonwealth countries; legations (B) were lower-ranking missions; consulates (D) look after citizens and trade, not full diplomacy. Memory tip: Commonwealth = High Commission.",
 "|The chairman of the UN Security Council emerges through|rotation|consensus|election|selection": "The officer this question calls the chairman is officially the President of the Security Council. The presidency rotates every month among the 15 members (5 permanent and 10 non-permanent), in the English alphabetical order of their names. The president calls and chairs meetings and speaks for the Council. The answer is rotation: A. The trap: election (C) is how the non-permanent members get their seats (the General Assembly elects them for two years), and how the UN General Assembly picks its president, but not how the Council's president is chosen. Memory tip: Security Council president changes with the calendar, every month.",
 "|A written constitution is distinguished by its|dependence on ordinary amendment procedures|cumbersome and complex amendment procedure|codification process|reliance on case laws": "A written constitution is one whose main rules are collected in a single formal document, like Nigeria's 1999 Constitution or the US Constitution. What distinguishes it is that it is codified: C. An unwritten constitution, like Britain's, is spread across statutes, case law and conventions. The trap: B (a cumbersome amendment procedure) describes a rigid constitution. Most written constitutions are rigid, but rigidity and writtenness are different questions, and some written constitutions are easy to amend. This question is debatable, since some textbooks link written with rigid. Memory tip: written vs unwritten is about form (codified or not); rigid vs flexible is about amendment.",
 "|One of the most essential characteristics of the state is|sovereignty|the rule of law|legitimacy|the civil service": "A state has four essential features: a population, a defined territory, a government, and sovereignty. Sovereignty, the supreme authority inside the territory and independence from outside control, is the most essential, because it is what separates a state from a province or colony. Lagos State has people, land and a government, but it is not sovereign. The answer is A. The trap: legitimacy (C) and the rule of law (B) are features of good government, not of statehood; an illegitimate military regime still governs a sovereign state. Memory tip: P-T-G-S (people, territory, government, sovereignty), and S is the key.",
 "|Fascism originated from|Italy|Greece|Germany|China": "Fascism began in Italy. Benito Mussolini founded his Fasci di Combattimento in 1919 and took power after the March on Rome in 1922. The name comes from the fasces, a bundle of rods with an axe carried in ancient Rome as a sign of authority. Fascism glorifies the nation and the state, rejects democracy and communism, and puts one leader above all. The answer is A. The trap: Germany (C) had Hitler's Nazism (National Socialism), a related movement that took power later, in 1933. Memory tip: fasces are Roman, so fascism is Italian.",
 "|The principle of separation of powers is intimately related to|checks and balances|the bureaucratic process|democratic rule|the rule of law": "Separation of powers, explained by Montesquieu in The Spirit of the Laws (1748), divides government into legislature, executive and judiciary, with different people and functions. It goes with checks and balances: each organ can limit the others, for example the president's veto, the legislature's power to impeach, and the courts' judicial review. Separation stops one organ holding all power, and the checks stop each organ abusing its own. The answer is A. The trap: the rule of law (D) and democratic rule (C) are related ideals, but checks and balances is the twin principle. Memory tip: separate, then check and balance.",
 "|Vote of confidence is basically a feature of|a coalition government|the parliamentary system|an aristocratic government|the presidential system": "In a parliamentary system the cabinet comes from parliament and stays in office only while it keeps parliament's confidence. That confidence is tested by a vote of confidence (or of no confidence): if the government loses, it must resign or call an election. Britain is the classic example, and Nigeria's First Republic used the same system. The answer is B. The trap: in the presidential system (D) the president has a fixed term and cannot be removed by a confidence vote, only by impeachment; a coalition (A) is just a government of several parties. Memory tip: parliament gives confidence, and parliament can take it away.",
 "|One of the greatest advocates of socialism was|Baron de Montesquieu|Karl Marx|A.V. Dicey|Adam Smith": "Karl Marx (1818–1883), a German philosopher, with Friedrich Engels wrote The Communist Manifesto (1848) and Das Kapital (1867). He argued that history is a class struggle, that capitalism exploits workers, and that workers would replace it with socialism and finally communism. He is the founder of \"scientific socialism\". The answer is B. The trap: Adam Smith (D) was the father of capitalism (The Wealth of Nations, 1776); Montesquieu (A) taught separation of powers; A. V. Dicey (C) explained the rule of law. Memory tip: Smith = market; Marx = socialism; Montesquieu = separation; Dicey = rule of law.",
 "|Public opinion helps a government to|develop the country in line with the people's aspirations|monitor its socio-cultural policy|monitor the economic situation in the country|identify those that are opposed to its policies": "Public opinion is the views of the people on public issues and on their government. Through the media, polls, protests and elections it tells government what the people want and how they judge its policies. A government that listens can shape its programmes to meet those wishes, which keeps it popular and legitimate. The answer is A. The trap: D (identifying opponents) treats public opinion as a spying tool, which is not its purpose in a democracy; B and C are too narrow, covering only one area of policy. Memory tip: public opinion is the people's voice, steering government toward their wishes.",
 "|The type of government in which an individual wields absolute power is called|theocracy|autocracy|oligarchy|plutocracy": "Autocracy (Greek autos, self, and kratos, rule) is government by one person who holds absolute, unlimited power, not checked by law or by other bodies; an absolute monarch or a dictator is an autocrat. The answer is B. The trap: oligarchy (C) is rule by a few; theocracy (A) is rule by religious leaders in God's name; plutocracy (D) is rule by the rich. Memory tip: count the rulers: one = autocracy, few = oligarchy, rich = plutocracy, priests = theocracy, people = democracy.",
 "|A distinctive feature of the presidential system of government is the|existence of two political parties|fusion of legislative and executive powers|existence of a ceremonial head of state|fusion of ceremonial and executive powers": "In a presidential system, such as the USA or Nigeria since 1979, one person, the president, is both head of state (the ceremonial role: national symbol, receiving ambassadors) and head of government (the executive role: running the administration). This fusion of ceremonial and executive powers is a distinctive feature: D. The trap: C (a ceremonial head of state) and B (fusion of legislative and executive powers) are features of the parliamentary system, where a monarch or president is ceremonial and ministers sit in parliament. A two-party system (A) can exist under either system. Memory tip: presidential = one person, two hats.",
 "|Individual as opposed to collective responsibility is a basic feature of the|presidential system of government|socialist system of government|monarchical system of government|parliamentary system of government": "In a presidential system the president appoints ministers (in Nigeria, with Senate confirmation) and each minister answers individually to the president, who can sack any one of them alone. In a parliamentary system the cabinet shares collective responsibility: all ministers defend cabinet decisions together, and if the government loses a vote of no confidence, the whole cabinet falls. So individual rather than collective responsibility is a feature of the presidential system: A. The trap: D (parliamentary) is the home of collective responsibility, the opposite. Memory tip: parliament = collective; presidential = individual.",
 "|A right enjoyed by citizens but denied non-citizens is the right to|equality before the law|vote and be voted for|life, liberty and security|freedom of worship": "Some rights belong to everyone living in a country; others belong only to its citizens. Civil rights such as life, personal liberty, freedom of worship and equality before the law protect citizens and foreigners alike. Political rights, such as the right to vote and to stand for election, are reserved for citizens: a foreign resident cannot vote in Nigeria's elections or contest for office. The answer is B. The trap: A, C and D are rights, but they are human rights that every person in Nigeria enjoys. Memory tip: everyone has civil rights; only citizens have the ballot.",
 "|In a federal system, the essence of specifying the constitutional relationship between the centre and the constituent units is to|ensure the superiority of the centre|diffuse political power|guarantee political autonomy|avoid functional conflicts": "In a federation, the constitution divides powers between the centre and the units (in Nigeria, the exclusive, concurrent and residual matters). Setting this out clearly tells each level what it may do, so they do not clash over the same functions: D. Option C is also true: the written division guarantees each level its autonomy in its own sphere, which is how K. C. Wheare defined federalism. This question is debatable, so both C and D are accepted. The trap: A (superiority of the centre) is the opposite of federalism and describes a unitary state. Memory tip: clear lists, no clashes, each level free in its own field.",
 "|In a dominant one-party system, there is|one party that controls the political parties|only one political party recognized by law|one among many parties that always wins elections|one party which embraces all the citizens": "Party systems are classified by how many parties compete and win. In a one-party state only one party is legal (Tanzania under Nyerere). In a dominant-party system many parties exist and compete freely, but one keeps winning election after election, such as the ANC in South Africa since 1994 or the PDP in Nigeria from 1999 to 2015. The answer is C. The trap: B (only one party recognised by law) describes a one-party state, not a dominant-party system. Memory tip: dominant means many may run, but one always wins.",
 "|One of the ways the executive performs legislative functions is through|administrative review|extrajudicial decision|judicial review|delegated legislation": "Delegated legislation is law made not by the legislature itself but by the executive or another body, under power delegated by an act of the legislature: ministers' regulations and orders, agencies' rules, and local councils' bye-laws. It saves parliamentary time and uses expert knowledge, so it is one way the executive performs legislative functions: D. The trap: judicial review (C) is the courts' power to cancel unconstitutional laws and actions, a judicial function. Administrative review (A) is checking decisions within the administration. Memory tip: delegated legislation = borrowed law-making power, controlled by the parent act.",
 "|The form of government in which the powers of the hereditary ruler are limited is|constitutional monarchy|aristocracy|absolute monarchy|totalitarianism": "A monarchy is rule by a hereditary king or queen. In an absolute monarchy the monarch's power has no legal limits (as in Saudi Arabia). In a constitutional monarchy, the monarch's powers are limited by the constitution, and real government is carried out by elected leaders; the monarch reigns but does not rule. Britain is the classic example. The answer is A. The trap: C (absolute monarchy) is the opposite; aristocracy (B) is rule by a noble class; totalitarianism (D) is total state control of life, usually under a dictator. Memory tip: constitutional monarchy = the crown under the constitution.",
 "|As a component of the state, the civil service is important because it ensures|gender equality|effective cost-benefit analysis|continuity in governance|equality among professions": "The civil service is the permanent body of officials who run government departments and carry out policy. Civil servants are appointed on merit, are politically neutral and stay in office when ministers and governments change. Because of this, they keep government working, preserve records and experience, and ensure continuity in governance: C. The trap: gender equality (A) and equality among professions (D) are not its purpose; cost-benefit analysis (B) is one technique officials may use. Memory tip: politicians come and go; the civil service stays.",
 "|A bill could become law without the president's assent if|overruled by the Supreme Court|supported by a two-thirds majority of the legislature|necessitated by a state of emergency|assented to by the Chief Justice": "Under Nigeria's 1999 Constitution (section 58), a bill passed by the National Assembly goes to the President for assent within 30 days. If he withholds assent, the bill can be passed again by a two-thirds majority in each house, the Senate and the House of Representatives, and it then becomes law without his assent. This veto override is a check on the executive. The answer is B. The trap: the Supreme Court (A) can strike down a law but cannot make one; the Chief Justice (D) has no role in assent. Memory tip: two-thirds of the legislature beats the president's veto.",
 "|A crucial requirement for the successful conduct of elections is|the existence of organized political parties|an independent electoral commission|a highly mobilized electorate|the adequate funding of political parties": "Elections are free and fair only if the body that runs them (registration, voting, counting, declaring results) is independent and impartial. If the electoral commission takes orders from the government or a party, results can be rigged whatever else is in place. That is why Nigeria's constitution makes INEC independent, and why its independence is always debated. The answer is an independent electoral commission: B. The trap: organised parties (A) and a mobilised electorate (C) also matter, but even good parties and keen voters cannot get a fair result from a biased umpire. Memory tip: a fair match needs a fair referee.",
 "|One major determinant of Nigeria's foreign policy is|commitment to world peace|cooperation among African states|non-alignment in world affairs|external influences": "A foreign policy has determinants: the factors that shape it. For Nigeria these include its colonial history, size and population, economic resources (especially oil), ethnic and religious make-up, the type of government and its leaders, and external influences from foreign powers, international bodies and the world economy. So the determinant here is D. The trap: A, B and C are principles or objectives of Nigeria's foreign policy (world peace, African unity, non-alignment): they are what the policy aims at, not the forces that shape it. This question is debatable, since some books use \"determinant\" loosely. Memory tip: determinants push the policy; principles guide it.",
 "|The Technical Aid Corps programme was initiated by Nigeria to contribute to the socio-economic development of|developing countries|West Africa|The Third World|Africa": "The Technical Aid Corps was set up in 1987 under General Babangida, based on an idea of Foreign Minister Professor Bolaji Akinyemi. It sends young Nigerian professionals (doctors, teachers, engineers) to serve in other countries for two years, as an alternative to cash aid. It serves African, Caribbean and Pacific (ACP) countries, with Africa as its main focus, matching Nigeria's Africa-centred policy: D. Options A (developing countries) and C (the Third World) also describe these countries, so this question is debatable and all three are accepted. The trap: West Africa (B) is too narrow. Memory tip: TAC = Nigeria's skills, not cash, for Africa and the ACP.",
 "|Nigeria became a frontline state in the Southern African liberation struggle because of her|influence on African proxy wars|commitment to end colonialism|anti-apartheid stance|connection with South Africa": "The frontline states were the countries nearest to white-minority South Africa and Rhodesia (Angola, Botswana, Mozambique, Tanzania, Zambia, and later Zimbabwe) that led support for the liberation movements. Nigeria, thousands of kilometres away, was called a frontline state because of its strong anti-apartheid stance: it funded the ANC and SWAPO, nationalised BP's assets in 1979 over oil to South Africa, and led boycotts such as the 1976 Olympics. The answer is C. Option B is also true, since Nigeria backed the fight against colonial rule in Angola, Zimbabwe and Namibia, so this question is debatable and both are accepted. Memory tip: far in distance, frontline in commitment.",
 "|The New Partnership for Africa's Development was mainly promoted by|Ghana, Sierra Leone and Nigeria|Kenya, South Africa and Senegal|Nigeria, Senegal and South Africa|Tanzania, Burkina Faso and Egypt": "The New Partnership for Africa's Development (NEPAD) was adopted in 2001 as Africa's own development plan: African leaders promised good governance, and rich countries promised aid, trade and investment in return. It was mainly promoted by President Olusegun Obasanjo of Nigeria, President Thabo Mbeki of South Africa and President Abdoulaye Wade of Senegal, joined by Algeria's Bouteflika and Egypt's Mubarak. The answer is C. The trap: B (Kenya, South Africa and Senegal) and D (with Egypt) each have one or two right names but miss Nigeria. NEPAD became the African Union Development Agency (AUDA-NEPAD) in 2018. Memory tip: NEPAD's big three = Obasanjo, Mbeki, Wade.",
 "|Nigeria's first participation in a United Nations peace-keeping operation was in|Congo|Lebanon|Angola|Chad": "Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960, and within weeks, in November 1960, Nigerian troops joined the UN Operation in the Congo (ONUC), sent after the Congo's independence collapsed into mutiny and secession. Nigerian police and soldiers served there until 1964, and Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi commanded the UN force in 1964. So Nigeria's first UN peacekeeping was in the Congo: A. The trap: Lebanon (B) came later (UNIFIL, 1978) and Chad (D) was an OAU operation (1981–82), not UN. Memory tip: born in 1960, keeping peace in the Congo the same year.",
 "|Nigeria's concern for decolonization in Africa was responsible for her abandonment of pro-West policy during the|Shonekan administration|Shagari administration|Aguiyi-Ironsi regime|Murtala regime": "General Murtala Mohammed ruled from July 1975 until his assassination in February 1976. Nigeria's earlier governments were conservative and pro-West. Murtala's regime broke with this over Angola: defying the United States, Nigeria recognised the MPLA government in November 1975, and in January 1976 Murtala gave his famous \"Africa has come of age\" speech at the OAU. It was a bold, Africa-centred policy driven by concern for decolonisation. The answer is D. The trap: Shagari (B) and Shonekan (A) were moderate; Aguiyi-Ironsi (C) ruled only six months and was busy at home. Memory tip: Murtala = Angola = \"Africa has come of age\".",
 "|The first Nigerian leader to become chairman of the Organisation of African Unity was|Murtala Muhammed|Yakubu Gowon|Johnson Aguiyi-Ironsi|Tafawa Balewa": "The chairman of the OAU was the head of state of the country hosting its annual summit, serving for one year. General Yakubu Gowon was the first Nigerian to hold the post, in 1973–74, chairing the 10th anniversary summit in Addis Ababa. Later Nigerian chairmen included Obasanjo (as AU chairman, 2004–2006). The answer is B. The trap: Tafawa Balewa (D) was a founding father of the OAU in 1963 but never its chairman; Murtala (A) and Ironsi (C) ruled too briefly. The OAU was replaced by the African Union in 2002. Memory tip: Gowon, first Nigerian OAU chair, 1973.",
 "|Which of the following is a hindrance to the democratization of the United Nations?|Resistance of the big powers|Proliferation of weapons of mass destruction|The multiplicity of official languages|Delayed payment of annual dues by members": "To \"democratize\" the UN means to share its power more equally among its 193 members. The main obstacle is the five permanent members of the Security Council (USA, Russia, China, Britain, France). Each holds a veto, and any amendment to the Charter also needs their approval, so they block reforms such as abolishing the veto or adding permanent seats for Africa or Latin America. So the hindrance is the resistance of the big powers: A. The trap: delayed dues (D) and weapons of mass destruction (B) are real UN problems, but they hurt its finances and security work, not its democracy. Memory tip: the VETO is the brake on UN democracy.",
 "|An essential principle that guides the activities of the United Nations is|the regulation of international trade|the protection of the United States of America against terrorism|respect for the sovereign equality of member states|the transfer of technology to developing countries": "Article 2(1) of the UN Charter (signed in San Francisco, 26 June 1945) says the organisation \"is based on the principle of the sovereign equality of all its Members\". That is why every state, from Nigeria to tiny Nauru, has one vote in the General Assembly, and why the UN may not interfere in matters essentially within a state's domestic jurisdiction (Article 2(7)). So the guiding principle is respect for the sovereign equality of member states: C. The trap: technology transfer (D) and trade (A) are goals pursued by UN agencies, not founding principles; protecting one country (B) contradicts equality. Memory tip: in the General Assembly, one state = one vote.",
 "|The African Union was established to|form an African High Command|promote global economic dominance by Africa|address current international developments affecting Africa|consolidate Africa's position in global politics": "The African Union replaced the OAU. It was planned in the Sirte Declaration (1999), its Constitutive Act was adopted at Lome in 2000, and it was launched in Durban, South Africa, in July 2002. Its objectives include greater unity among African states and enabling Africa \"to play its rightful role in the global economy and in international negotiations\", in other words to consolidate Africa's position in global politics: D. The trap: C sounds similar, but \"addressing current developments\" is vague and describes any organisation's daily work; an African High Command (A) was Nkrumah's 1960s idea, and Africa does not seek global economic dominance (B). Memory tip: OAU freed Africa; AU positions Africa in the world.",
 "|A principal defect of the Commonwealth is|lack of commitment by member states|lack of quorum at annual summits|poverty among member states|lack of a democratic head": "The Commonwealth is a voluntary association of mainly former British territories (now 56 members). Its Head is the British monarch: Queen Elizabeth II held the title from 1952, and King Charles III has been Head since 2022. The headship is not filled by any election among the members, which critics call undemocratic for a body that preaches democracy (Harare Declaration, 1991). The paper treats this as its principal defect: D. The trap: lack of commitment (A) is also often said of the Commonwealth, since its decisions are not binding, so this question is open to argument; D is the expected answer. Memory tip: Commonwealth head = crown, not ballot.",
 "|Nigeria declared that she would not attend the OAU summit in Tripoli in 1982 unless|Colonel Gaddafi resigned as the Libyan Head of State|Libya withdrew her troops from Chad|President Shehu Shagari became the chairman of OAU|Cameroun was suspended for killing Nigerian soldiers at Bakassi": "In 1980 Libya sent troops into Chad, Nigeria's north-eastern neighbour, and in 1981 Gaddafi announced a \"merger\" of the two countries. Nigeria, which saw Chad as part of its security zone, demanded the withdrawal of Libyan troops and insisted on this before attending the OAU summit due in Tripoli in 1982. That summit failed twice for lack of a quorum. So Nigeria's condition was that Libya withdraw her troops from Chad: B. The trap: D mixes in a later issue; the Bakassi dispute with Cameroon was settled by the ICJ in 2002 and the peninsula handed over in 2008. Memory tip: Tripoli 1982: no summit until Libya leaves Chad.",
 "|Nigeria's non-aligned policy was criticized because of the|ECOWAS Treaty|Nuclear Test Ban Treaty|Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact|Non-proliferation Treaty": "Non-alignment means not joining either side of the Cold War (the Western bloc led by the USA or the Eastern bloc led by the USSR). At independence the Balewa government signed the Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact (1960), which allowed Britain military facilities and tied Nigeria to a Western power. Students and the opposition protested that it made non-alignment a sham, and it was abrogated in January 1962: C. The trap: the Nuclear Test Ban Treaty (B, 1963) and the Non-proliferation Treaty (D, 1968) were signed by countries of both blocs, so joining them shows no alignment; ECOWAS (A, 1975) is a regional body. Memory tip: a defence pact with one bloc breaks non-alignment.",
 "|NEPAD was adopted during the OAU summit held in|Tunis|Lusaka|Cairo|Abuja": "NEPAD, the New Partnership for Africa's Development, came from merging Mbeki's Millennium Africa Plan and Wade's Omega Plan. The combined plan, called the New African Initiative, was adopted at the 37th (and last) OAU summit in Lusaka, Zambia, in July 2001. In October 2001 the implementation committee of heads of state, meeting in Abuja, renamed it NEPAD. So the OAU summit was in Lusaka: B. The trap: Abuja (D) is tempting because the name NEPAD was adopted there, but that Abuja meeting was not an OAU summit. Memory tip: Lusaka adopted the plan; Abuja gave it the name.",
 "|Nigeria's interest in teaming up with other developing countries in the world is predicated on|her desire to consume more manufactured goods|her desire to produce more raw materials|preserving her independence and promoting development|getting a permanent seat in the United Nations Security Council": "Nigeria cooperates with other developing countries through the Non-Aligned Movement, the Group of 77, the OAU/AU and ECOWAS. The aim is to protect their hard-won independence from big-power domination and to press for development: fairer prices for commodities, aid, debt relief and a New International Economic Order. So it is predicated on preserving her independence and promoting development: C. The trap: a permanent Security Council seat (D) is one goal Nigeria has pursued, but it is a single ambition, not the basis of South-South cooperation; producing more raw materials (B) would deepen dependence. Memory tip: the South unites to stay free and grow.",
 "|Which of the following was once Nigeria's permanent representative at the United Nations?|Professor Bolaji Akinyemi|Major General Ike Nwachukwu|Professor Jubril Aminu|Major General Joseph Garba": "Major General Joseph Nanven Garba, who announced the 1975 coup that brought Murtala Mohammed to power and was Foreign Minister (1975–1978), became Nigeria's Permanent Representative to the United Nations from 1984 to 1989. In 1989 he was elected President of the 44th UN General Assembly: D. The trap: Professor Bolaji Akinyemi (A, 1985–87) and Major General Ike Nwachukwu (B, 1987–89) were Foreign Ministers, not UN ambassadors; Professor Jibril Aminu (C) was a minister and later Nigeria's Ambassador to the USA. Memory tip: Garba went from Dodan Barracks to the UN podium.",
 "|Ethics and accountability in government can be guaranteed through|legislative cooperation|executive and judicial immunity|due process and transparency|administrative reforms": "Ethics means acting rightly; accountability means answering for how public power and money are used. These are guaranteed when officials follow the laid-down rules and procedures (due process) and act openly so the public, the press and the legislature can see what they do (transparency). Nigeria's Due Process Office (2001) and later the Public Procurement Act 2007 were built on this idea: C. The trap: immunity (B) does the opposite, since it shields the president, vice-president, governors and deputies from prosecution while in office (section 308). Reforms (D) are too vague. Memory tip: sunlight is the best disinfectant: openness forces accountability.",
 "|The process of law-making by the legislature begins with|initiating a bill|commencing debate|a public hearing|the first reading": "A bill becomes law through set stages in each house: first reading (the title is read and the bill is published), second reading (debate on its general principles), committee stage (clause-by-clause study), report stage, third reading, and then assent. The formal process inside the legislature begins with the first reading: D. The trap: \"initiating a bill\" (A) sounds like a beginning, but drafting and sponsoring often happen outside the chamber, for example in a ministry for an executive bill; the bill only enters the legislative process when it is read the first time. Public hearings (C) happen at committee stage. Memory tip: 1st reading = title; 2nd = principles; committee = details.",
 "|A non-permanent feature of a state is|society|population|territory|government": "A state has four essential elements: population, a defined territory, a government and sovereignty. Of these, government is the one that changes: rulers come and go through elections, coups or deaths, yet the state continues. Nigeria has had civilian and military governments since 1960 but has remained the same state. So the non-permanent feature is government: D. The trap: society (A) and population (B) change in size and make-up, but a state always has a people; territory (C) is the fixed land. Memory tip: the state is permanent; the government is its temporary manager.",
 "|The ancient Greeks practised|direct democracy|representative democracy|liberal democracy|benevolent dictatorship": "In ancient Greek city-states, above all Athens in the fifth century BC, the citizens (free adult men only) met in the assembly, the ecclesia, to debate and pass laws, choose generals and decide on war themselves. Because the city was small, they did not need to elect representatives. This is direct democracy: A. The trap: representative democracy (B) arose in large modern states, where citizens elect others to decide for them; liberal democracy (C) is a modern idea stressing individual rights. Memory tip: Athens = the people themselves in the assembly; Abuja = elected representatives.",
 "|The formation of confederations is based primarily on|hegemonic considerations|historical reasons|security considerations|geographical factors": "A confederation is a loose union of sovereign states that keep their independence but give a weak central body limited powers, mostly over defence and foreign affairs. States usually join for security against a common threat, as the thirteen American states did under the Articles of Confederation (1781) during and after their war with Britain, and as the Swiss cantons did for centuries. So it is based primarily on security considerations: C. The trap: hegemony (A) means domination by one state, which confederation's equal members reject; history (B) and geography (D) can help, but they are not the main motive. Memory tip: confederation = sovereign states sheltering under one umbrella.",
 "|The declaration of election results is the exclusive duty of|political party chairmen|electoral commissions|chief justices|election petition tribunals": "Elections in Nigeria are conducted by an independent electoral commission. Its returning officers announce the results at each level, and only the commission can declare a candidate winner. Today that body is INEC, set up in 1998; earlier ones were FEDECO (1979–83) and NEC (1987–93). So declaring results is the exclusive duty of electoral commissions: B. The trap: election petition tribunals (D) hear complaints only AFTER results are declared and may nullify an election or order a rerun, but they do not make the first declaration. Party chairmen (A) are interested parties. Memory tip: the commission declares; the tribunal reviews.",
 "|The traditions of a people that shape their government and politics represent their|political culture|political ideology|political acculturation|political socialization": "Political culture, a term made popular by Gabriel Almond and Sidney Verba, is the set of attitudes, beliefs, values and traditions that a people hold about government and politics. It shapes how they see authority, whether they vote and how they treat rulers. So traditions that shape government and politics are the people's political culture: A. The trap: political socialization (D) is the PROCESS by which culture is passed on, through family, school, religion and the media; ideology (B) is an organised set of ideas such as socialism; acculturation (C) is absorbing another people's culture. Memory tip: culture is WHAT is passed on; socialization is HOW it is passed on.",
 "|According to Karl Marx, the mode of production that precedes capitalism is|mercantilism|feudalism|socialism|communalism": "Karl Marx (1818–1883) saw history as a series of modes of production, each with its own ruling and exploited class: primitive communalism, slavery (masters and slaves), feudalism (lords and serfs), capitalism (bourgeoisie and proletariat), then socialism and finally communism. The stage immediately before capitalism is feudalism: B. The trap: communalism (D) is the very first stage, far earlier; mercantilism (A) was a trade policy of European states, not one of Marx's modes; socialism (C) comes after capitalism. Memory tip: count Marx's six stages, Communalism, Slavery, Feudalism, Capitalism, Socialism, Communism; feudalism sits just before capitalism.",
 "|The executive is mainly concerned with the|confirmation of appointments|approval of treaties|approval of budgets|implementation of policies": "The three arms of government each have one main job: the legislature makes laws, the executive implements (executes) them, and the judiciary interprets them. So the executive is mainly concerned with the implementation of policies: D. The trap: confirming appointments (A), approving treaties (B) and approving budgets (C) are all things the legislature does to check the executive; in Nigeria the Senate confirms ministers and the National Assembly passes the Appropriation Act. Memory tip: legislature MAKES, executive EXECUTES, judiciary JUDGES.",
 "|A distinguishing feature of a federal state is|a written constitution|resource control|separation of powers|cultural diversity": "A federal state divides power between a central government and regional governments, each supreme in its own sphere (K.C. Wheare). That division must be written down and protected, so every federation has a written, usually rigid, constitution, along with a supreme court to settle disputes between the tiers. A written constitution is therefore a feature of every federal state: A. The trap: cultural diversity (D) is a common REASON countries adopt federalism, as in Nigeria, but it is not part of the federal structure: Germany is a federation, while many diverse states are unitary. The question is debatable on this point. Memory tip: no written division of powers, no federation.",
 "|The recognition and general acceptance of the right to rule in a country based on laid-down regulations is known as|traditional authority|constitutional authority|sovereign authority|legitimate authority": "Authority is power that people accept as rightful. Legitimacy is that recognition and acceptance of a ruler's right to rule, which can rest on tradition, on charisma or on laws and procedures such as a free election under a constitution. Recognition and general acceptance of the right to rule is therefore legitimate authority: D. The trap: constitutional authority (B) looks attractive because of the words \"laid-down regulations\", which describe Max Weber's legal-rational kind of authority, so a reader could argue for it; but the key idea in the question is acceptance, which is legitimacy. Traditional authority (A) rests on custom, not regulations. Memory tip: legitimacy = the people say \"you have the right\".",
 "|A political ideology that advocates extreme nationalism and suppression of individual liberties is|socialism|communism|feudalism|fascism": "Fascism was founded by Benito Mussolini, who took power in Italy in 1922, and a related form, Nazism, was led by Adolf Hitler in Germany (1933–1945). It glorifies the nation and the race (extreme nationalism), puts the all-powerful state above the individual, bans opposition parties, controls the press, and rules through one leader backed by force. So it is fascism: D. The trap: communism (B) also suppresses liberties in practice, but its ideology is internationalist (\"Workers of the world, unite!\") and class-based, not extreme nationalism. Memory tip: fascism = Flag and Force: nation first, freedom last.",
 "|In a democracy, a major setback in the judicial process is|political neutrality|lack of professionalism|freedom from punishment|inadequate material resources": "In a democracy the judicial process is meant to deliver fair, impartial and speedy justice. That depends on the quality of the people who run it: judges and lawyers who are competent, ethical and diligent. A lack of professionalism, shown in corruption, bias, incompetence and endless adjournments, is a major setback to justice: B. The trap: \"freedom from punishment\" (C) sounds like impunity, which is indeed bad for justice, but it describes a problem of law enforcement, not of the judicial process itself; political neutrality (A) is a virtue of judges. Inadequate resources (D) matter too, which makes the question somewhat open. Memory tip: justice is only as good as those who deliver it.",
 "|A vote taken by the citizenry on an important political issue is a|recall|general election|bye-election|referendum": "A referendum is a direct vote by the whole electorate on a particular political question, such as approving a new constitution or a change of status. Examples are Ghana's 1960 republican referendum and the United Kingdom's 2016 vote on leaving the European Union: D. The trap: a recall (A) is a vote to remove a particular legislator; a general election (B) chooses representatives for the whole country; a bye-election (C) fills one vacant seat. Memory tip: elections choose PEOPLE; referendums decide ISSUES.",
 "|A constitution is classified as rigid if|it requires special procedures to amend|its core provisions are made by decree|its provisions cannot be amended|any of its provisions can be amended": "A rigid constitution can be amended only through a special procedure more difficult than ordinary law-making. Nigeria's 1999 Constitution is rigid: under section 9, an amendment needs a two-thirds majority in each house of the National Assembly and approval by at least two-thirds (24) of the State Houses of Assembly. The USA's constitution is another example: A. The trap: C says it cannot be amended at all, which overstates rigidity, since every constitution can be amended somehow; D describes a flexible constitution, like Britain's, changed by ordinary Acts of Parliament. Memory tip: rigid = hard to change, not impossible to change.",
 "|An exception to the mandate of the legislature is the|enactment of laws|oversight of the executive|representation of the citizenry|conduct of external relations": "The legislature's mandate covers law-making (A), oversight of the executive through questions, investigations and budget approval (B), and representing the people of their constituencies (C). Conducting external relations, meaning negotiating treaties, appointing ambassadors and dealing with other states, is the job of the executive: the President with the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. So the exception is the conduct of external relations: D. The trap: the legislature does have a say in foreign affairs (the Senate confirms ambassadors, and treaties need domestication by the National Assembly under section 12), but this is oversight, not conduct. Memory tip: the President conducts; the legislature checks.",
 "|One of the obligations of a citizen is|obedience to the laws of the land|active participation in partisan politics|sticking to his beliefs|loyalty to the ruling party": "Citizenship carries rights (to vote, to free speech, to fair hearing) and obligations. The obligations of a Nigerian citizen include obeying the laws of the land, paying taxes, respecting the national symbols, defending the country and helping the police; section 24 of the 1999 Constitution lists such duties. So the obligation here is obedience to the laws of the land: A. The trap: active partisan politics (B) is a RIGHT, which a citizen may or may not use; loyalty is owed to the nation, not to the ruling party (D). Memory tip: rights you MAY use; duties you MUST perform.",
 "|One of the objectives of a multiparty system is to|prevent rigging|provide alternatives|avoid ethnicity|eliminate corruption": "A multiparty system has three or more parties able to compete for power, as in Nigeria since 1999. Its main purpose is to give voters real alternatives: a choice between different candidates, policies and programmes, and the chance to replace a failing government peacefully: B. The trap: preventing rigging (A) and eliminating corruption (D) depend on honest electoral bodies and strong institutions, not on the number of parties; multiparty systems can even encourage ethnic parties (C), as in Nigeria's First Republic. Memory tip: multi-party = multiple choice for voters.",
 "|A constitution formulated and imposed on a country without popular participation is said to be created by|fiat|directives|command|decree": "Constitutions can be made with popular participation, through a constituent assembly, a referendum or an elected legislature, or without it. A constitution handed down by a ruler, a colonial power or a military regime, with no part played by the people, is an imposed constitution, made by command: C. This question has two right answers: \"fiat\" (A) means exactly that, an authoritative command, so \"imposed by fiat\" is also correct. The trap: decree (D) is the legal instrument a military regime uses, and directives (B) are administrative instructions; the idea tested is imposition by command. Memory tip: no assembly, no referendum = imposed by command.",
 "|In a parliamentary system, the term shadow cabinet is often used to refer to the|back-benchers in the house|deputy prime ministers and assistant ministers|rebellious members of the ruling party|portfolio designates of the party in opposition": "In a parliamentary system such as Britain's, the main opposition party forms a shadow cabinet: senior opposition MPs are each given a portfolio, for example a shadow Foreign Secretary or shadow Chancellor, to watch and criticise the matching minister and present alternative policies. They are a government-in-waiting, ready to take those posts if the opposition wins: D. The trap: back-benchers (A) are ordinary MPs without ministerial or shadow posts; deputy and assistant ministers (B) belong to the actual government. Memory tip: the shadow follows the minister everywhere.",
 "|Which of the following made the earliest contact with the Nigerian society?|The Portuguese|The Germans|The British|The French": "The Portuguese were the first Europeans to reach the Nigerian coast. Their sailors arrived at the Benin kingdom in the 1470s and 1480s (João Afonso de Aveiro visited about 1486), trading in pepper, ivory, cloth and later slaves, and sending missionaries. The British came much later as major traders, and only took Lagos in 1861: A. The trap: the British (C) are the obvious answer because they colonised Nigeria, but colonisers were not the first visitors. The Germans (B) ruled neighbouring Cameroon, and the French (D) surrounded Nigeria. Memory tip: Portugal first (1400s), Britain last (1861 onward).",
 "|The adoption of a federal system of government in Nigeria under colonial rule was necessitated mainly by|geographical proximity|administrative convenience|cultural diversity|vast economic resources": "Nigeria is home to over 250 ethnic groups with different languages, religions and traditions, especially the Hausa-Fulani in the North, the Yoruba in the West and the Igbo in the East. To hold them together while letting each region keep its identity and manage its own affairs, the colonial rulers moved from regionalism (Richards, 1946) to a true federation under the Lyttelton Constitution of 1954. So federalism was necessitated mainly by cultural diversity: C. The trap: administrative convenience (B) explains the earlier division into regions, not federalism; vast resources (D) and proximity (A) do not require power-sharing. Memory tip: many peoples, one country: federalism.",
 "|Public corporations are established mainly to|offer free services|make profit for government|provide jobs for the unemployed|provide services at subsidized rates": "Public corporations are bodies created by law and owned by government to run essential services, such as the former NEPA (electricity), the Nigerian Railway Corporation and water boards. Their main purpose is to provide these services to the public at affordable, often subsidised, rates, especially where private firms would not invest or would charge too much: D. The trap: making profit (B) is not their primary aim, though they are expected to be efficient; they do not give free services (A). Memory tip: public corporation = public service first, profit second.",
 "|Indirect rule as a British colonial policy was first introduced in|India|Uganda|Ghana|Nigeria": "Indirect rule means governing through existing local rulers and institutions. Britain first used it in India, where hundreds of princely states such as Hyderabad were left under their own rulers, supervised by British residents, from the 18th and 19th centuries. Lord Lugard, who knew this practice, applied it systematically in Northern Nigeria after 1900 and described it in \"The Dual Mandate\" (1922), so Nigeria became its best-known example: A. The trap: Nigeria (D) is where indirect rule was made famous, not where it was first introduced; some textbooks blur this. Memory tip: India first; Lugard made it famous in Nigeria.",
 "|Following the 1976 Local Government Reforms in Nigeria, the local governments were empowered to|promulgate edicts|promulgate and enforce bye-laws|appoint and dismiss chairmen|appoint and dismiss directors": "The 1976 Local Government Reform, under the Murtala/Obasanjo regime, created a uniform, single-tier system across the country and recognised local government as the third tier of government. Local governments received statutory grants and were empowered to make and enforce bye-laws, rules on local matters such as markets, motor parks and sanitation: B. The trap: edicts (A) were laws made by state military governors, not local councils; chairmen (C) are elected, not appointed and dismissed by the council. Memory tip: federal Acts, state Laws (edicts under the military), local Bye-laws.",
 "|In the Nigerian Constitution, the powers to create local governments lie with the|state governors|National Assembly|Presidency|state houses of assembly": "Under section 8(3) of the 1999 Constitution, a new local government area is created by a law of the State House of Assembly. The request needs two-thirds support from the area's members in the House of Assembly and its local councils, approval by two-thirds of the area's people in a referendum, and finally a two-thirds resolution of the House of Assembly. Because the 774 LGAs are listed in the constitution, the National Assembly must then make a consequential amendment for the new LGA to be fully recognised: D. The trap: the National Assembly (B) has only that final step; governors (A) cannot create LGAs on their own. The new LGAs Lagos State created in 2002 stayed in dispute because the National Assembly never made that final amendment. Memory tip: LGAs are born in the State House.",
 "|The pre-colonial Igbo political system was largely|republican|dictatorial|monarchical|authoritarian": "Most pre-colonial Igbo communities had no kings. Power was spread among the family heads (holders of the Ofo staff), the council of elders, age grades, title holders such as the Ozo, and the village assembly (Oha-na-eze), where adult males could speak and decisions were reached by consensus. Because power was decentralised and the people took part, the system is called republican, or acephalous (headless): A. The trap: monarchical (C) describes the Hausa-Fulani emirates and the Yoruba kingdoms, not the Igbo, although a few places such as Onitsha had kings (Obi). Memory tip: Igbo enwe eze: \"the Igbo have no king\".",
 "|Which of the following states were created in 1996?|Akwa Ibom and Kogi|Gombe and Zamfara|Adamawa and Ekiti|Ebonyi and Osun": "On 1 October 1996 General Sani Abacha created six new states, raising the number from 30 to 36: Bayelsa, Ebonyi, Ekiti, Gombe, Nasarawa and Zamfara. Gombe and Zamfara are both on the list: B. The trap: each other option mixes years. Akwa Ibom (A) was created in 1987 and Kogi in 1991. Adamawa (C) dates from 1991, though Ekiti is from 1996. Ebonyi (D) is from 1996, but Osun from 1991. Nigeria still has 36 states today. Memory tip: 1996 = \"BEE-GNZ\": Bayelsa, Ebonyi, Ekiti, Gombe, Nasarawa, Zamfara.",
 "|Acculturation in colonial administration is associated with the|French policy of assimilation|British policy of direct rule|French policy of association|British indirect rule system": "Acculturation means absorbing another people's culture. France's policy of assimilation, used in places such as Senegal's Four Communes, aimed to turn Africans into \"black Frenchmen\" who spoke French, followed French law and culture and could become French citizens. So acculturation goes with the French policy of assimilation: A. The trap: association (C) was France's later policy (from the early twentieth century), which accepted that Africans would keep their own cultures; British indirect rule (D) also preserved local culture and institutions. Memory tip: assimilate = absorb French culture; associate = keep your own.",
 "|Military disengagement from politics is always preceded by|the retirement of military personnel|the making of a new constitution|an increase in workers' salaries|the establishment of more universities": "Before soldiers hand power back to civilians, they set up a transition programme, and a key step is a new constitution for the coming civilian government. In Nigeria, the Murtala/Obasanjo regime produced the 1979 Constitution before handing over to Shagari in October 1979, and the Abubakar regime produced the 1999 Constitution before handing over to Obasanjo in May 1999: B. The trap: retiring officers (A) may happen afterwards, and salary increases (C) or new universities (D) are unrelated to handing over. Memory tip: new constitution first, then the soldiers go back to barracks.",
 "|The principle of federal character was first enunciated in the|1989 Constitution|1963 Constitution|1999 Constitution|1979 Constitution": "Federal character means that the composition of the government and its agencies, and appointments to them, should reflect the diversity of Nigeria, so that no state or ethnic group dominates. The principle was first written into the 1979 Constitution, in section 14(3), following the work of the Constitution Drafting Committee (1975–76). It was kept in the 1989 and 1999 Constitutions, and the Federal Character Commission was set up in 1996: D. The trap: the 1999 Constitution (C) contains the principle and the Commission, but it did not originate it; the 1963 Constitution (B) had no such clause. Memory tip: federal character is born in '79.",
 "|The final court of appeal in Nigeria during the colonial era was the|privy council|high court|supreme court|appeal court": "During colonial rule, appeals from Nigerian courts could go finally to the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council in London, made up of senior British judges. This continued after independence until 1963, when Nigeria became a republic and the Supreme Court of Nigeria became the final court of appeal: A. The trap: a supreme court (C) existed in colonial Nigeria, but appeals from it could still go to the Privy Council, so it was not final. Memory tip: colonial justice ended in London; republican justice ends in Abuja.",
 "|The Native Authority system of local administration was introduced by the British in Nigeria to|introduce Western education|ensure the administration of justice|empower traditional rulers|facilitate tax collection": "The Native Authority system grew out of indirect rule, which Lugard introduced in Northern Nigeria and extended to the South after amalgamation in 1914. Britain had few officials and little money, so it governed through traditional rulers. A central purpose was to collect taxes cheaply through the chiefs and native treasuries, which funded local administration: D. The trap: empowering traditional rulers (C) was the METHOD used, not the purpose; the attempt to impose direct taxation through warrant chiefs led to the Aba Women's Riot of 1929. Memory tip: native authority = chiefs as tax collectors for the colonial state.",
 "|The major objective of the manifesto of the People's Redemption Party in the Second Republic was|housing for all|welfare of the nation|emancipation of the masses|free education": "The People's Redemption Party (PRP) of the Second Republic (1979–1983) was led by Mallam Aminu Kano, who had earlier founded NEPU (1950) to fight for the talakawa, the common people of the North, against the aristocracy. Its manifesto aimed at the emancipation of the masses from social, political and economic oppression: C. The trap: free education (D) was the famous programme of Awolowo's UPN, and \"housing for all\" (A) and welfare (B) were slogans of other parties such as Shagari's NPN. Memory tip: PRP = People's Redemption = redeem the poor masses.",
 "|The privatization of public corporations in Nigeria is undertaken by the|Bureau of Public Enterprises|Nigerian Stock Exchange|Federal Civil Service Commission|Federal Executive Council": "The Bureau of Public Enterprises (BPE), set up in 1993 to replace the Technical Committee on Privatisation and Commercialisation (1988) and re-established by the Public Enterprises Act of 1999, is the secretariat of the National Council on Privatisation, chaired by the Vice-President. It prepares and carries out the sale of government-owned enterprises such as NITEL and the power companies: A. The trap: the Nigerian Stock Exchange (B) is only a market where some shares are sold; the Federal Executive Council (D) makes broad policy but does not carry out privatisation. Memory tip: BPE = Bureau that sells Public Enterprises.",
 "|The main goal of the NNDP was to|agitate for Nigeria's independence|achieve franchise for Nigerians|secure limited autonomy for the natives|secure independence for Nigeria": "The Nigerian National Democratic Party was founded by Herbert Macaulay in 1923 to contest the three Lagos seats created by the Clifford Constitution (1922), and it won them all until 1938. Its aims were self-government for Lagos, more African participation in government, and better education and economic chances for Africans, all within British rule. That is limited autonomy for the natives: C. The trap: A and D both say independence, which the NNDP never demanded; independence demands came from the NCNC and others in the 1940s and 1950s. The NNDP was also Lagos-based, not national. Memory tip: NNDP = Lagos first, not independence first.",
 "|The Structural Adjustment Programme was introduced by the|Babangida regime|Abacha regime|Murtala/Obasanjo regime|Buhari regime": "The Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP) was introduced in July 1986 by the military regime of General Ibrahim Babangida (1985–1993), after Nigeria rejected an IMF loan in a public debate. Its measures included devaluing the naira through a foreign exchange market, removing subsidies, deregulating the economy, privatising public enterprises and cutting imports: A. The trap: the Buhari regime (D, 1984–85) used austerity and counter-trade but refused IMF conditions; Abacha (B) came later, in 1993. Memory tip: IBB = I Brought Belt-tightening (SAP 1986).",
 "|The Ogboni cult in the pre-colonial Yoruba political system played the role of the|judiciary|executive|police|legislature": "In the old Oyo Empire, power was balanced between the Alaafin (king) and the Oyo Mesi (seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun). The Ogboni, a secret society of elders and earth-cult priests, stood between them: it judged serious cases, especially those involving bloodshed, settled disputes between the Alaafin and the Oyo Mesi, and checked the power of the Oyo Mesi. So it played the role of the judiciary: A. The trap: the legislature (D) is closer to the Oyo Mesi, and the executive (B) to the Alaafin. Memory tip: Alaafin rules, Oyo Mesi checks, Ogboni judges. (The paper printed option C as \"policy\"; \"police\" is meant.)",
 "|The head of the electoral body that conducted the June 12, 1993 election was|Humphrey Nwosu|Ovie-Whiskey|Eme Awa|Dagogo-Jack": "Professor Humphrey Nwosu was chairman of the National Electoral Commission (NEC) under the Babangida regime. NEC conducted the presidential election of 12 June 1993, widely regarded as Nigeria's freest; it was won by M.K.O. Abiola but annulled by Babangida. INEC has run elections since 1998: A. The trap: Justice Victor Ovie-Whiskey (B) chaired FEDECO for the 1979 and 1983 elections; Professor Eme Awa (C) was the first NEC chairman (1987–89), before Nwosu; Professor Dagogo-Jack (D) chaired NECON for the Abacha transition. Memory tip: June 12 = Nwosu's NEC; 1979/83 = Ovie-Whiskey's FEDECO.",
 "|Deregulation as an economic policy borders on|the removal of subsidies on public utilities|the removal of restrictions and extension of competition|privatization|commercialization": "Deregulation means removing government controls and restrictions, such as fixed prices, licences and monopolies, from an industry, so that private firms can enter and compete freely. Nigeria deregulated telecommunications (GSM licences, 2001) and the downstream oil sector this way: B. The trap: removing subsidies (A) often goes along with deregulation, especially for petrol, but it is a consequence, not the meaning; privatization (C) is selling government enterprises to private owners; commercialization (D) is making a public enterprise run on commercial, profit-seeking lines while it stays public. Memory tip: deregulate = de-restrict = let competitors in.",
 "|Ethnic politics in Nigeria can be traced to the|Macpherson Constitution|Lyttelton Constitution|Clifford Constitution|Richards Constitution": "The Richards Constitution (1946) divided Nigeria into three regions, North, West and East, each with its own House of Assembly, and each dominated by one major group: the Hausa-Fulani, the Yoruba and the Igbo. This regionalism made politics a contest between regions and their dominant groups, so it is usually said to have laid the foundation of ethnic politics: D. The trap: the Macpherson Constitution (A, 1951) is also a strong candidate, because its regional elections led to ethnic-based parties such as the Action Group and the NPC, so the question is open to argument. Memory tip: Richards drew the regions; Macpherson filled them with ethnic parties.",
 "|A member of the Commonwealth that was not colonized by Britain is|Malaysia|Uganda|Mozambique|Jamaica": "The Commonwealth grew out of the British Empire, so almost all members are former British colonies. Mozambique was ruled by Portugal until 1975, yet it joined in 1995 because its neighbours were all members and it backed their struggle against apartheid. It was the first member never colonised by Britain: C. Today Rwanda (2009), Gabon and Togo (2022) have joined the same way. The trap: Malaysia (A), Uganda (B) and Jamaica (D) were all British colonies, so they are \"normal\" members. Memory tip: Mozambique = the Portuguese-speaking odd one out in the Commonwealth.",
 "|The Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries came into existence at a conference held in|Tripoli|Caracas|Riyadh|Baghdad": "OPEC (Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries) was founded at the Baghdad Conference, 10–14 September 1960, by five states: Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela. Its aim was to coordinate oil policy and get fairer prices from the big Western oil companies. Nigeria joined in 1971. So the founding conference was in Baghdad: D. The trap: Caracas (B) is tempting because Venezuela's minister Pérez Alfonzo was a co-founder, but the meeting was in Iraq; OPEC's headquarters moved to Vienna in 1965. Memory tip: OPEC was \"Born in Baghdad, 1960.\"",
 "|ECOWAS main achievement in recent years has been in the area of|infrastructural development|cultural integration|security and defence|political unity": "ECOWAS was created by the Treaty of Lagos (May 1975) mainly for economic integration, but its most visible success has been in security. Its ceasefire monitoring group, ECOMOG, entered Liberia in August 1990, then acted in Sierra Leone (1997–98) and Guinea-Bissau (1998), and in 1999 ECOWAS adopted a Mechanism for conflict prevention and peacekeeping. So its main recent achievement is security and defence: C. The trap: political unity (D) is a distant goal, not an achievement; members still quarrel and coups have occurred. Memory tip: ECOWAS = ECOMOG, the soldiers it sent to keep the peace.",
 "|In which of the following countries has the UN carried out peacekeeping operations?|Cyprus, Singapore and South Africa|Lebanon, the Congo and Mexico|Cyprus, Somalia and Yugoslavia|Nigeria, Botswana and the Congo": "UN peacekeeping places neutral, lightly armed forces between warring parties. Real missions include Cyprus (UNFICYP, from 1964 and still there), Somalia (UNOSOM, 1992–1995) and the former Yugoslavia (UNPROFOR, 1992–1995). All three countries in C had UN missions: C. The trap: B looks right because the UN did serve in Lebanon (UNIFIL) and the Congo (ONUC, 1960–64), but never in Mexico; A and D each include countries with no peacekeeping mission (Singapore, South Africa, Nigeria, Botswana). Memory tip: one wrong country spoils the whole option, so check every name.",
 "|Which of the following was the main organ of the defunct OAU?|The Liberation Committee|The Council of Ministers|The Commission for Mediation, Conciliation and Arbitration|The Assembly of Heads of State and Government": "The Organisation of African Unity (founded 25 May 1963, Addis Ababa) had four main organs: the Assembly of Heads of State and Government, the Council of Ministers, the General Secretariat, and the Commission of Mediation, Conciliation and Arbitration. The Assembly was the supreme organ: it met yearly and took the key decisions: D. In 2002 the OAU was replaced by the African Union, whose Assembly is still the top organ. The trap: the Council of Ministers (B) only prepared the Assembly's agenda; the Liberation Committee (A) was a special committee for freedom movements. Memory tip: the top organ is where the top people (Heads of State) sit.",
 "|A key characteristic of the civil service is|transparency|patriotism|merit system|quota system": "The civil service is the permanent body of officials who run government ministries. Its main features are permanence, neutrality (political impartiality), anonymity, and recruitment and promotion on merit, through examinations and qualifications rather than favour. So the key characteristic here is the merit system: C. The trap: the quota system (D) does exist in Nigeria under the federal character principle, but it is a modification of merit, not a defining feature of civil services everywhere. Patriotism and transparency are good values for anyone, not special features. Memory tip: civil service = \"PANM\": Permanence, Anonymity, Neutrality, Merit.",
 "|Democracy is a perverted form of which of the following?|Feudalism|Republicanism|Monarchy|Oligarchy": "Aristotle classified governments by who rules and for whom. Rule by one for all is monarchy (perverted: tyranny); by the few for all is aristocracy (perverted: oligarchy); by the many for all is polity, often translated \"republic\" or constitutional government (perverted: democracy, which he saw as mob rule of the poor). So democracy is the perverted form of republic/polity: B. The paper's wording was reversed, so the question is shown corrected. The trap: oligarchy (D) is a perversion, but of aristocracy, not of democracy. Memory tip: Aristotle's pairs: Monarchy–Tyranny, Aristocracy–Oligarchy, Polity–Democracy.",
 "|An example of a state with an unwritten constitution is|Canada|India|Britain|France": "A constitution is unwritten when it is not collected in one single document. Britain's rests on Acts of Parliament (such as Magna Carta 1215 and the Bill of Rights 1689), judicial decisions, conventions and works of authority. It is better called \"uncodified\", because parts of it are written. So the example is Britain: C. The trap: Canada (A) shares Britain's parliamentary traditions, but it has a written Constitution Act (1867, patriated 1982); India and France also have written constitutions. Memory tip: the only big example JAMB uses for unwritten is Britain (with Israel and New Zealand).",
 "|A characteristic of a fascist state is the existence of a|zero-party system|two-party system|one-party system|multi-party system": "Fascism was the system of Mussolini's Italy (from 1922) and, in its Nazi form, Hitler's Germany. It glorifies the nation and the leader, uses propaganda and force, and allows only one party (the Fascist Party, the Nazi Party), banning all others. So a fascist state has a one-party system: C. The trap: a zero-party system (A) is a state with no parties at all, such as military regimes that ban politics or traditional monarchies; fascists ruled through their party. Memory tip: Fascism = one Leader, one Party, one Nation.",
 "|The right that enables citizens to express their electoral preferences is|franchise|suffrage|coat-tails|referendum": "Franchise is the right to vote in elections, the way citizens express their electoral choices: A. \"Suffrage\" (B) means exactly the same thing, as in \"universal adult suffrage\", so it is accepted too. Both words come from the struggle to extend the vote to all adults, including women (Nigerian women in the North voted only from 1976–79). The trap: a referendum (D) is a vote on a question or issue, not the right itself; \"coat-tails\" (C) is when a strong candidate pulls weaker candidates of his party into office. Memory tip: franchise = suffrage = right to vote.",
 "|Membership of a society is|reserved|voluntary|mandatory|honorary": "A society or association, such as a club, trade union, church or professional body, is formed by people with shared interests, and people join or leave it freely. Membership is therefore voluntary: B. This is a key difference from the state: you belong to a state by birth or naturalisation, membership is compulsory, and you must obey its laws. The trap: mandatory (C) describes membership of the state, not of a society. Memory tip: State = compulsory; Society/association = choice.",
 "|Equality before the law is a component of|separation of powers|checks and balances|the rule of law|constitutional law": "The rule of law, as explained by A.V. Dicey (1885), means three things: the supremacy of law (no one is punished except for a breach of law proved in court), equality before the law (rich and poor, rulers and ruled, are subject to the same laws and courts), and rights protected by the ordinary courts. So equality before the law is part of the rule of law: C. The trap: separation of powers (A) and checks and balances (B) are about dividing power among the three arms, not about how citizens are treated in court. Memory tip: Dicey's rule of law = \"Supremacy, Equality, Rights\".",
 "|An example of promotional pressure group is the|Supreme Council for Islamic Affairs|Nigerian Medical Association|Civil Liberties Organization|Manufacturers Association of Nigeria": "Pressure groups are of two main kinds. Interest (protective) groups defend their own members, such as the NMA (doctors) and MAN (manufacturers). Promotional (cause) groups fight for a cause that benefits the wider public, such as human rights or the environment. The Civil Liberties Organisation, founded in Lagos in 1987, campaigns for human rights for all: C. The trap: the Nigerian Medical Association (B) does speak on health, but its main job is protecting doctors' interests. Memory tip: Promotional = Public cause; Protective = members' Pockets.",
 "|An example of a country that operates a bicameral legislature is|Nigeria|Sierra Leone|Kenya|Libya": "A bicameral legislature has two chambers. Nigeria's National Assembly has the Senate (109 members) and the House of Representatives (360), as in the USA: A. In 2008 Sierra Leone, Kenya and Libya each had a single chamber. That has partly changed: Kenya's 2010 constitution created a Senate, first elected in 2013, so Kenya is now bicameral too. The trap: Kenya (C) is tempting today, but it was unicameral when this paper was set. Memory tip: \"bi\" = two, as in Nigeria's Senate + House of Representatives.",
 "|An institution that transforms a myriad of demands into a more manageable proposal is the|executive|legislature|government|political party": "Society produces countless demands. Political scientists (Gabriel Almond) call the process of combining them into a few clear, manageable policy alternatives \"interest aggregation\", and it is a main function of political parties: they bring many groups' wishes together into one manifesto that voters can choose: D. This is a debatable point: government (C) does convert demands into final decisions and policies, but \"manageable proposals\" describes what parties offer before power is won. The trap is C. Memory tip: pressure groups articulate interests; parties aggregate them; government decides.",
 "|Government exercises political power on behalf of the|elite|citizens|party caucus|vocal few": "In a modern state, government is the agent of the state that exercises political power. In a democracy that power comes from the people and is held in trust for them; Abraham Lincoln called democracy \"government of the people, by the people, for the people\". So government acts on behalf of the citizens: B. The trap: the elite (A) and the \"vocal few\" (D) may in practice influence government, but in principle government serves all citizens, not a section. Memory tip: government is the people's trustee.",
 "|A major characteristic of a confederal state is that|there is supremacy of the constitution|the citizens often owe allegiance to the centre|political stability is usually low|the centre overrides the regions": "A confederation is a loose union of sovereign states that keep most powers and can leave at will; the centre is weak and depends on them. Examples: the USA under the Articles of Confederation (1781–89), and the Senegambia Confederation (1982–89), which collapsed. Because members can withdraw or ignore the centre, political stability is usually low: C. The trap: supremacy of the constitution (A) and the centre overriding the regions (D) describe federal and unitary states. Memory tip: Confederation = weak centre, strong members, shaky union.",
 "|The citizenship of a country can be acquired by an alien through|habitation|assimilation|association|naturalization": "Citizenship can be acquired by birth, by registration (for example, a foreign woman who marries a Nigerian man), by naturalisation, or by honour. An alien (a foreigner) usually becomes a citizen by naturalisation: he applies after a long period of lawful residence (15 years under Nigeria's 1999 Constitution), shows good character and takes the oath of allegiance: D. The trap: assimilation (B) is a French colonial policy of turning Africans into \"Frenchmen\", not a legal way to gain citizenship. Memory tip: Naturalisation = Non-native becomes National.",
 "|An advantage of delegated legislation is|supremacy|convenience|effectiveness|efficiency": "Delegated legislation is law made by bodies other than parliament (ministers, local councils, agencies) under powers given by an Act. Its advantages: it saves parliament's time, allows experts to fill in technical detail, and permits quick action in emergencies. In short it makes law-making efficient: D. The trap: supremacy (A) is the opposite, since delegated rules are subordinate to the parent Act and can be struck down if they exceed it; convenience (B) is close but less precise. Memory tip: Delegated legislation = Detailed rules made Efficiently by experts.",
 "|A system of government that promotes the rights and freedom of the citizens is called|oligarchy|plutocracy|autocracy|democracy": "Democracy, from Greek demos (people) and kratos (rule), is government by the people, directly or through elected representatives. It rests on majority rule, regular free elections and the protection of rights such as free speech, press and association. So the system that promotes citizens' rights and freedom is democracy: D. The trap: oligarchy (A) is rule by a few, plutocracy (B) rule by the rich, and autocracy (C) rule by one person with unlimited power; all limit freedom. Memory tip: Demo-cracy = the people's power.",
 "|In the presidential system of government, the leader is basically the head of the state and|government|the police|the military|the judiciary": "In a presidential system (USA; Nigeria since 1979) the president is elected for a fixed term and is both head of state, the ceremonial symbol of the nation, and head of government, the chief executive. So the leader is head of state and government: A. The trap: the president is also commander-in-chief of the armed forces (C), but that is one of his executive powers, not a separate \"headship\" in the textbook sense; the judiciary (D) is independent of him. Memory tip: in a parliamentary system the job is split (Queen/King + Prime Minister); in a presidential system one person wears both hats.",
 "|An economic system that encourages even distribution of wealth in the society is|liberalism|socialism|mercantilism|capitalism": "Socialism holds that the main means of production (land, factories, banks) should be owned or controlled by the community or state, so that wealth is shared fairly and the gap between rich and poor narrows. Its principle is \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his work\". So the system that encourages even distribution of wealth is socialism: B. The trap: capitalism (D) and liberalism (A) favour private ownership and the free market, which tend to produce uneven wealth. Memory tip: Socialism = Sharing.",
 "|An attribute that makes a state to be recognized as independent is|government|population|sovereignty|territory": "A state has four elements: population, territory, government and sovereignty. Many groups have the first three (a region or a colony), but only sovereignty, supreme authority inside and independence from outside control, makes a state independent and recognised by others. So the attribute is sovereignty: C. The trap: government (A) is necessary, but colonial Nigeria had a government before 1960 without being independent. Memory tip: no sovereignty, no independence; Nigeria gained it on 1 October 1960.",
 "|The act of denying an eligible person the right to vote in an election is|gerrymandering|disenfranchisement|filibustering|restricted franchise": "Disenfranchisement is denying or taking away a qualified person's right to vote, for example by unfair registration, intimidation or laws that exclude certain groups (as Northern Nigerian women were excluded until 1976). So the act described is disenfranchisement: B. The trap: restricted franchise (D) is a legal limit on who may vote (by sex, property or education), not the act of denying an eligible voter; gerrymandering (A) is manipulating constituency boundaries; filibustering (C) is long speeches to delay a bill. Memory tip: dis-enfranchise = take away the franchise.",
 "|In the monarchical system of government, sovereignty is vested in the|people|royalty|legislature|military": "In a monarchy the head of state is a king, queen or emperor who inherits the throne. In an absolute monarchy (old France, Saudi Arabia today) supreme authority lies with the royal ruler. So sovereignty is vested in royalty: B. The trap: the people (A) hold sovereignty in a democracy or republic; in constitutional monarchies like Britain real power has passed to parliament (C), but the classic textbook answer for \"monarchical system\" is the crown. Memory tip: Monarchy = \"mono-arche\", rule by one royal.",
 "|One merit of a written constitution is its|legibility|total flexibility|rigidity|ease of reference": "A written constitution is a single document setting out the structure of government and citizens' rights (Nigeria 1999, USA 1787). Its merits: it is clear, certain and easy to refer to, so courts, officials and citizens can quickly check what the law says: D. The trap: rigidity (C) is usually counted as a demerit, since it makes amendment slow, and \"total flexibility\" (B) belongs to unwritten constitutions. Legibility (A) only means \"readable handwriting\". Memory tip: Written = easy to look up.",
 "|A human community that is usually cohesive and homogeneous is a|state|kinship|nation|clan": "A nation is a group of people bound by common descent, language, culture, history and a sense of belonging together, such as the Hausa, Yoruba or Igbo. These shared ties make it cohesive and homogeneous: C. A state, by contrast, can contain many nations (Nigeria has over 250 ethnic groups), so it is often diverse, not homogeneous. The trap: state (A) is wrong because it is a legal-political unit defined by territory, government and sovereignty, not by sameness. Kinship and clan are smaller family-based groups. Memory tip: Nation = shared culture; State = shared government.",
 "|In the process of implementing laws, the executive sometimes performs|judicial function|bureaucratic function|oversight function|legislative function": "Under separation of powers each arm has its main job, but in practice they overlap. The executive implements laws, yet it sometimes acts judicially: administrative tribunals settle disputes, and the President or Governor exercises the prerogative of mercy (pardon): A. It also legislates, through delegated legislation and executive orders (and decrees under military rule), so D is accepted too; both are standard examples of overlapping functions. The trap: \"bureaucratic function\" (B) is just ordinary implementation, not something it does \"sometimes\". Memory tip: the executive also makes rules (quasi-legislative) and settles disputes (quasi-judicial).",
 "|Which of the following justifies the Afro-centric foreign policy of Nigeria?|Her location in the West African sub-region|Her colonization by a foreign power|Her physical size, population and resource endowments|Her ability to contribute to peace keeping missions": "Since independence (1960) Nigeria has made Africa the centrepiece of her foreign policy: fighting colonialism and apartheid, leading ECOWAS and peacekeeping. This role is justified by her physical size, Africa's largest population and large resource endowments, especially oil, which give her the means and responsibility to lead: C. The trap: location in West Africa (A) explains her regional ties but many small states share that location; peacekeeping (D) is a result of Afrocentrism, not its justification. Memory tip: the \"Giant of Africa\" plays an Africa-first foreign policy because it is a giant.",
 "|A virile foreign policy is one characterized by|a vibrant press, vibrant public opinion and a dynamic civil society|propaganda, espionage and economic embargoes|a stable polity, buoyant economy and visionary leadership|the nature and dynamics of the international system": "A virile (strong, vigorous) foreign policy depends on the country's power at home. A state can act boldly abroad only if it has political stability, a buoyant economy to fund its goals and visionary leadership to set clear aims. Murtala Mohammed's firm stand on Angola in 1975 rested on oil wealth and decisive leadership. So a virile foreign policy is marked by a stable polity, buoyant economy and visionary leadership: C. The trap: propaganda, espionage and embargoes (B) are tools of foreign policy, not its foundation. Memory tip: foreign policy begins at home.",
 "|Nigeria hosted the Commonwealth conference which eventually led to the independence of|Mozambique|Malawi|Namibia|Zimbabwe": "In November 1965 Ian Smith's white minority government in Rhodesia declared independence illegally (UDI). In January 1966 Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa hosted a special Commonwealth Prime Ministers' Conference in Lagos to deal with it, the first Commonwealth heads meeting held outside London. The pressure that followed, together with the liberation war and the 1979 Lancaster House talks, led to black-majority rule and Zimbabwe's independence on 18 April 1980: D. The trap: Mozambique (A) won independence from Portugal in 1975, not through the Commonwealth. Memory tip: Lagos 1966 → Rhodesia → Zimbabwe 1980.",
 "|Multilateralism in Nigeria's foreign policy entails her|stand on Africa as the centrepiece of her policy|non-aligned posture in the international system|quest for a permanent seat in the UN security council|membership of international organizations": "Multilateralism means conducting foreign relations through organisations with many members, rather than only one-to-one (bilateral) dealings. Nigeria practises it through membership of the UN (joined 7 October 1960), the OAU/AU, ECOWAS, the Commonwealth, OPEC and the Non-Aligned Movement. So multilateralism entails membership of international organisations: D. The trap: non-alignment (B) is a separate principle, refusing to take sides in the Cold War between East and West blocs; Africa as centrepiece (A) is Afrocentrism. Memory tip: multi-lateral = many sides = many organisations.",
 "|The first military coup in Nigeria toppled the government of|General Yakubu Gowon|Dr. Nnamdi Azikiwe|Alhaji Abubakar Tafawa Balewa|Major General Aguiyi-Ironsi": "Nigeria's first military coup took place on 15 January 1966, led by young majors including Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu. It overthrew the First Republic government of Prime Minister Alhaji (Sir) Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, who was killed along with Ahmadu Bello and others: C. Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi (D) then took over as the first military head of state, and was himself killed in the July 1966 counter-coup that brought Yakubu Gowon (A) to power. The trap: Azikiwe (B) was the ceremonial President, not the head of government, and he was abroad at the time. Memory tip: coup 1 (Jan 1966) toppled Balewa; coup 2 (July 1966) toppled Ironsi.",
 "|Adjudication of conflict between the federal and regional governments in the first Republic was reserved for|National industrial court|Supreme Court|Federal Court of Appeal|Federal High court": "In a federation, disputes between the centre and the regions need a neutral umpire. Under the 1960 and 1963 Constitutions, the Supreme Court had original jurisdiction over disputes between the federation and a region, or between regions, so such cases went straight to it: B. The trap: the Federal Court of Appeal (C) was created only in 1976 and hears appeals; the Federal High Court (D) began in 1973 as the Federal Revenue Court; the National Industrial Court (A) handles labour disputes. Memory tip: federal vs regional disputes go straight to the top court.",
 "|The officer in charge of the administration of the palace in the Yoruba political structure is the|ba'ale|oluwo|ilari|iwefa": "In Old Oyo the Alaafin's palace was run by eunuchs (iwefa), headed by three titled officials: the Ona Efa (justice), the Otun Efa (religion) and the Osi Efa (palace affairs and finance). They were in charge of palace administration: D. The paper printed \"iwarefa\", which is the inner council of Ogboni/Osugbo chiefs, so the option is shown as \"iwefa\". The trap: the ilari (C) also lived in the palace, but they were the king's messengers and agents sent to the provinces, not its administrators; the ba'ale (A) was a village head. Memory tip: Iwefa = eunuchs who ran the palace.",
 "|Prior to 1966, the system of local government in western region was based on the three-tier structure of|native authority, division and district councils|district, local and area councils|division, district and local councils|province, district and local council": "The Western Region pioneered elected local government in Nigeria. Its Local Government Law of 1952, modelled on British councils, replaced native authorities with a three-tier system of divisional councils, district councils and local councils, with elected members and traditional rulers: C. This lasted until military rule, and the 1976 reform later made local government single-tier nationwide. The trap: native authority (A) belongs to the old indirect-rule system the 1952 law replaced, and \"province\" (D) was an administrative unit, not a council. Memory tip: West 1952 = Division, District, Local (\"DDL\").",
 "|A major shortcoming of the Lyttelton Constitution was that|it did not provide for a second chamber at the federal level|the Governor-General continued to head the government|no provision was made for the office of the prime minister|it failed to provide for the office of a premier": "The Lyttelton Constitution (1954), named after Colonial Secretary Oliver Lyttelton, made Nigeria a federation of three regions plus Lagos, and created regional premiers. But at the centre there was no prime minister; the Governor-General still presided over the Council of Ministers. The federal premiership came only in 1957, filled by Tafawa Balewa. So the major shortcoming was no provision for a prime minister: C. The trap: D is false, since Lyttelton did create premiers in the regions (Awolowo, Azikiwe, Ahmadu Bello). Memory tip: Lyttelton gave premiers to the regions but no prime minister to the centre.",
 "|The option A-4 voting system was introduced into the political process in Nigeria during the|Third Republic|fourth Republic|First Republic|Second Republic": "Option A4 was an open-ballot method introduced by the Babangida regime during the Third Republic transition (1989–1993): voters lined up openly behind the photograph or symbol of their preferred candidate and were counted. It was meant to stop ballot-box stuffing and was used in party primaries and elections, ending with the transition annulled after 12 June 1993: A. The trap: the Fourth Republic (B) began in 1999 and uses secret ballots under INEC. Memory tip: A4 = Third Republic, Babangida, the two parties SDP and NRC.",
 "|In orientation, early nationalism in Nigeria was essentially|revolutionary|reformist|reactionary|radical": "Early Nigerian nationalism (roughly 1920s–1930s), led by men such as Herbert Macaulay, whose Nigerian National Democratic Party was formed in 1923 after the Clifford Constitution, was moderate. It asked for reforms within colonial rule: more elected seats, more Africans in the civil service, better education and services. It did not yet demand independence. So it was essentially reformist: B. The trap: radical (D) describes later nationalism after 1945, such as the Zikist Movement demanding self-government now. Memory tip: early nationalists Reformed; later nationalists demanded Independence.",
 "|Which of the following in the Sokoto Caliphate performed functions similar to that of the Bashorun in Oyo Kingdom?|Waziri|Galadima|Ma'aji|Alkali": "In Oyo the Bashorun was head of the Oyo Mesi (the seven kingmakers) and acted like a prime minister, the most powerful chief after the Alaafin. In the Sokoto Caliphate the equivalent was the Waziri, the chief adviser and head of administration under the Sultan or Emir: A. The trap: the Galadima (B) administered the capital city, the Ma'aji (C) was the treasurer and the Alkali (D) was the Islamic judge. Memory tip: Waziri = Wazir = vizier, the prime minister of Muslim states.",
 "|Problems arising from population census in Nigeria are mainly caused by|public opinions of the people|unreliable census figures|political manipulation by the elite|the registration of the under-aged people": "Because population decides the number of seats and the share of federal revenue, censuses in Nigeria have been fiercely contested. The 1962 count was cancelled, the 1963 count disputed, the 1973 census annulled, and the 1991 and 2006 results questioned. The figures have been unreliable, and that is the source of the problems: B. Behind the unreliable figures lies the inflation of numbers for political gain (C), so the two are linked; the textbook answer names the unreliable figures themselves. The trap: registering under-aged people (D) affects voters' lists, not censuses. Memory tip: wrong numbers mean wrong seats and wrong money.",
 "|Which of the following formalized colonial penetration into Africa?|Paris Treaty|Treaty of Westphalia|Berlin Conference|Treaty of Versailles": "At the Berlin Conference (November 1884 to February 1885), called by German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck, European powers agreed rules for claiming African territory: \"effective occupation\" and notifying other powers. This formalised the scramble for Africa and colonial penetration: C. The trap: the Treaty of Westphalia (B, 1648) created the modern European system of sovereign states; the Treaty of Versailles (D, 1919) ended the First World War and took away Germany's colonies, such as Kamerun, but did not start colonial rule. Memory tip: Berlin 1884–85 = Africa carved up on a map.",
 "|The 1989 Nigerian Constitution provided for a|multi-party system|zero-party system|two-party system|one-party system": "The 1989 Constitution, prepared for Babangida's planned Third Republic, allowed only two political parties. When none of the associations seeking registration was approved, the government itself created the Social Democratic Party (SDP), \"a little to the left\", and the National Republican Convention (NRC), \"a little to the right\": C. The constitution never fully came into force because of the 1993 annulment. The trap: multi-party (A) describes the 1979 and 1999 constitutions. Memory tip: 1989 = two parties, SDP and NRC.",
 "|The Nigerian federalism is a|four-tier system|three-tier system|two-tier system|one-tier system": "Nigeria's federal system has three tiers of government: the federal government, the 36 state governments (plus the FCT), and the 774 local governments. The 1976 Local Government Reform first recognised local government as the third tier, and the 1979 and 1999 Constitutions entrenched it: B. The trap: classic federalism, as in the USA, has two tiers (C), federal and state, which is why students sometimes choose it, but Nigeria constitutionally adds local government. Memory tip: Nigeria = 1 + 36 + 774, three tiers.",
 "|The arm of government mainly responsible for the implementation of law in Nigeria is the|judiciary|police|legislature|executive": "Each arm of government has a main job: the legislature makes laws, the executive implements (executes) them, and the judiciary interprets them and settles disputes. In Nigeria the executive is headed by the President, with ministers, ministries and agencies carrying laws into effect: D. The trap: the police (B) do enforce laws, but they are an agency under the executive, not an arm of government. Memory tip: Legislature Legislates, Executive Executes, Judiciary Judges.",
 "|The British colonial rule in Nigeria brought about|a stagnation in social formations|the pampering of traditional rulers|a change in the mode of production|stabilization in the price of raw materials": "British rule changed Nigeria's economy and society deeply. It introduced a money economy and British currency, cash crops for export (cocoa, groundnuts, palm oil), wage labour, railways and private property in land, shifting the mode of production from subsistence and communal production towards capitalism: C. This is a debatable point: some dependency writers say colonialism stunted Africa's development, but social formations did not stagnate; new classes of workers, traders and educated elite emerged. The trap is A. Memory tip: colonialism brought cash crops, coins and classes.",
 "|A primary objective of the privatization policy in Nigeria is|probity|responsiveness|transparency|accountability": "Privatisation means selling government-owned enterprises (fully or partly) to private investors. Nigeria's programme began under the 1988 Privatisation and Commercialisation Decree and was expanded by the Public Enterprises Act of 1999 under the Bureau of Public Enterprises. Its aims include ending waste and losses, reducing the burden on government, and making managers answerable for results, that is, accountability: D. The trap: transparency (C) describes how the sale itself should be conducted, not the objective of privatising. Memory tip: private owners demand an account of every naira.",
 "|Which of the following is saddled with the conduct of general elections in Nigeria's Fourth Republic?|National Electoral Commission of Nigeria|Independent National Electoral Commission|National Electoral Commission|Federal Electoral Commission": "The Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) was established in 1998 by the Abubakar regime and has conducted every Fourth Republic general election, from 1999 to today; the 1999 Constitution (Third Schedule) provides for it: B. The trap: the Federal Electoral Commission, FEDECO (D), ran the 1979 and 1983 elections of the Second Republic; the National Electoral Commission, NEC (C), ran the Third Republic elections including 12 June 1993; NECON (A) served under Abacha. Memory tip: FEDECO = Second Republic, NEC = Third, INEC = Fourth.",
 "|Public corporations are established to|reduce government spending|boost government revenue|improve provision of social amenities|create vacancies for political appointments": "Public corporations are bodies owned by government but run like businesses, set up by law to provide essential services: water, electricity, railways, broadcasting (for example the old NEPA, Nigerian Railway Corporation and NTA). Their main purpose is to provide social amenities and services to the public at affordable cost, which private firms might not provide: C. The trap: boosting revenue (B) is not their main aim, since many run at a loss; creating jobs for politicians (D) is an abuse, not a purpose. Memory tip: public corporations serve the public first, profit second.",
 "|The political structure of Nigeria under the first military regime was|confederal|quasi-federal|federal|unitary": "After the January 1966 coup, Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi became head of state. On 24 May 1966 he issued the Unification Decree (Decree No. 34), which abolished the regions and the federal system and declared Nigeria a unitary state with a single public service: D. The decree caused fear in the North and helped spark the July 1966 counter-coup; Gowon restored federalism and in 1967 created 12 states. The trap: quasi-federal (B) describes later military regimes, which kept states but ran them under a central command. Memory tip: Ironsi's Decree 34 = Nigeria made unitary.",
 "|The Inspector General of Police is appointed by the executive on the advice of the|Nigeria Police Council|Police Service Commission|House of Representatives|Senate of the Federal Republic": "The Inspector-General of Police (IGP) heads the Nigeria Police Force. Section 215(1)(a) of the 1999 Constitution says the IGP is appointed by the President on the advice of the Nigeria Police Council, chosen from among serving members of the Force. The Council is made up of the President (chairman), the 36 state governors, the chairman of the Police Service Commission and the IGP. So the answer is A. The trap: the Police Service Commission (B) appoints, promotes and disciplines the other police officers, but not the IGP. Memory tip: the IGP comes from the COUNCIL; everyone below him comes from the COMMISSION.",
 "|A major problem associated with the Nigerian federalism is|ethnic conflicts|environmental pollution|boundary disputes|fiscal allocation": "In a federation the centre and the states each need money, so revenue must be shared. In Nigeria most revenue (oil money) is collected at the centre and divided through the Federation Account, and the sharing formula has caused endless disputes: the Phillipson (1946), Hicks-Phillipson (1951), Chick (1953), Raisman (1958), Binns (1964), Dina (1968) and Aboyade (1977) commissions, and today the RMAFC. The quarrel over derivation for oil states continues today. So the problem is fiscal allocation: D. The trap: ethnic conflicts (A) are a problem of Nigerian society generally, not a defect of the federal arrangement as such. Memory tip: in Nigerian federalism, \"who gets what money\" is the oldest fight.",
 "|The six African members of the Nigerian Council were nominated by the|Queen|district officers|natives|governors": "On amalgamation in 1914, Lord Lugard set up the Nigerian Council, a purely advisory body that met once a year and had no power to make laws. Its members were mostly British officials, plus a few unofficial members; the six African members (such as the Alaafin of Oyo and the Sultan of Sokoto) were nominated by the Governor-General, not elected. The option \"governors\" stands for the colonial governor (Governor-General): D. The trap: the \"Queen\" (A) is wrong in two ways: in 1914 Britain had a king, George V, and the Crown did not pick council members. Memory tip: before 1922 Africans were NOMINATED; the 1922 Clifford Constitution first let some be ELECTED.",
 "|In the Second Republic, the total membership of each House of Assembly was determined by the|electoral commission|political parties|constitution|electorate": "The 1979 Constitution, which created Nigeria's Second Republic (1979–1983), fixed the size of every State House of Assembly: three times the number of seats the state had in the federal House of Representatives. So a state with 10 seats in the House of Representatives had 30 Assembly members. The membership was therefore set by the constitution: C. The trap: the electoral commission (FEDECO, A) drew constituency boundaries and ran elections, but it could not change the total, which the constitution itself set. Memory tip: 1979 House of Assembly = 3 x House of Reps seats.",
 "|Privatization and commercialization programmes in Nigeria have|increased public utilities|de-emphasized government ownership|reduced government revenue|encouraged corruption": "Privatization means selling all or part of government shares in public enterprises to private investors; commercialization means making the enterprises that remain run like businesses, covering their costs or making profit without subsidies. Nigeria began this under the Structural Adjustment Programme (Decree No. 25 of 1988, with TCPC), and the Bureau of Public Enterprises later took over. The main effect is to reduce the place of government ownership in the economy: B. The trap: privatization often brings government a one-time income from share sales, so \"reduced government revenue\" (C) is not its defining effect. Memory tip: privatize = Private takes over; government steps back.",
 "|The fear of domination in the public service led to the|adoption of population as a formula for revenue allocation|establishment of the Civil Service Commission|introduction of rotational presidency|establishment of the Federal Character Commission": "Nigeria's groups feared that one region or tribe would fill the public service. The 1979 Constitution (s.14(3)) introduced the federal character principle: appointments must reflect the country's diversity so that no group dominates. To enforce it, the Abacha regime set up the Federal Character Commission in 1996, and s.153 of the 1999 Constitution keeps it. So the answer is D. The trap: the Civil Service Commission (B) handles appointments and discipline in the civil service but was not created because of fear of domination. Rotational presidency (C) is a party arrangement about the top political office, not the public service. Memory tip: FEDERAL CHARACTER = Fair Each Region's share.",
 "|In the Igbo political system, the most senior member of the council of elders is the|okpara|obi|eze|ofo": "The Igbo were acephalous: no king, with power shared among the village assembly, age grades, title holders and elders. Each lineage's eldest man was the okpara. He held the ofo, a sacred staff of authority and truth linking the living to the ancestors, and so he was the most senior member of the council of elders: A. The trap: ofo (D) is the staff the okpara holds, not a person. Obi and eze (B, C) are titles of kings found only in a few Igbo communities such as Onitsha and Nri. Memory tip: the OKpara is the OLDest man who keeps the OFO.",
 "|The immediate cause of the 1966 military coup d'etat in Nigeria was|nepotism|political instability|military ambition|corruption": "The First Republic was shaken by the 1962 Action Group crisis and emergency in the West, the disputed 1962–63 census, the 1964 federal election boycott, and the rigged 1965 Western Region election, which led to riots and arson (\"Operation Wetie\"). This breakdown of law and order, political instability, was the immediate trigger of the coup of 15 January 1966 led by Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu. So the answer is B. The trap: corruption (D) was a cause the coup plotters named, but it was a long-standing background factor; the immediate spark was the Western crisis. Memory tip: immediate cause = the last straw (the 1965 Western election violence).",
 "|The exclusive list is reserved for legislation by the|regional government|state government|local government|federal government": "A federal constitution divides legislative power into lists. The Exclusive Legislative List (originally 68 items in the 1999 Constitution) covers matters only the federal government may legislate on, such as defence, currency, foreign affairs, citizenship and the police. The Concurrent List is shared by federal and state governments, and residual matters (not on either list) belong to the states. So the exclusive list is for the federal government: D. The trap: the regional government (A) was the second tier before 1967, and it had power over residual matters, not exclusive ones. Memory tip: Exclusive = Excludes everyone but the centre.",
 "|The participation of Nigerians in the Second World War created in the nationalists the awareness that the|Africans were superior|Africans were more qualified to rule|Europeans were not superior|Europeans were less tactful in warfare": "About 100,000 Nigerians served in the British forces during the Second World War (1939–45), many in Burma with the 81st and 82nd West African Divisions. They saw European soldiers wounded, afraid, defeated and dying like anyone else. Returning ex-servicemen and the nationalists drew the lesson that Europeans were not superior beings with a natural right to rule Africa: C. The trap: A and B go too far; nationalists claimed equality and the right to self-rule, not African superiority. Memory tip: WWII broke the \"white superiority\" myth, and the Atlantic Charter (1941) gave nationalists the language of self-determination.",
 "|Which of the following mostly militates against the efficiency and productivity of public corporations in Nigeria?|Incessant interference by government|The management teams are always appointed at will|Boards are often non-professionals|Members of the management team are always at loggerheads": "Public corporations (such as NEPA, the railways and Nigeria Airways) were supposed to run like businesses at arm's length from politics. In practice governments kept interfering: appointing boards as political rewards, fixing prices below cost, forcing over-staffing and directing contracts. This incessant interference is the main reason for their poor performance, and a key argument for privatization: A. The trap: non-professional boards (C) are a real problem, but they are themselves a result of political interference, which is the root cause. Memory tip: too many political hands in the till make a public corporation ill.",
 "|The power to nominate members of the judiciary at the federal level under the 1999 Constitution is vested in the|Federal Judicial Service Commission|Supreme Court|National Judicial Council|Nigerian Bar Association": "Under the 1999 Constitution (Third Schedule), the Federal Judicial Service Commission (FJSC) advises the National Judicial Council (NJC) and supplies names, but it is the NJC that recommends (nominates) persons to the President for appointment as Chief Justice, Justices of the Supreme Court and Court of Appeal, and federal High Court judges. The President then appoints, with Senate confirmation for the top posts. So the power to nominate rests with the NJC: C. The trap: the FJSC (A) only \"advises the NJC in nominating\"; it does not make the nomination itself. Memory tip: FJSC shortlists, NJC nominates, President appoints, Senate confirms.",
 "|Prior to 1976, the local government system practised in Northern Nigeria was|chief-and-council|chief-in-council|prefectorial|council": "Under indirect rule the North was governed through native authorities. At first the emir was a Sole Native Authority with near-absolute power. Reforms in the 1950s (Native Authority Law, 1954) made him rule with a council, as \"chief-in-council\" where his word was final, or \"chief-and-council\" where he had to accept the council's decision. The typical arrangement in the North up to the 1976 reform was chief-in-council: B. The trap: chief-and-council (A) was the weaker form, used where the chief's power was more limited. Memory tip: chief-IN-council = the chief is IN charge; chief-AND-council = the council shares power.",
 "|In the segmented Tiv society, governance was usually confined to the|age grade|chiefdom|family|clan": "The Tiv of the Benue valley were a segmentary, acephalous society: they had no chiefs or central government before colonial rule. The basic unit was the family (compound or minimal lineage) under its eldest man, who settled disputes with the other elders. Larger lineages and clans came together only temporarily, for war or a serious dispute, then split up again. So governance was usually confined to the family: C. The trap: \"chiefdom\" (B) contradicts the very idea of a segmented society; the office of Tor Tiv was created by the British in 1946. Memory tip: Tiv and Igbo are stateless: power stays with family elders.",
 "|Which of the following influenced nationalist activities in Nigeria?|Indian Congress Party|Negro Diaspora Congress|Pan-African Congress|National Congress of British West Africa": "The National Congress of British West Africa (NCBWA) was founded in Accra in 1920 by J.E. Casely Hayford and others from all four British West African colonies. It demanded elected African members in the legislative councils. Its pressure led to the elective principle in the Clifford Constitution of 1922, which in turn led Herbert Macaulay to form the first Nigerian party, the NNDP (1923). So it directly influenced nationalist activity in Nigeria: D. Note: the Pan-African Congress (C), especially the 1945 Manchester Congress, also inspired Nigerian nationalists, so this question is open to argument; D is the expected answer. Memory tip: NCBWA (1920) to Clifford (1922) to NNDP (1923).",
 "|The party that did not control any state in the Second Republic was|NAP|PRP|NPP|GNPP": "Five parties contested the 1979 elections: NPN (won 7 states), UPN (5), NPP (3), GNPP (2) and PRP (2), so all of PRP, NPP and GNPP controlled states. The Nigeria Advance Party (NAP), led by lawyer Tunji Braithwaite, was registered only in 1982 for the 1983 elections, and it won no state at all: A. The trap: the PRP (B) was small, but it controlled Kano and Kaduna in 1979. Memory tip: 1979 = 5 parties, 19 states; the late-comer NAP won nothing.",
 "|'Pacification' refers to a process through which some areas in Nigeria were acquired by|military conquest|gunboat diplomacy|signing of treaties|peaceful negotiation": "\"Pacification\" was the British colonial word for bringing resistant peoples under control by force. Officials said they were \"pacifying\" an area when they sent punitive expeditions: the Ijebu expedition (1892), Benin (1897), the Aro Expedition (1901–02) and the conquest of the Sokoto Caliphate (1903). So pacification means acquisition by military conquest: A. The trap: the word sounds like \"peace\", which tempts students to choose peaceful negotiation (D); treaties (C) were a separate method, often signed before force was used. Memory tip: colonial \"pacification\" = peace imposed with the gun.",
 "|Counter-trade was introduced by the government led by|Abdulsalami Abubakar|Olusegun Obasanjo|Muhammadu Buhari|Ibrahim Babangida": "When oil prices fell in the early 1980s, Nigeria ran short of foreign exchange to import essential goods. The Buhari regime (31 December 1983 – 27 August 1985) answered with counter-trade: a barter arrangement exchanging Nigerian crude oil directly for machinery, raw materials and goods from countries such as Brazil, France and Austria, without paying cash. So the answer is C. The trap: Babangida (D), who overthrew Buhari in 1985, is linked with SAP and deregulation, not counter-trade. Memory tip: Buhari bartered: oil for goods.",
 "|A major limitation of the Commonwealth is that it lacks|the power to set formal rules to govern the affairs of member-nations|an organizational structure and a definite identity|enough funds to run its affairs|the power to compel member-nations to comply with its decisions": "The Commonwealth is a voluntary association of 56 states, most of them former British territories. Its decisions are reached by consensus and are not legally binding; it has no army, court or sanctions machinery to enforce them. The most it can do is suspend a member, as it did to Nigeria in 1995 after the execution of Ken Saro-Wiwa. Its limitation is lack of power to compel compliance: D. The trap: the Commonwealth does have a structure and identity (B): a Secretariat in London (since 1965), a Secretary-General and CHOGM summits. Memory tip: the Commonwealth can advise and suspend, but it cannot enforce.",
 "|Which of the following countries has withdrawn her membership of the ECOWAS?|Senegal|Mauritania|Guinea Bissau|Burkina Faso": "ECOWAS was founded by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975 by 15 states; Cape Verde joined in 1977. Mauritania, a founding member, withdrew in 2000, partly because it was turning towards the Arab Maghreb Union. When this paper was set in 2009, Mauritania was the only country that had left: B. The trap: Senegal and Guinea-Bissau (A, C) have always remained members. Today: Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger also left with effect from 29 January 2025, so ECOWAS now has 12 members; on this 2009 paper the answer is Mauritania. Memory tip: Mauritania was the first out (2000); the Sahel trio followed in 2025.",
 "|Which of the following is a major threat to OPEC?|Persistence of war in the Middle East|The discovery of crude oil in more countries|Antagonism by the major world powers|Development of viable alternatives to crude oil": "OPEC (founded in Baghdad, 1960; headquarters Vienna) earns its influence from the world's dependence on crude oil. Anything that makes oil less necessary weakens it: nuclear, solar and wind power, biofuels and now electric vehicles. If viable alternatives replace crude oil, OPEC loses its power, so this is the major threat: D. The trap: discovery of oil in more countries (B) increases competition, but new producers can join OPEC or still sell oil, so the market for oil survives; alternatives threaten the market itself. Memory tip: OPEC fears a world that no longer needs oil.",
 "|The AU's right to intervene in a member country in crisis portrays it as|an international organization|a regional organization|a supranational organization|a continental organization": "A supranational organisation has authority over and above its member states, so it can act inside a member even against that member's wishes. Article 4(h) of the AU Constitutive Act (2000) gives the AU the right to intervene in a member state in grave circumstances: war crimes, genocide and crimes against humanity. The OAU, by contrast, stuck to non-interference. This right portrays the AU as supranational: C. The trap: the AU is also international, regional and continental (A, B, D), but those words only describe its membership; only \"supranational\" describes power over states. Memory tip: SUPRA = above; a supranational body stands above its members' sovereignty.",
 "|The permanent membership of the UN Security Council is|ten|fifteen|five|eleven": "The UN Security Council has 15 members. Five are permanent and hold the veto: the USA, Russia (formerly the USSR), China, the United Kingdom and France, the victors of the Second World War. The other ten are elected by the General Assembly for two-year terms (there were six before 1965, when the Council grew from 11 to 15). So permanent membership is five: C. The trap: fifteen (B) is the total membership and ten (A) is the non-permanent members. Memory tip: the \"P5\" = 5 permanent; 5 + 10 = 15.",
 "|An advantage of flexible constitution is that it allows for|political awareness|constitutional stability|reduction of ambiguities|ease of amendment": "A flexible constitution can be amended by the same simple procedure as an ordinary law; Britain's largely unwritten constitution is the classic example. Its chief advantage is ease of amendment: it adapts quickly to changing circumstances or emergencies: D. The trap: constitutional stability (B) is an advantage of a rigid constitution, which needs special procedures (in Nigeria, two-thirds of each house of the National Assembly plus two-thirds of state Houses of Assembly) and so changes rarely. Memory tip: Flexible = easy to Fix; Rigid = Resists change.",
 "|Power refers to the|ability to impose one's will upon others|legal nature of a constitution|duties political parties demand of their registered members|duties an individual imposes on himself": "Power is the ability to influence others or make them act as one wishes, even against their will, by persuasion, reward, threat or force. Authority is power that is accepted as rightful (legitimate). So power refers to the ability to impose one's will upon others: A. The trap: the other options describe different things: the legal nature of a constitution (B) concerns its supremacy, and party or self-imposed duties (C, D) are obligations, not power. Memory tip: Power = can make you; Authority = has the right to; Legitimacy = people accept it.",
 "|Prorogation is the bringing of a session of a parliament to an end through|royal proclamation|final reading|guillotine|impeachment": "Under the Westminster system, a parliament meets in sessions. Prorogation ends a session without dissolving parliament: the monarch, on the Prime Minister's advice, issues a royal proclamation, and unfinished business usually lapses. Parliament then meets again in a new session opened by the King's Speech. So prorogation is by royal proclamation: A. The trap: the guillotine (C) is a motion that cuts short debate on a bill, not a session; dissolution, unlike prorogation, ends the life of parliament and leads to a general election. Memory tip: PROrogation = PROclamation pauses parliament.",
 "|The right of all eligible citizens to vote and be voted for is referred to as|gender equality|political mandate|restricted franchise|universal suffrage": "Universal adult suffrage gives every qualified adult citizen the right to vote (and, if qualified, to stand for election) without regard to sex, wealth, education or religion. In Nigeria it is set at age 18. It came gradually: women in the North could not vote until the late 1970s. So the answer is D. The trap: restricted franchise (C) is the opposite, limiting the vote by income, sex or property, as the 1922 Clifford Constitution did (men with £100 income). Memory tip: Universal = everyone qualified; Restricted = only some.",
 "|Values, beliefs and orientation of a political system are internalized through|political culture|political socialization|political development|political power": "Political culture is the set of values, beliefs and attitudes a society holds about politics. Political socialization is the process by which individuals learn and internalize that culture, through agents such as the family, school, peer groups, religious bodies, the mass media and political parties. So values are internalized through political socialization: B. The trap: political culture (A) is the content that is learnt, not the process of learning it. Memory tip: Culture is WHAT we believe; Socialization is HOW we come to believe it.",
 "|Occupational pressure groups pursue their objectives mainly by|issuing ultimatums and embarking on strikes|alienating the government|embarking on guerrilla warfare|contesting elections": "Occupational (professional or sectional) pressure groups, such as the Nigeria Labour Congress, ASUU, the Nigerian Medical Association and the NUT, exist to protect their members' interests. Their main weapons are negotiation backed by threats: issuing ultimatums and going on strike to force the government to act: A. The trap: contesting elections (D) is what political parties do; pressure groups seek to influence government, not to capture it. Guerrilla warfare (C) is illegal rebellion, not lobbying. Memory tip: parties want POWER; pressure groups want INFLUENCE (and strike to get it).",
 "|In a cabinet system, the legislature comprises the|prime minister and members of the parliament|prime minister and members of the jury|prime minister and ministers|president and ministers": "In a parliamentary (cabinet) system, as in Britain or Nigeria's First Republic, there is a fusion of powers: the prime minister and all ministers must be members of parliament. The legislature therefore comprises the prime minister and the members of parliament: A. The trap: \"prime minister and ministers\" (C) describes the cabinet (the executive), not the legislature, and \"president and ministers\" (D) is the executive in a presidential system. Juries (B) belong to the courts. Memory tip: cabinet system = the PM sits in parliament with every other MP.",
 "|The committee that scrutinizes budget proposals in the legislature is the|Rules and Business Committee|Public Accounts Committee|Finance Committee|Appropriation Committee": "In Nigeria's National Assembly, the President lays the budget (Appropriation Bill) before both houses, and the Senate and House Committees on Appropriations scrutinise it, consulting the other committees, before it is passed. So the committee is the Appropriation Committee: D. The trap: the Public Accounts Committee (B) checks how money was spent after the year, using the Auditor-General's report; Finance (C) deals with revenue and taxation; and Rules and Business (A) arranges the order of business. Memory tip: Appropriation = before the money is spent; Public Accounts = after.",
 "|Democracy is a system of government that is|constituted by the will of the majority of the citizens|imposed on the majority of the citizens|designed to favour the elite|put in place to favour the party caucus": "Democracy (Greek demos = people, kratos = rule) is government of the people, by the people and for the people (Abraham Lincoln, 1863). Rulers are chosen in free elections and govern with the consent of the majority, while respecting minority rights. So democracy is constituted by the will of the majority: A. The trap: options B, C and D describe the opposite: imposed government (dictatorship) and rule for an elite or party caucus (oligarchy). Memory tip: democracy = majority rule with minority rights.",
 "|Collective ownership of property is associated with|feudalism|communism|mercantilism|capitalism": "Communism, developed by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels (The Communist Manifesto, 1848), aims at a classless society in which the means of production (land, factories, mines) are owned collectively by the community, and goods are shared \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his needs\". So collective ownership is associated with communism: B. The trap: capitalism (D) is built on private ownership, feudalism (A) on lords owning land worked by serfs, and mercantilism (C) is a trade policy of building national wealth through exports. Memory tip: COMmunism = COMmon ownership.",
 "|The agency responsible for investigating allegations of maladministration in the civil service is the|Federal Civil Service Commission|Code of Conduct Bureau|Public Complaints Commission|Federal Character Commission": "An ombudsman is an official who investigates citizens' complaints against public officers and agencies. Nigeria's ombudsman is the Public Complaints Commission, set up in 1975 (Decree No. 31) by the Murtala Mohammed regime; it investigates injustice and maladministration in the public service. So the answer is C. The trap: the Code of Conduct Bureau (B) deals with public officers' asset declarations and conflicts of interest, not general complaints; the Civil Service Commission (A) appoints and disciplines staff. Memory tip: complain about maladministration to the Public COMPLAINTS Commission.",
 "|A political entity that possesses independence is a|community|nation|society|state": "A state has four elements: population, defined territory, government and sovereignty. Sovereignty means it is independent of outside control and supreme within its borders. A nation is a people bound by common culture, language and history; it may or may not have its own state (for example, the Kurds). Community and society are general terms for groups of people. So the entity with independence is a state: D. The trap: \"nation\" (B) is often used loosely for a country, but strictly it describes a people, not an independent political unit. Memory tip: STATE = Sovereign + Territory + Assembly of people + Government.",
 "|The principle that advocates strict adherence to the fundamental laws of a country is|constitutionalism|individual responsibility|absolutism|collective responsibility": "Constitutionalism means that the constitution is supreme and that both rulers and the ruled obey it; it is government limited by law, with checks on power. It goes with the rule of law and separation of powers. So the principle of strict adherence to a country's fundamental laws is constitutionalism: A. The trap: absolutism (C) is the opposite, unlimited power; collective and individual responsibility (B, D) are conventions about ministers in a cabinet system. Memory tip: constitutionalism = everyone, even the President, plays by the constitution.",
 "|One advantage of the unitary system over the federal system is its|bureaucratic nature|cost effectiveness|small membership|ratification of appointments": "In a unitary state (for example Britain or France), all power comes from one central government; local units are only its agents. There is no duplication of governors, state legislatures, ministries and civil services, so it is cheaper to run than a federation: B. A unitary system also allows quicker decisions and uniform policies. The trap: \"bureaucratic nature\" (A) is a disadvantage, not an advantage, and \"ratification of appointments\" (D) is a legislative check found in presidential systems. Memory tip: one government, one bill: unitary = cheaper.",
 "|Which of the following is a duty of an electoral commission?|Registration of political parties|Creation of political parties|Establishment of electoral tribunals|Inauguration of the parliament": "An electoral commission (in Nigeria, INEC since 1998) organises and supervises elections. Its duties include registering political parties and monitoring their finances, registering voters, delimiting constituencies, screening candidates' papers, conducting elections and declaring results. So registration of political parties is a duty: A. The trap: parties are created (B) by citizens who share ideas, and electoral tribunals (C) are set up under the constitution and staffed by the judiciary. Memory tip: INEC Registers, Conducts and Declares; it does not Create parties or Judge disputes.",
 "|A form of government in which the nobles rule for the benefit of all is|monarchy|gerontocracy|aristocracy|plutocracy": "Aristotle classified governments by who rules and for whom. Rule by one for all is monarchy; by a few for all is aristocracy; by many for all is polity. Their corrupt forms (rulers serving themselves) are tyranny, oligarchy and democracy (mob rule). So rule by nobles for the benefit of all is aristocracy: C. The trap: plutocracy (D) is rule by the rich, gerontocracy (B) is rule by elders, and monarchy (A) is rule by one. Memory tip: aristos = best; aristocracy = the best few ruling for all.",
 "|Fascism as an ideology facilitates|totalitarianism|socialism|communalism|liberalism": "Fascism arose in Italy under Benito Mussolini (in power 1922–1943), and Nazism in Germany under Hitler is a close cousin. It glorifies the state and the nation, worships a single leader, allows only one party, crushes opposition and controls the economy, press and education. A state that controls every aspect of life is totalitarian, so fascism facilitates totalitarianism: A. The trap: socialism (B) also gives the state a large role, but fascism was violently anti-socialist and anti-communist; liberalism (D) defends individual freedom, the opposite of fascism. Memory tip: Fascism = one Leader, one Party, total State.",
 "|A prominent feature of a presidential system of government is|dual executive|vote of no confidence|the rule of law|separation of powers": "In a presidential system (USA; Nigeria since 1979) the three organs of government are separated: the President and ministers are not members of the legislature, each organ has its own powers, and they check each other. So separation of powers is the prominent feature: D. The trap: dual executive (A) and vote of no confidence (B) belong to parliamentary systems; the rule of law (C) applies to every democratic system, so it is not distinctive of the presidential one. Memory tip: presidential = separated; parliamentary = fused.",
 "|The executive checks the excesses of the legislature through|assent to bills|the use of veto power|the ratification of treaties|issuance of orders": "Under checks and balances, each organ restrains the others. The executive checks the legislature mainly by the veto: the President may refuse assent to a bill, and in Nigeria (s.58, 1999 Constitution) the National Assembly can only override with a two-thirds majority in each house. So the answer is B. The trap: assent to bills (A) is the opposite, approving what the legislature has passed; ratification of treaties (C) is usually a legislative check on the executive. Memory tip: VETO is Latin for \"I forbid\".",
 "|The consciousness of a common identity of a people is called|nationalism|integration|indoctrination|emancipation": "Nationalism is the feeling of a people that they belong together as one nation, sharing history, culture or destiny; it often becomes a demand for self-government, as in Nigeria's struggle for independence. So consciousness of a common identity is nationalism: A. The trap: integration (B) is the process of bringing different groups together into one political community; it may produce a common identity but is not the consciousness itself. Indoctrination (C) is one-sided teaching; emancipation (D) is being set free. Memory tip: nationalism = \"we are one people\".",
 "|Citizenship of a state by descent requires that|one is born and bred in the state|one's mother was born in the state|one's grandparents were citizens of that state|one is domiciled in that state": "Citizenship by descent (jus sanguinis, \"right of blood\") is acquired through one's ancestors, not one's place of birth. Section 25 of the 1999 Constitution makes a person a Nigerian by birth if born in Nigeria to parents or grandparents who belong to a community indigenous to Nigeria, or born outside Nigeria to a parent who is a Nigerian citizen. Among the options, only C ties citizenship to ancestors who were citizens: C. The trap: being born and bred in a state (A) is citizenship by birth (jus soli), and domicile (D) leads to naturalisation, not descent. Memory tip: DESCENT = it DESCENDS from your parents and grandparents.",
 "|In order to dispense justice impartially, the judiciary primarily needs|discipline|logistics|motivation|independence": "The judiciary can only judge impartially, without fear or favour, if it is independent: free from pressure by the executive, the legislature, parties or the rich. Independence is secured by security of tenure, appointment through bodies like the NJC, salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund, and immunity for judicial acts. So the primary need is independence: D. The trap: discipline, logistics and motivation (A, B, C) all help judges work well, but none guarantees impartiality if judges can be removed or punished for unpopular rulings. Memory tip: no independence, no impartiality.",
 "|A state with a hegemonic political party is one in which|there is one dominant party|there is no opposition party|there is only one party|other parties are officially recognized": "In a dominant-party (hegemonic) system, several parties exist legally and elections are held, but one party is so strong that it wins time after time and controls government, like the ANC in South Africa or Mexico's PRI for most of the twentieth century. So the answer is A. The trap: \"only one party\" (C) describes a one-party state, where other parties are banned, and \"no opposition party\" (B) means the same thing; in a hegemonic system the opposition exists but cannot win. Memory tip: hegemonic = dominant, not alone.",
 "|Nigeria's commitment to the liberation of Africa could best be seen in her policy towards|Namibia and Mozambique|The Congo and Zimbabwe|Sierra Leone and Chad|South Africa and Angola": "Africa was the centrepiece of Nigeria's foreign policy. Nigeria's liberation commitment is best shown by Angola and South Africa. In November 1975 the Murtala regime recognised the MPLA government of Angola, defying the USA and swinging other African states behind it. Against apartheid South Africa, Nigeria led boycotts (e.g. of the 1976 Olympics), nationalised BP's assets in 1979 over oil sales to South Africa, and funded the ANC. So the answer is D. The trap: Nigeria also supported Namibia, Zimbabwe and Mozambique, but Angola and South Africa were the defining cases. Memory tip: \"Angola Recognised, Apartheid Resisted.\"",
 "|The set of policies on the basis of which countries interact with one another is called|constructive engagement policy|socio-economic policy|national policy|foreign policy": "Foreign policy is the set of principles and goals that guide a country's relations with other countries and international organisations, such as Nigeria's Afrocentric policy, non-alignment and economic diplomacy. So the answer is D. The trap: constructive engagement (A) was a specific policy, the Reagan administration's soft approach to apartheid South Africa in the 1980s, not the general term; national and socio-economic policies (B, C) are about domestic affairs. Memory tip: FOREIGN policy faces outward; domestic policy faces inward.",
 "|Nigeria's membership of the UN complements her foreign policy in the area of|bilateral diplomacy|regional diplomacy|sub-regional diplomacy|multilateral diplomacy": "Diplomacy is classified by how many states take part. Bilateral diplomacy is between two states; sub-regional means ECOWAS; regional means the AU; and multilateral means many states together in global bodies like the UN (193 members) or the Commonwealth. Nigeria joined the UN on 7 October 1960 as the 99th member and pursues goals such as peacekeeping and decolonisation there, so UN membership is multilateral diplomacy: D. The trap: the UN is not \"regional\" (B), because it covers the whole world. Memory tip: Bi = 2, Multi = many; the UN is the biggest \"multi\".",
 "|Nigeria teamed up with other developing countries to assert a neutral posture under the platform of|Nationalist Movement|Pan-African Movement|Non-aligned Movement|Negritude Movement": "During the Cold War, the world was split between the Western bloc (USA) and the Eastern bloc (USSR). Developing countries that refused to take sides formed the Non-Aligned Movement at Belgrade in 1961, inspired by the 1955 Bandung Conference and leaders such as Nehru, Nasser, Tito, Sukarno and Nkrumah. Nigeria adopted non-alignment as a principle of its foreign policy after independence. So the platform was the Non-Aligned Movement: C. The trap: the Pan-African Movement (B) united Africans and people of African descent against colonialism; it was not about Cold War neutrality. Memory tip: NAM = No Alignment with Moscow or Washington.",
 "|Nation-state is synonymous with|self-actualization|sovereignty|liberation|nationalism": "A nation is a people bound by common history, culture or language; a state is a political unit with territory, population, government and sovereignty. A nation-state is where the two coincide: a nation that has its own independent state. What makes it a state rather than just a nation is sovereignty, the supreme power to make and enforce laws within its territory free from outside control. So nation-state is synonymous with sovereignty: B. The trap: nationalism (D) is the feeling or movement that seeks a nation-state, not the state itself; liberation (C) is the struggle that may lead to one. Memory tip: nation = people; state = sovereignty; nation-state = a people with sovereignty.",
 "|A fundamental component of political culture is|social values|family values|community structure|economic values": "Political culture, a term made popular by Gabriel Almond and Sidney Verba (\"The Civic Culture\", 1963), is the pattern of beliefs, attitudes and values that members of a society hold towards government and politics. Values about authority, equality, participation and obedience are therefore its basic building blocks, and they grow out of the wider social values of the society. So a fundamental component of political culture is social values: A. The trap: family values (B) and economic values (D) are narrower; each is only one part of the society's values. Community structure (C) is about how society is organised, not what people believe. Memory tip: political culture = what a society values and believes about power.",
 "|A form of oligarchy in which gifted people are at the helm of affairs is|aristocracy|theocracy|plutocracy|gerontocracy": "Oligarchy means rule by a few. Its forms are named by WHO the few are. In an aristocracy (Greek \"aristos\", best) the few are the best: the most gifted, talented, wise or noble. Plato and Aristotle saw it as the good form of rule by the few. So oligarchy of gifted people is aristocracy: A. The trap: plutocracy (C) is rule by the wealthy, gerontocracy (D) rule by the old, and theocracy (B) rule by priests or in God's name. Each is also rule by a few, but the qualification differs. Memory tip: ARISTocracy = the best (ARISTos); PLUTOcracy = the rich (Pluto, god of wealth); GERONTocracy = the old.",
 "|A state that is ruled by an elected citizen is|a monarchy|a plutocracy|a republic|an empire": "A republic is a state whose head is an ordinary citizen chosen by election, directly by the people or by a legislature or electoral college, for a fixed term, rather than someone who inherits the throne. Nigeria became a republic on 1 October 1963, when Nnamdi Azikiwe, elected by Parliament, replaced the Queen as Head of State. So a state ruled by an elected citizen is a republic: C. The trap: a monarchy (A) is headed by a hereditary king or queen; an empire (D) is a ruler over many conquered peoples; a plutocracy (B) is rule by the rich. Memory tip: republic = res publica, \"the public thing\"; its head is elected, not born.",
 "|A true democracy in the modern sense exists where the|elected representatives rule|majority of the people rule|majority of the people vote|elite rules": "Democracy is government of the people, by the people, for the people (Abraham Lincoln, 1863). In ancient Athens citizens ruled directly in the assembly, but modern states have millions of people and cannot all meet to decide. So democracy in the modern sense is representative democracy: citizens elect representatives who govern on their behalf and can be voted out at the next election. That is where elected representatives rule: A. The trap: \"majority of the people rule\" (B) describes direct democracy, which is impossible in a large modern state; merely voting (C) is not enough if the voters' choices do not govern; elite rule (D) is oligarchy. Memory tip: modern democracy = ballots choose the rulers.",
 "|In a parliamentary system, when the legislature passes a vote of no confidence on the executive, it means that the|executive is expected to go on suspension|legislature ceases to trust the executive|executive is required to resign|legislature commences legal proceeding against the executive": "In a parliamentary system (Britain, Nigeria 1960–66) the executive, the prime minister and cabinet, comes from the legislature and stays in office only as long as it has the confidence (majority support) of parliament. This is collective responsibility. If parliament passes a vote of no confidence, the government must resign, or the prime minister may ask the head of state to dissolve parliament and call an election. So the executive is required to resign: C. The trap: B is true but is only the reason for the vote, not what it requires. Legal proceedings (D) belong to impeachment in a presidential system. Memory tip: no confidence = no government.",
 "|The legislative body of the United States of America is the|Parliament|National Assembly|Congress|Council": "The legislature of the United States is called Congress. It is bicameral: the Senate has two senators from each of the 50 states, and the House of Representatives has 435 members shared out by population. Nigeria copied the model in 1979 but named it the National Assembly. So the US legislative body is Congress: C. The trap: National Assembly (B) is Nigeria's name for the same kind of body; Parliament (A) is the British (and Commonwealth parliamentary) name. Memory tip: USA = Congress, UK = Parliament, Nigeria = National Assembly.",
 "|Unicameralism is a feature of the legislature in|Israel|the United States|the United Kingdom|Gambia": "A unicameral legislature has only one chamber. Israel's Knesset (120 members) has one chamber, and so does the National Assembly of The Gambia, which has been single-chamber since independence in 1965. By contrast, the United States Congress (Senate and House of Representatives) and the United Kingdom Parliament (House of Lords and House of Commons) are bicameral. So unicameralism is a feature of Israel: A, and of Gambia as well, so D is also accepted. The trap: the USA and UK are the textbook examples of TWO chambers. Memory tip: one chamber is common in small or unitary states (Israel, Gambia, Ghana); two chambers in big federations and old monarchies.",
 "|The upper house in most federal systems is created to|ensure equality of federating units|prevent excesses of the executive|oversee and check the lower house|enable experienced elders make inputs to governance": "Federations join units of very different sizes. If both houses were based on population, the big units would always outvote the small ones. So most federations create an upper house in which every federating unit has equal representation: two senators for each US state, and in Nigeria three senators for each of the 36 states plus one for the FCT. So the upper house exists to ensure equality of federating units: A. The trap: checking the lower house (C) or the executive (B) are things an upper house does, and the House of Lords includes experienced elders (D), but none is the reason federations create one. Memory tip: Senate = equal seats for unequal states.",
 "|In which of the following systems is the power of the component units more than that of the central government?|Monarchical|Federal|Unitary|Confederal": "The three ways of sharing power between centre and units differ in who is stronger. In a unitary state the centre holds all power and delegates some; in a federation the centre and the units are each supreme in their own sphere; in a confederation sovereign states keep most power and give a weak centre only a few tasks, and they can leave. The USA under the Articles of Confederation (1781–1789) and the Senegambia Confederation (1982–1989) are examples. So the units are stronger than the centre in a confederal system: D. The trap: federal (B) gives the units real but not superior power. Memory tip: unitary = strong centre; federal = shared; confederal = strong units.",
 "|One of the general tenets of fascist doctrine is that the leader is|supreme relative to the constitution|weak relative to the constitution|subordinate to the laws of the state|subordinate to the norms of the society": "Fascism, developed by Benito Mussolini in Italy (from 1922) and copied by Hitler's Nazism in Germany, glorifies the nation and the state and places all authority in one leader (Il Duce, Der Führer). The leader is seen as the embodiment of the nation's will, so his word stands above the constitution and the law; there is no rule of law or limited government. So the fascist leader is supreme relative to the constitution: A. The trap: C and D describe constitutionalism and the rule of law found in liberal democracies, the very things fascism rejects. Memory tip: fascism = one nation, one party, one leader above the law.",
 "|In a cabinet system of government, executive power is exercised by the|head of government|monarch|president|dominant party": "A cabinet (parliamentary) system separates the head of state from the head of government. The head of state, a monarch as in Britain or a ceremonial president as in Nigeria's First Republic, only reigns. Real executive power is exercised by the head of government, the prime minister, together with the cabinet, who are drawn from parliament and responsible to it. So executive power is exercised by the head of government: A. The trap: the monarch (B) is the nominal executive in whose name things are done, but has no real power; an executive president (C) belongs to a presidential system. Memory tip: in a cabinet system the Queen reigns, the Prime Minister rules.",
 "|The principle of separation of powers is best practised in the|presidential system|parliamentary system|monarchical system|feudal system": "Separation of powers, set out by Montesquieu in \"The Spirit of the Laws\" (1748), means the legislative, executive and judicial powers are held by different organs and different people. The presidential system (USA; Nigeria since 1979) applies it most fully: the president is elected separately from the legislature, ministers may not be members of the legislature, and each organ has its own source of authority. So separation of powers is best practised in the presidential system: A. The trap: in the parliamentary system (B) ministers sit in parliament, so the executive and legislature are fused. Monarchical and feudal systems (C, D) concentrate power in the ruler. Memory tip: presidential = separated; parliamentary = fused.",
 "|A typical form of delegated legislation is|an act|a bill|a decree|a bye-law": "Delegated legislation is law made not by the legislature itself but by another body, such as a minister, a local government or a statutory corporation, using power delegated to it by an Act of the legislature. Bye-laws (local laws made by local governments and public corporations), orders, rules and regulations are its typical forms. So a typical form of delegated legislation is a bye-law: D. The trap: an Act (A) is law made directly by the legislature, and a bill (B) is a proposed law not yet passed. A decree (C) is a law made by a military government that has seized legislative power, not one delegated to it. Memory tip: the parent Act delegates; the bye-law is its child.",
 "|The rights of a citizen can be withdrawn by the state if the person|opposes the government violently|leaves the country permanently|is convicted of a serious crime|is pronounced dead": "Citizens' rights, such as personal liberty, freedom of movement and in some countries the vote, are protected by the constitution. A state may lawfully take them away only through due process: when a court tries a person and convicts him of a serious crime, he may lose his liberty (imprisonment) and other rights. So rights can be withdrawn if the person is convicted of a serious crime: C. The trap: opposing the government violently (A) is a crime, but rights may be withdrawn only after a court convicts, not simply because the government says so. Leaving the country (B) does not end citizenship, and death (D) ends rights naturally, not by state action. Memory tip: no conviction, no withdrawal of rights.",
 "|An electoral process in which candidates are selected for elective offices by party members is|primary election|electoral college|bye election|general election": "Before a general election each party must choose one candidate for each office. A primary election is the contest held inside the party, in which party members (directly, or through delegates as in Nigeria's indirect primaries) choose the party's candidate. So selection of candidates by party members is a primary election: A. The trap: the general election (D) is the contest between candidates of different parties; a bye-election (C) fills a seat that falls vacant between general elections; an electoral college (B) is a body that elects someone on behalf of the voters, like the one that elects the US President. Memory tip: primary = first round, inside the party.",
 "|In theory one major advantage of the one-party system is that it|eliminates intra-party conflict|serves as an instrument of national integration|promotes greater mass participation in government|guarantees social justice": "A one-party state allows only one legal party. African leaders such as Julius Nyerere (Tanzania) and Kwame Nkrumah (Ghana) defended it in the 1960s by arguing that many parties would split their new nations along ethnic and regional lines, while a single party would bring all groups together. In theory, then, its major advantage is that it serves as an instrument of national integration: B. The trap: a single party does not eliminate intra-party conflict (A); the quarrels simply move inside the party. Mass participation (C) and social justice (D) are claims critics deny, since there is no real choice. Memory tip: one party, one nation: that was the argument.",
 "|A tactic employed by pressure groups to achieve their objectives is|memorandum|electioneering|propaganda|lobbying": "Pressure groups (interest groups) try to influence government policy without seeking to form the government. Their methods include lobbying, petitions and memoranda, publicity, strikes and demonstrations, and court action. Lobbying, meeting legislators and officials directly to persuade them, is the most typical tactic. So the tactic is lobbying: D. The trap: electioneering (B), campaigning to win elections, is what political parties do, since they seek power; pressure groups do not field candidates. A memorandum (A) is a document sent, and propaganda (C) is one-sided persuasion, but lobbying is the recognised tactic named in every textbook. Memory tip: parties want the seat; pressure groups want the ear.",
 "|Public opinion can be measured through|negotiation|referendum|strike action|rumour": "Public opinion is the collective view of the people on public issues. It can be measured through opinion polls, elections and referendums. A referendum puts a specific question directly to the electorate, so the result shows exactly what the majority thinks on that issue; Britain's 2016 vote on leaving the European Union is a famous example. So public opinion is measured through referendum: B. The trap: strike action (C) expresses the views of one group, such as workers, but cannot measure the opinion of the whole public; rumour (D) is unreliable; negotiation (A) is a way of settling disputes. Memory tip: to know what people think, ask them: referendum, poll or election.",
 "|Which of the following is the main function of the civil service?|Implementing government policies|Allocating resources to the federating units|Supporting the party in power|Mobilizing grass root support for government": "The civil service is the body of permanent, professional officials in government ministries and departments. Ministers make policy; civil servants advise them and then carry out, or implement, the policies. It is expected to be neutral (anonymous and non-partisan), serving any party in power equally. So its main function is implementing government policies: A. The trap: supporting the party in power (C) and mobilising grassroots support (D) are partisan jobs for politicians and parties; a civil servant must stay politically neutral. Sharing revenue among federating units (B) is done by laws and bodies such as the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission. Memory tip: politicians decide; civil servants deliver.",
 "|Who was the political head of the Old Oyo Empire?|Bashorun|Oyomesi|Aremo|Alaafin": "The Old Oyo Empire was ruled by the Alaafin (the \"owner of the palace\"), the king and political head, believed to be the companion of the gods. His power was checked by the Oyo Mesi, a council of seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun, who could ask him to commit suicide by presenting an empty calabash or parrot's eggs; the Ogboni society in turn checked the Oyo Mesi. So the political head was the Alaafin: D. The trap: the Bashorun (A) was powerful as head of the Oyo Mesi and even acted as regent, but he was not the head; the Aremo (C) was the crown prince. Memory tip: Alaafin rules; Oyo Mesi checks; Ogboni checks the checkers.",
 "|The Igbo political system was based on|age grades|Umunna|family ties|Umuada": "Pre-colonial Igbo society was acephalous (segmentary): there was no central king. The basic unit was the family, and families of common descent formed lineages and villages. Each family was headed by its eldest man, the Okpara, who held the ofo staff, and village decisions were taken by assemblies of family heads and adult men. So the Igbo political system was based on family (kinship) ties: C. The trap: the Umunna (B) is itself the patrilineal kinship group, one expression of these family ties, so it points the same way; age grades (A) and the Umuada (D, married daughters of the lineage) carried out important tasks but were not the base of the system. Memory tip: Igbo politics starts at home, with the family.",
 "|The Aro system in Igboland was|a religious organization|a political organization|a commercial organization|an imperial organization": "The Aro of Arochukwu built their influence on the Ibini Ukpabi oracle, the \"Long Juju\". People from all over Igboland and beyond came to it as the final court of appeal, and Aro agents spread across the land to bring cases and pilgrims to it. Its authority came from religious belief, so the Aro system was a religious organization: A. The British destroyed the oracle in the Aro Expedition of 1901–02. The paper printed \"Aro age-grade system\", but age grades had nothing to do with the oracle. The trap: the Aro also ran a large trade network, including the slave trade (C), but that trade grew from the oracle's religious hold. Memory tip: Aro = oracle = religion.",
 "|France introduced the policy of assimilation in her colonies primarily to|teach them the art of leadership|give them a sound education|change their way of life|discourage them from ritual killings": "France ruled its colonies (Senegal, Ivory Coast and others) through assimilation: Africans were to become \"black Frenchmen\" by adopting the French language, law, religion, dress and culture. An African who did so could become a French citizen (an \"évolué\") and even sit in the French parliament, as Blaise Diagne of Senegal did in 1914. So assimilation was introduced primarily to change their way of life: C. The trap: education (B) was used as a tool of assimilation, but it was the means, not the purpose. Leadership training (A) and stopping ritual killings (D) were not the aim. Memory tip: assimilate = make similar, i.e. make Africans French.",
 "|The foremost British trading company on the West African coast was|Royal Niger Company|United African Company|Lever Brothers|John Holt and Sons": "The United African Company (UAC) was formed in 1929 when the Niger Company (by then owned by Lever Brothers) merged with the African and Eastern Trade Corporation. It became the biggest British trading firm on the West African coast, dominating the export of palm produce and cocoa and the import of manufactured goods throughout the colonial period. So the foremost British trading company was the UAC: B. The trap: the Royal Niger Company (A) was important earlier, ruling the Niger area under a charter from 1886 until 1900, but its trade later passed into the UAC. Lever Brothers (C) was the parent company; John Holt (D) was smaller. Memory tip: RNC ruled, UAC traded.",
 "|Which of these rulers resisted colonial rule and was deported to Calabar?|King Kosoko|King Dosunmu|Oba Ovonramwen|King Jaja": "Oba Ovonramwen Nogbaisi of Benin tried to keep the British out of his kingdom. After the killing of Acting Consul James Phillips's party in January 1897, the British launched a punitive expedition, sacked Benin City in February 1897 and looted the Benin Bronzes. Ovonramwen was tried and deported to Calabar, where he died in 1914. So the ruler deported to Calabar was Oba Ovonramwen: C. The trap: King Jaja of Opobo (D) also resisted and was deported in 1887, but to the Gold Coast and then the West Indies, not Calabar. Kosoko and Dosunmu (A, B) were Lagos kings; Dosunmu ceded Lagos in 1861. Memory tip: Benin's Oba went to Calabar; Opobo's Jaja went overseas.",
 "|Nigerian nationalism was described as two-phased by|John Payne Jackson|Edward Wilmot Blyden|James S. Coleman|David Ricardo": "The American political scientist James S. Coleman wrote \"Nigeria: Background to Nationalism\" (1958), a classic study of the rise of Nigerian nationalism. He divided it into two phases: traditional or primary nationalism, the early resistance of traditional rulers and peoples to British conquest, and modern nationalism, the organised movement for self-government led by educated Nigerians through newspapers and parties. So the scholar was James S. Coleman: C. The trap: Edward Wilmot Blyden (B) was a 19th-century Pan-Africanist thinker who inspired West African nationalists, but he did not analyse Nigerian nationalism in phases. John Payne Jackson (A) edited the Lagos Weekly Record; David Ricardo (D) was a British economist. Memory tip: Coleman wrote the Background.",
 "|One major weakness of the Independence Constitution is that it|failed to provide the country with full sovereignty|gave total independence to Nigeria|gave full powers to the Supreme Court in Nigeria|empowered Britain to continue to rule": "Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960, but the Independence Constitution left two links with Britain. Queen Elizabeth II remained Head of State, represented in Nigeria by a Governor-General (Nnamdi Azikiwe from November 1960), and the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council in London remained Nigeria's final court of appeal. So it failed to provide the country with full sovereignty: A. These links ended with the 1963 Republican Constitution. The trap: D goes too far: Britain did not continue to rule, since Nigerians governed through the Balewa government. B is the opposite of a weakness, and C is false because the Privy Council stood above the Supreme Court. Memory tip: 1960 independence; 1963 full sovereignty.",
 "|The first law-making body in Nigeria after amalgamation was|Nigerian Council|National Assembly|Legislative Council|Regional Assembly": "After the 1914 amalgamation Lugard set up the Nigerian Council, but it was purely advisory: it could discuss matters but had no power to make laws, and leading members seldom attended. Laws were made by the Lagos Legislative Council (for the Colony) and by the Governor's proclamations. The Clifford Constitution of 1922 then created a new Legislative Council for Lagos and the Southern Provinces, with Nigeria's first elected members. So the first law-making body was the Legislative Council: C. The trap: the Nigerian Council (A) came first in time, but it was not a law-making body. Memory tip: 1914 Nigerian Council talked; the Legislative Council legislated.",
 "|The designation of ministers as chief executives and accounting officers was recommended by a commission headed by|Jerome Udoji|S.J. Cookey|Simeon Adebo|Dotun Phillips": "Nigeria's civil service was reformed by several commissions. The Dotun Phillips panel (1985), put into effect by Decree No. 43 of 1988 under Babangida, made each minister the chief executive and accounting officer of his ministry, renamed the Permanent Secretary Director-General, and made that post political, so it would end with the government that appointed it. So the commission was headed by Dotun Phillips: D. The trap: Jerome Udoji (A) chaired the 1972–74 commission that introduced grade levels 1–17 and the \"Udoji award\"; Simeon Adebo (C) chaired the 1971 wages commission. Memory tip: Udoji = grade levels; Phillips = ministers in charge and Directors-General.",
 "|A major shortcoming of the Ombudsman is|lack of adequate resources|lack of clear-cut mandate|its inability to restrain bureaucratic excesses|lack of executive power to enforce decisions": "The Ombudsman, an office from Sweden (1809), investigates citizens' complaints of injustice or maladministration by public officials. Nigeria's version is the Public Complaints Commission, set up in 1975. It can investigate and recommend that a wrong be put right, but it has no executive power to enforce its decisions: it must rely on the officials or government concerned to act. So its major shortcoming is lack of executive power to enforce decisions: D. The trap: C is the RESULT of this weakness, since bureaucratic excesses go unchecked because it cannot enforce; lack of resources (A) is a problem, but not the defining one. Memory tip: the Ombudsman can bark but cannot bite.",
 "|One of the strong points of the multi-party Nigeria's Fourth Republic is|the provision for a bicameral legislature|wider political participation|government interference|wider anti-democracy campaign": "Nigeria's Fourth Republic began on 29 May 1999. Unlike the two-party arrangement of the aborted Third Republic (SDP and NRC), it allows many political parties to be registered. More parties mean more choices for voters and more avenues for groups to organise, contest elections and take part in politics. So a strong point is wider political participation: B. The trap: a bicameral legislature (A) is a feature of the 1999 Constitution, but it has nothing to do with the number of parties; government interference (C) and anti-democracy campaigns (D) are weaknesses, not strengths. Memory tip: multi-party = many doors into politics.",
 "|In which of the following is the ceremonial and executive powers fused?|Presidential system of government|Parliamentary system of government|Federal system of government|Unitary system of government": "In a presidential system the president combines two roles: head of state (ceremonial: receiving ambassadors, national symbol) and head of government (executive: running the government). Nigeria's president since 1979 and the US president hold both. In a parliamentary system the roles are separate: a monarch or ceremonial president is head of state and a prime minister is head of government, as in Nigeria in 1963–66 (Azikiwe and Balewa). So the powers are fused in the presidential system: A. The trap: \"fused\" makes people think of the parliamentary system (B), but there it is the EXECUTIVE and LEGISLATURE that are fused, not the ceremonial and executive roles. Memory tip: presidential = two hats, one head.",
 "|A major contentious issue confronting Nigerian federalism is|poverty|education|health care delivery|revenue allocation": "Since the Richards Constitution of 1946, how to share the revenue collected by the federal government among the federal, state and local governments, and by which principles (derivation, population, equality of states, need), has been the most contentious issue in Nigerian federalism. Commissions from Phillipson (1946) and Raisman (1958) to Aboyade (1977) and Okigbo (1980) have tried to settle it, and oil-producing areas still demand \"resource control\". So the contentious issue is revenue allocation: D. The trap: poverty, education and health care (A, B, C) are serious national problems, but they are not disputes about how the federation itself works. Memory tip: in Nigerian federalism, the fight is over the money.",
 "|The main purpose of establishing public enterprises in Nigeria is to|increase government revenue|provide essential services|enrich the elite|compete with the private sector": "Public enterprises (public corporations and government-owned companies) were set up in Nigeria, such as NEPA (electricity), the Nigerian Railway Corporation and NITEL, because essential services needed huge capital, were too important to be left to profit-seeking private firms, or had to reach people who could not pay full cost. So the main purpose was to provide essential services: B. The trap: increasing government revenue (A) can be a by-product, but profit was not the main aim; many ran at a loss and needed subsidies. Competing with the private sector (D) was not the purpose, and enriching the elite (C) is an abuse. Memory tip: public enterprise = public service first.",
 "|Parastatals are established to|enhance entrepreneurial skills|maximize government profits|expand business transactions|render social services": "Parastatals are government-owned agencies and corporations with a degree of independence from the ministries, such as power, water, broadcasting and transport bodies. They are established mainly to render social services, essential services to the public, at reasonable cost, not to make the most money. So parastatals are established to render social services: D. The trap: maximising government profits (B) sounds businesslike, but service, not profit, is their main goal, which is why many were later commercialised or privatised. Enhancing entrepreneurial skills (A) and expanding business (C) are aims of private enterprise. Memory tip: parastatal = state service with a business face.",
 "|The General-Purpose Committee of the local government is the|cabinet of the local government|local government public relations unit|body responsible for supervising self-help projects|body for awarding contracts": "Under the 1976 Local Government Reform each council had a chairman, supervisory councillors and a secretary. Together they formed the General Purpose Committee, the executive arm of the local government. It made policy, prepared the budget, coordinated departments and advised the council, just as the cabinet does at federal level. So the General Purpose Committee is the cabinet of the local government: A. The trap: supervising self-help projects (C) and awarding contracts (D) are particular tasks it may oversee, but they do not describe what the committee is; public relations (B) is a minor unit. Memory tip: GPC = the local cabinet.",
 "|The highest organ of the state during the Babangida Regime was the|Provisional Ruling Council|Supreme Military Council|Armed Forces Ruling Council|Federal Executive Council": "Each military regime named its supreme ruling body. Gowon and Murtala/Obasanjo had the Supreme Military Council (SMC), and Buhari (1984–85) kept the SMC. When Babangida seized power in August 1985 he replaced it with the Armed Forces Ruling Council (AFRC), which was the highest organ of state until 1993. So under Babangida it was the Armed Forces Ruling Council: C. The trap: the Provisional Ruling Council (A) was Abacha's body (1993–98), kept by Abdulsalami Abubakar; the Supreme Military Council (B) belonged to earlier regimes; the Federal Executive Council (D) was the cabinet below the AFRC. Memory tip: SMC, then AFRC (Babangida), then PRC (Abacha).",
 "|Decree 34 of 1966 was unacceptable to many Nigerians because it was|seen as an instrument of impoverishment|perceived to abolish the federal system|promulgated without consultation with the people|considered as alien": "After the January 1966 coup, Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi issued the Unification Decree (Decree No. 34) on 24 May 1966. It abolished the four regions, turned them into \"groups of provinces\" and unified the regional civil services, making Nigeria a unitary state. Many Nigerians, especially in the North, feared domination and saw it as the end of the federal system, and riots followed; it helped bring about the counter-coup of 29 July 1966. So it was perceived to abolish the federal system: B. The trap: C is true of all military decrees, so it does not explain why THIS decree was so opposed. Memory tip: Decree 34 unified; Gowon restored federalism (Decree 8 and 12 states, 1967).",
 "|Nigeria broke diplomatic relations with France in 1961 because of|France's atomic test in the Sahara Desert|General de-Gaulle's negative attitude towards her|her poor relations with the Francophone countries|France's diplomatic relations with Israel": "France tested its first atomic bomb in the Sahara (at Reggane, Algeria) on 13 February 1960, with further tests that year, close to West Africa. Nigeria protested strongly, and after the third test in December 1960 the Balewa government broke diplomatic relations with France in January 1961 and closed its ports and airports to French ships and aircraft. So it was because of France's atomic test in the Sahara Desert: A. The trap: relations with Francophone neighbours (C) and De Gaulle (B) were later issues, for example France's support for Biafra in 1967–70, but they did not cause the 1961 break. Memory tip: 1960 bomb in the Sahara, 1961 break with Paris.",
 "|An attribute that Nigeria shares with most non-aligned countries is|the state of her economy|her heterogeneous population|her large population|her large size": "The Non-Aligned Movement (founded in Belgrade in 1961) is made up mostly of former colonies in Africa, Asia and Latin America. What most of them share with Nigeria is the state of their economies: developing, dependent on exporting raw materials such as oil, cocoa or copper, and seeking economic independence. So the shared attribute is the state of her economy: A. The trap: Nigeria's large population (C) and large size (D) set her APART from most non-aligned states, many of which are small; a heterogeneous population (B) is common, but it is not what defines the group. Memory tip: what unites the non-aligned is underdevelopment, not size.",
 "|Nigeria spearheaded the formation of ECOWAS during the regime of|Olusegun Obasanjo|Yakubu Gowon|Murtala Muhammed|Ibrahim Babangida": "The Economic Community of West African States was created by the Treaty of Lagos, signed on 28 May 1975 by 15 West African states. Nigeria's head of state, General Yakubu Gowon, drove the project together with Togo's President Gnassingbé Eyadema, and Nigeria offered to bear much of the cost. Two months later, in July 1975, Gowon was overthrown. So ECOWAS was formed under Gowon: B. The trap: Murtala Muhammed (C) took over in July 1975, after the treaty was signed; Obasanjo (A) followed in 1976 and, much later as civilian president, helped turn ECOWAS into a Commission. Memory tip: Gowon signed in Lagos, May 1975.",
 "|Nigeria was classified as a frontline state for|participating in peacekeeping in the Congo|supporting the liberation efforts in Southern Africa|spearheading the formation of the African Union|helping to end the crisis in Liberia": "The \"frontline states\" were the Southern African countries bordering white-ruled territories (Tanzania, Zambia, Botswana, Mozambique, Angola and later Zimbabwe) which supported the liberation struggle. Nigeria is thousands of kilometres away, but from the 1970s it gave so much money, diplomatic support and help to movements in Angola, Mozambique, Zimbabwe, Namibia and South Africa that it was treated as an honorary frontline state. So it was for supporting the liberation efforts in Southern Africa: B. The trap: Congo peacekeeping (A, 1960–64) and ECOMOG in Liberia (D, from 1990) were in other regions. Memory tip: frontline = the front against apartheid.",
 "|The prominent role Nigeria played in the UN in the 70's earned her|non-permanent membership position|membership of the Security Council|permanent representation at the UN|chairmanship of the General Assembly": "The UN Security Council has 5 permanent members and (since 1965) 10 non-permanent members elected for two years. In the 1970s Nigeria was very active at the UN, especially in the fight against apartheid (Nigerians chaired the UN Special Committee against Apartheid from 1972), and it was elected to the Security Council for 1978–1979. So its role earned it membership of the Security Council: B. Option A, printed \"non-permanent membership position Liberia\", does not say of which body. The trap: Nigeria had a permanent representative (C) from admission in 1960, which every member has, so it was not earned. Chairmanship of the General Assembly (D) came in 1989 (Joseph Garba). Memory tip: 1970s activism, a Council seat for 1978–79.",
 "|The head of Nigeria's foreign mission in a Commonwealth nation is known as|high commissioner|charge d'affaires|ambassador|attaché": "Commonwealth countries regard one another as members of one family, so they do not exchange ambassadors. The head of a diplomatic mission from one Commonwealth country to another is called a High Commissioner, and the mission is a High Commission. So Nigeria's envoy in London, Accra or New Delhi is a high commissioner: A. The trap: an ambassador (C) is the title for the head of a mission in a non-Commonwealth country, such as Nigeria's ambassador in Paris or Washington. A chargé d'affaires (B) runs an embassy when there is no ambassador, and an attaché (D) is a junior specialist officer. Memory tip: Commonwealth = High Commissioner; everyone else = Ambassador.",
 "|One major function of the Authority of Heads of State and Government of ECOWAS is|organizing international conferences|appointing the Executive Secretary|appointing staff of the Secretariat|preparing the budget of the Community": "Under the 1975 ECOWAS Treaty, the Authority of Heads of State and Government is the supreme organ. It directs the Community's policy and appoints its top officer, which was then the Executive Secretary. So a major function of the Authority was appointing the Executive Secretary: B. Today the Executive Secretariat has been replaced (since 2007) by the ECOWAS Commission, whose President the Authority appoints. The trap: appointing staff of the Secretariat (C) was the job of the Executive Secretary, not of the heads of state; the budget (D) was prepared by the Secretariat and considered by the Council of Ministers. Memory tip: heads of state choose the chief; the chief chooses the staff.",
 "|The tenure of non-permanent members of the Security Council is|5 years|2 years|4 years|6 years": "The UN Security Council has 15 members. Five are permanent (USA, Russia, China, Britain, France), and ten are non-permanent members elected by the General Assembly for two-year terms, five each year, and they cannot be re-elected immediately. So the tenure is 2 years: B. Nigeria has served several such terms, for example 1978–79 and 2014–15. The trap: 5 years (A) is the usual term of the UN Secretary-General; 4 years (C) is the term of Nigeria's president and governors; 6 years (D) is not a UN term. Memory tip: non-permanent means short: two years on the Council.",
 "|The Secretary-General of the United Nations is appointed by the|Security Council acting alone|General Assembly on the recommendation of the Security Council|Permanent members of the Security Council on the recommendation of the General Assembly|General Assembly in plenary session": "Article 97 of the UN Charter says the Secretary-General \"shall be appointed by the General Assembly upon the recommendation of the Security Council\". The Council chooses a candidate (and the five permanent members can veto), then the General Assembly formally appoints. The term is usually five years. So the Secretary-General is appointed by the General Assembly on the recommendation of the Security Council: B. The trap: the Security Council acting alone (A) only recommends; option C reverses the two bodies; the General Assembly alone (D) cannot appoint without a Council recommendation. Memory tip: Council proposes, Assembly appoints.",
 "|The approval of treaties and agreements of the Economic Community of West African States is the responsibility of the|secretariat|ECOWAS Tribunal|Council of Ministers|Assembly of Heads of State and Government": "The ECOWAS institutions under the 1975 treaty were the Authority of Heads of State and Government, the Council of Ministers, the Executive Secretariat, the Tribunal and specialised commissions. The Authority is the supreme organ: it takes the final decisions, including approving the Community's treaties, protocols and agreements, while the Council of Ministers advises it and the Secretariat implements. So approval of treaties and agreements is the responsibility of the Assembly (Authority) of Heads of State and Government: D. The trap: the Council of Ministers (C) prepares and recommends, but it is the heads of state who approve. Memory tip: in ECOWAS the final word is with the heads of state."
};
